window.FIWO_WORKBOOK = {"version":"2026-09-11","builtAt":"2026-09-11T09:39:24.560Z","phases":[{"n":1,"title":"Exist, name, and describe (L1–L5)","lessons":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","R1"],"ability":"walk into a room and say what things are and what you are doing."},{"n":2,"title":"People, possession, and time (L6–L11)","lessons":["L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","R2"],"ability":"talk about other people, and about yesterday and tomorrow."},{"n":3,"title":"Ask, answer, and command (L12–L16)","lessons":["L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","R3"],"ability":"stop making statements and start having conversations."},{"n":4,"title":"Inner life (L17–L20)","lessons":["L17","L18","L19","L20","R4"],"ability":"say what you feel, think, want, and prefer."},{"n":5,"title":"Connect your thoughts (L21–L25)","lessons":["L21","L22","L23","L24","L25","R5"],"ability":"stop speaking in short sentences."},{"n":6,"title":"Number, money, and the calendar (L26–L30)","lessons":["L26","L27","L28","L29","L30","R6"],"ability":"count, shop, calculate, and put a date on things."},{"n":7,"title":"Out in the world (L31–L34)","lessons":["L31","L32","L33","L34","R7"],"ability":"locate things, travel, and describe the world around you."},{"n":8,"title":"Real Fiwo (L35–L38)","lessons":["L35","L36","L37","L38","FR"],"ability":"precision, self-sufficiency, and a finished language."}],"outro":"<p>You have finished the course. You know 328 core roots, all 38 rules, and enough derivation to build words nobody has written down.</p>\n<p>What remains is not more lessons. It is <strong>use</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Narrate your day aloud, every day. Four sentences about the weather is enough.</li>\n<li>Keep the speaker log. Every time you reach for something you cannot say, write it down — that list is what drives the language forward now.</li>\n<li>Read the Translations stories and the Idiom phrasebook.</li>\n<li>Write something nobody asked for: a diary entry, a joke, a song.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The dictionary holds over a thousand more roots in Tiers 2 and 3. You do not need them yet. You need to talk.</p>\n","lessons":[{"id":"L1","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 1","title":"Read any Fiwo word aloud and know what it is","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> pronounce any Fiwo word correctly on sight, and tell what <em>kind</em> of word it is without a dictionary.</p>\n<p>This is the only lesson that does not teach you something to say. It is the decoder for every lesson after it. Do not skip it — the whole language rides on the last letter of each word, and you need to <em>hear</em> that letter before anything else works.</p>\n<h3>1. One letter, one sound</h3>\n<p>Every letter is one sound. Always. There are no silent letters, no irregular spellings, no exceptions. If you see it, you say it.</p>\n<p><code>sydo</code> is <em>sy-do</em>. Two syllables, four sounds, no surprises.</p>\n<p><strong>No diphthongs.</strong> Two vowels next to each other are two separate syllables, never glided together. <code>nauxa</code> is <em>na-u-xa</em> — three syllables. English wants to say &quot;now-sha&quot;; Fiwo says <em>na-u-xa</em>.</p>\n<h3>2. Stress falls on the final vowel</h3>\n<p>Stress always lands on the <strong>last vowel of the word</strong>, and stretches forward through anything that follows it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Say it</th>\n<th>Stress</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>nofa</code></td>\n<td>no-<strong>fa</strong></td>\n<td>on <code>fa</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nofap</code></td>\n<td>no-<strong>fap</strong></td>\n<td>on <code>fap</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>xalidyq</code></td>\n<td>xa-li-<strong>dyq</strong></td>\n<td>on <code>lidyq</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>You never have to guess. Find the last vowel, and stress everything from there to the end.</p>\n<p>This is not decoration. The endings carry all the grammar, so Fiwo puts the loudest part of the word exactly where the information is. In a noisy room, the part you still hear is the part that matters.</p>\n<h3>3. The last vowel tells you what the word is</h3>\n<p>This is the core of Fiwo. A word&#39;s final vowel is its grammatical category — its part of speech is visible on its face.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>-a</code></td>\n<td>Biological noun</td>\n<td>living things, body parts</td>\n<td><strong>nofa</strong> person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-o</code></td>\n<td>Concrete noun</td>\n<td>objects, materials, places</td>\n<td><strong>sydo</strong> house</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-u</code></td>\n<td>Abstract noun</td>\n<td>ideas, feelings, time</td>\n<td><strong>dionu</strong> time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-i</code></td>\n<td>Verb</td>\n<td>actions and states</td>\n<td><strong>nomi</strong> to eat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-e</code></td>\n<td>Modifier</td>\n<td>describes a noun or verb</td>\n<td><strong>tande</strong> big</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-y</code></td>\n<td>Preposition</td>\n<td>relationships</td>\n<td><strong>zy</strong> to</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>You do not memorise which words are nouns. You <em>look</em>.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"-a","english":"Biological noun"},{"fiwo":"-o","english":"Concrete noun"},{"fiwo":"-u","english":"Abstract noun"},{"fiwo":"-i","english":"Verb"},{"fiwo":"-e","english":"Modifier"},{"fiwo":"-y","english":"Preposition"}],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Say it aloud.","note":"Read each word, then write how many syllables it has.","text":"`sydo`","type":"oral","key":"`sy-do` — 2","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Say it aloud.","note":"Read each word, then write how many syllables it has.","text":"`nauxa`","type":"oral","key":"`na-u-xa` — 3 (two vowels together are two syllables)","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Say it aloud.","note":"Read each word, then write how many syllables it has.","text":"`hoxop`","type":"oral","key":"`ho-xop` — 2","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Say it aloud.","note":"Read each word, then write how many syllables it has.","text":"`bryklo`","type":"oral","key":"`bry-klo` — 2","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Say it aloud.","note":"Read each word, then write how many syllables it has.","text":"`kalo`","type":"oral","key":"`ka-lo` — 2","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"What kind of word is it?","note":"Give the category from the final vowel alone.","text":"`niboo` — trick question, look carefully","type":"explain","key":"There is no such word — but the final `-o` would make it a concrete noun. The point: you can classify a word you have never seen.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"What kind of word is it?","note":"Give the category from the final vowel alone.","text":"`grawi`","type":"explain","key":"Verb (`-i`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"What kind of word is it?","note":"Give the category from the final vowel alone.","text":"`tande`","type":"explain","key":"Modifier (`-e`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"What kind of word is it?","note":"Give the category from the final vowel alone.","text":"`dionu`","type":"explain","key":"Abstract noun (`-u`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"B","prompt":"What kind of word is it?","note":"Give the category from the final vowel alone.","text":"`deta`","type":"explain","key":"Biological noun (`-a`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Where is the stress?","text":"`nauxa`","type":"oral","key":"na-u-**xa**","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"Where is the stress?","text":"`sydop`","type":"oral","key":"sy-**dop**","answers":[],"points":4},{"n":13,"part":"C","prompt":"Where is the stress?","text":"`nomid`","type":"oral","key":"no-**mid**","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L2","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 2","title":"Greet someone and say who you are","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say hello and goodbye, say who you are, say who someone else is, say yes and no, and say that you thank.</p>\n<h3>1. Saying what something is — no verb needed</h3>\n<p>To say <em>X is Y</em>, put X and Y side by side. That is the whole rule. Fiwo has no word for &quot;is&quot; in a plain statement of identity.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nofa.</strong> — I am a person.\n<strong>Suk nauxa.</strong> — You are a friend.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>mik</code> = I/me · <code>suk</code> = you · <code>nofa</code> = person · <code>nauxa</code> = friend</p>\n<p>This is called the <strong>Zero Copula</strong>. English needs &quot;am/is/are&quot;; Fiwo just puts the two things next to each other and lets the word order do the work.</p>\n<h3>2. Your name is a proper noun</h3>\n<p>Names keep their original spelling and pronunciation. They do <em>not</em> need to end in a functional vowel, and they always take a capital letter — that capital is a flag telling the parser &quot;this is a name, stop applying the vowel rules.&quot;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik Josh.</strong> — I am Josh.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. A subject and a verb, with nothing else</h3>\n<p>Not every verb needs an object. Some sentences are just <em>who</em> and <em>what they do</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik grawi.</strong> — I thank.\n<strong>Mik xwewi.</strong> — I excuse.\n<strong>Nofap grawi.</strong> — The person thanks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>grawi</code> = to thank · <code>xwewi</code> = to excuse</p>\n<p>Notice <code>grawi</code> ends in <code>-i</code>. You already know from Lesson 1 that this makes it a verb — you did not have to be told.</p>\n<p><strong>These are not yet &quot;thank you&quot; and &quot;excuse me.&quot;</strong> Both of those need a second person in the sentence — the one being thanked, the one being excused — and that slot does not exist until Lesson 4. <code>Mik grawi.</code> says who is thanking and nothing about who is thanked. Read it to yourself as <em>I thank</em>, not as <em>thanks</em>: the difference is the whole of Lesson 4, and starting with the wrong equation makes that lesson harder than it needs to be.</p>\n<h3>4. Words that stand alone</h3>\n<p>A few short words are complete utterances by themselves. They are not nouns or verbs; they are social signals.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>sal</code></td>\n<td>hello</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tex</code></td>\n<td>goodbye</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jas</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nes</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cef</code></td>\n<td>please</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sal!</strong> — Hello!\n<strong>Jas.</strong> — Yes.\n<strong>Tex!</strong> — Goodbye!</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Your first conversation</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sal!</strong> — Hello!\n<strong>Sal! Mik Josh.</strong> — Hello! I am Josh.\n<strong>Mik nauxa.</strong> — I am a friend.\n<strong>Jas. Mik grawi.</strong> — Yes. I thank.\n<strong>Tex!</strong> — Goodbye!</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"sal","english":"hello"},{"fiwo":"tex","english":"goodbye"},{"fiwo":"jas","english":"yes"},{"fiwo":"nes","english":"no"},{"fiwo":"cef","english":"please"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nofa.","english":"I am a person."},{"fiwo":"Suk nauxa.","english":"You are a friend."},{"fiwo":"Mik Josh.","english":"I am Josh."},{"fiwo":"Mik grawi.","english":"I thank."},{"fiwo":"Mik xwewi.","english":"I excuse."},{"fiwo":"Nofap grawi.","english":"The person thanks."},{"fiwo":"Sal!","english":"Hello!"},{"fiwo":"Jas.","english":"Yes."},{"fiwo":"Tex!","english":"Goodbye!"},{"fiwo":"Sal!","english":"Hello!"},{"fiwo":"Sal! Mik Josh.","english":"Hello! I am Josh."},{"fiwo":"Mik nauxa.","english":"I am a friend."},{"fiwo":"Jas. Mik grawi.","english":"Yes. I thank."},{"fiwo":"Tex!","english":"Goodbye!"}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Sal!","Mik","Josh."],"blank":0,"answer":"Sal","pre":"","post":"!","english":"Hello! I am Josh.","fiwo":"Sal! Mik Josh."},{"tokens":["Jas.","Mik","grawi."],"blank":0,"answer":"Jas","pre":"","post":".","english":"Yes. I thank.","fiwo":"Jas. Mik grawi."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am a person.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nofa.`","answers":["Mik nofa."],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You are a friend.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk nauxa.`","answers":["Suk nauxa."],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Hello!","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sal!`","answers":["Sal!"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You thank.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk grawi.`","answers":["Suk grawi."],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am Sam.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik Sam.`","answers":["Mik Sam."],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Suk nofa.`","type":"type-en","key":"You are a person.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nofap grawi.` — (`nofap` means *the* person; you meet this properly in Lesson 3)","type":"type-en","key":"The person thanks.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nes.`","type":"type-en","key":"No.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik xwewi.`","type":"type-en","key":"I excuse.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Fix the mistake.","text":"`Mik hi nofa.` — a learner added a word for \"am\". Why is it unnecessary?","type":"explain","key":"Fiwo uses the Zero Copula — putting two nouns side by side already means \"X is Y\". (A word `hi` for \"to be\" does exist and is legal, but it is optional and emphatic, never the default.)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Fix the mistake.","text":"`mik josh.` — what is wrong with this?","type":"explain","key":"The name `Josh` must be capitalised — the capital is the flag that marks a proper noun.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L3","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 3","title":"Name the things around you","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> point at anything in the room and say what it is, and say whether you mean <em>the</em> one or just <em>a</em> one.</p>\n<h3>1. The specific and the non-specific</h3>\n<p>English puts &quot;the&quot; and &quot;a&quot; in front of a noun. Fiwo attaches a consonant to the end of it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Suffix</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>-p</code></td>\n<td>specific — <em>the</em>, <em>that one</em></td>\n<td><code>hoxop</code> the door</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-r</code></td>\n<td>non-specific — <em>a</em>, <em>some</em></td>\n<td><code>hoxor</code> a door</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sil hoxop.</strong> — This is the door.\n<strong>Fos nibor.</strong> — That is a book.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The suffix goes after the final vowel and never changes the word&#39;s category. <code>hoxo</code> is a concrete noun; so is <code>hoxop</code>. You can still see the <code>-o</code>.</p>\n<h3>2. Pointing: this and that</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>sil</code></td>\n<td>this (near me)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fos</code></td>\n<td>that (over there)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>These combine with the Zero Copula from Lesson 2 — no verb needed.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sil zufop.</strong> — This is the bed.\n<strong>Fos xiolor.</strong> — That is a chair.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>sil</code> and <code>fos</code> stand alone as <em>this</em> and <em>that</em>. To pin one onto a noun — <em>this book</em>\nrather than bare <em>this</em> — use their modifier forms, which look left like any modifier:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>sile</code></td>\n<td>this, these</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fose</code></td>\n<td>that, those</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>nibop sile</code> — this book\n<code>xiolop fose</code> — that chair</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Watch the difference: <strong>Sil nibop.</strong> is <em>this is the book</em> — a whole sentence, with the\nZero Copula doing the work. <code>nibop sile</code> is just <em>this book</em>, a phrase naming a thing.\nThe <code>-e</code> is doing the same job it always does; you will see why it matters in Lesson 5.</p>\n<h3>3. Your first room</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>sydo</code></td>\n<td>house</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>dego</code></td>\n<td>table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>krulo</code></td>\n<td>room</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xiolo</code></td>\n<td>chair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hoxo</code></td>\n<td>door</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>luso</code></td>\n<td>key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wilo</code></td>\n<td>window</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>nibo</code></td>\n<td>book</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zufo</code></td>\n<td>bed</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kalo</code></td>\n<td>cup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hano</code></td>\n<td>box</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>busko</code></td>\n<td>clothes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>All twelve end in <code>-o</code>. They are all concrete nouns, and you can see it.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"-p","english":"specific — *the*, *that one*"},{"fiwo":"-r","english":"non-specific — *a*, *some*"},{"fiwo":"sil","english":"this (near me)"},{"fiwo":"fos","english":"that (over there)"},{"fiwo":"sile","english":"this, these"},{"fiwo":"fose","english":"that, those"},{"fiwo":"sydo","english":"house"},{"fiwo":"dego","english":"table"},{"fiwo":"krulo","english":"room"},{"fiwo":"xiolo","english":"chair"},{"fiwo":"hoxo","english":"door"},{"fiwo":"luso","english":"key"},{"fiwo":"wilo","english":"window"},{"fiwo":"nibo","english":"book"},{"fiwo":"zufo","english":"bed"},{"fiwo":"kalo","english":"cup"},{"fiwo":"hano","english":"box"},{"fiwo":"busko","english":"clothes"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Sil hoxop.","english":"This is the door."},{"fiwo":"Fos nibor.","english":"That is a book."},{"fiwo":"Sil zufop.","english":"This is the bed."},{"fiwo":"Fos xiolor.","english":"That is a chair."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"the window","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`wilop`","answers":["wilop"],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"a cup","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`kalor`","answers":["kalor"],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"the key","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`lusop`","answers":["lusop"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"a room","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`krulor`","answers":["krulor"],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"This is the house.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sil sydop.`","answers":["Sil sydop."],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"That is a table.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fos degor.`","answers":["Fos degor."],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"This is the book.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sil nibop.`","answers":["Sil nibop."],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Fos buskop.`","type":"type-en","key":"That is the clothing.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Sil hanor.`","type":"type-en","key":"This is a box.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Sil kalop.`","type":"type-en","key":"This is the cup.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L4","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 4","title":"Say what you are doing","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> make a real sentence — somebody does something to something.</p>\n<h3>1. The order never changes</h3>\n<p>Fiwo sentences run in fixed slots:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Subject → Verb → Object</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik xosi wilop.</strong> — I open the window.\n<strong>Suk beati hoxop.</strong> — You close the door.\n<strong>Mik nomi bryklop.</strong> — I eat the bread.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The order is not a preference. It is how the parser knows which noun is doing and which is being done to. Fiwo never moves words around for emphasis — if you want emphasis, there are tools for it later, but the slots stay put.</p>\n<h3>2. Verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>xosi</code></td>\n<td>to open</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>sapi</code></td>\n<td>to make</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>beati</code></td>\n<td>to close</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>swami</code></td>\n<td>to put</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zyli</code></td>\n<td>to use</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>neki</code></td>\n<td>to sit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>karxi</code></td>\n<td>to find</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>usi</code></td>\n<td>to sleep</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>And two more social verbs, now that you have an object slot to put people in:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>enconi</code></td>\n<td>to meet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>umzi</code></td>\n<td>to welcome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik enconi nauxap.</strong> — I meet the friend.\n<strong>Mik umzi suk.</strong> — I welcome you.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Food and drink</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>bata</code></td>\n<td>food</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>dorso</code></td>\n<td>water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nomi</code></td>\n<td>to eat</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>lamso</code></td>\n<td>milk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roli</code></td>\n<td>to drink</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>bryklo</code></td>\n<td>bread</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>bata</code> ends in <code>-a</code> — a biological noun, because food comes from living things. <code>dorso</code> ends in <code>-o</code> — a concrete noun. The vowels are telling you how Fiwo classifies the world.</p>\n<h3>4. You can leave the object out</h3>\n<p>If the verb does not need an object, do not give it one. You already did this in Lesson 2.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik usi.</strong> — I sleep.\n<strong>Suk neki.</strong> — You sit.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"xosi","english":"to open"},{"fiwo":"sapi","english":"to make"},{"fiwo":"beati","english":"to close"},{"fiwo":"swami","english":"to put"},{"fiwo":"zyli","english":"to use"},{"fiwo":"neki","english":"to sit"},{"fiwo":"karxi","english":"to find"},{"fiwo":"usi","english":"to sleep"},{"fiwo":"enconi","english":"to meet"},{"fiwo":"umzi","english":"to welcome"},{"fiwo":"bata","english":"food"},{"fiwo":"dorso","english":"water"},{"fiwo":"nomi","english":"to eat"},{"fiwo":"lamso","english":"milk"},{"fiwo":"roli","english":"to drink"},{"fiwo":"bryklo","english":"bread"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik xosi wilop.","english":"I open the window."},{"fiwo":"Suk beati hoxop.","english":"You close the door."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomi bryklop.","english":"I eat the bread."},{"fiwo":"Mik enconi nauxap.","english":"I meet the friend."},{"fiwo":"Mik umzi suk.","english":"I welcome you."},{"fiwo":"Mik usi.","english":"I sleep."},{"fiwo":"Suk neki.","english":"You sit."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","xosi","wilop."],"blank":1,"answer":"xosi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I open the window.","fiwo":"Mik xosi wilop."},{"tokens":["Suk","beati","hoxop."],"blank":1,"answer":"beati","pre":"","post":"","english":"You close the door.","fiwo":"Suk beati hoxop."},{"tokens":["Mik","nomi","bryklop."],"blank":1,"answer":"nomi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I eat the bread.","fiwo":"Mik nomi bryklop."},{"tokens":["Mik","enconi","nauxap."],"blank":1,"answer":"enconi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I meet the friend.","fiwo":"Mik enconi nauxap."},{"tokens":["Mik","umzi","suk."],"blank":1,"answer":"umzi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I welcome you.","fiwo":"Mik umzi suk."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I open the door.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik xosi hoxop.`","answers":["Mik xosi hoxop."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklo","hoxop.","Mik","sapi","xosi"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You eat the food.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk nomi batap.`","answers":["Suk nomi batap."],"points":10,"bank":["bata","batap.","nomi","Suk","xosi"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I drink the water.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik roli dorsop.`","answers":["Mik roli dorsop."],"points":10,"bank":["dorsop.","Mik","nomi","roli","swami"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I find the key.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik karxi lusop.`","answers":["Mik karxi lusop."],"points":10,"bank":["dorso","enconi","karxi","lusop.","Mik"]},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik usi.`","answers":["Mik usi."],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Suk sapi batap.`","type":"type-en","key":"You make the food.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nofap swami kalop.`","type":"type-en","key":"The person puts the cup.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik umzi suk.`","type":"type-en","key":"I welcome you.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Suk zyli lusop.`","type":"type-en","key":"You use the key.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Xosi mik hoxop.` — the learner put the verb first.","type":"explain","key":"Slot 1 must be the subject. As written, the parser reads `Xosi` as the subject slot and fails. Correct: `Mik xosi hoxop.`","answers":["Mik xosi hoxop."],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Mik hoxop xosi.` — the learner put the object before the verb.","type":"explain","key":"The verb must come before the object. Correct: `Mik xosi hoxop.`","answers":["Mik xosi hoxop."],"points":6}]},{"id":"L5","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 5","title":"Describe what you see","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> describe anything — and say that something <em>is</em> a certain way, which is not the same thing.</p>\n<h3>1. Modifiers look left</h3>\n<p>A modifier goes <strong>after</strong> the word it describes, and attaches to whatever is on its left.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>batap gawe</strong> — the good food\n<strong>hoxop leupe</strong> — the red door</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is a phrase, not a sentence. It is a <em>thing</em>, not a statement. Read it aloud and notice it feels unfinished — that is correct.</p>\n<h3>2. To make it a sentence, add <code>-i</code></h3>\n<p>To say <em>the house is big</em>, you must fill the verb slot. Turn the modifier into a verb by adding <code>-i</code> — the verb vowel you learned in Lesson 1.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop tandei.</strong> — The house is big.\n<strong>Nibop waskei.</strong> — The book is old.\n<strong>Kalop locwei.</strong> — The cup is white.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is the difference that trips up every beginner:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>sydop tande</code></td>\n<td>a phrase</td>\n<td>the big house</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sydop tandei</code></td>\n<td>a sentence</td>\n<td>the house is big</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>One letter. Say them aloud back to back until the difference is automatic.</p>\n<h3>3. Stacking modifiers</h3>\n<p>You can use more than one. Each looks left.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hoxop leupe nifnei.</strong> — The red door is small.\n<strong>Suk sapi batap jaxke.</strong> — You make the new food.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>4. Size and quality</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>tande</code></td>\n<td>big</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>gawe</code></td>\n<td>good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nifne</code></td>\n<td>small</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>boke</code></td>\n<td>bad</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dwime</code></td>\n<td>long</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>stobe</code></td>\n<td>hard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>veke</code></td>\n<td>short</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>rese</code></td>\n<td>soft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zave</code></td>\n<td>tall</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>dote</code></td>\n<td>strong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>twave</code></td>\n<td>heavy</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>paxe</code></td>\n<td>weak</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>5. Colour and appearance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>leupe</code></td>\n<td>red</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>cape</code></td>\n<td>black</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kivwe</code></td>\n<td>blue</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>locwe</code></td>\n<td>white</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>luare</code></td>\n<td>green</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>jaxke</code></td>\n<td>new</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>loske</code></td>\n<td>yellow</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>waske</code></td>\n<td>old</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>6. Two more social words</h3>\n<p>Now that you can build a stative verb, two words from Lesson 2&#39;s family finally work:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>toce</code></td>\n<td>sorry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>romve</code></td>\n<td>again</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik tocei.</strong> — I am sorry.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"sydop tande","english":"a phrase"},{"fiwo":"sydop tandei","english":"a sentence"},{"fiwo":"tande","english":"big"},{"fiwo":"gawe","english":"good"},{"fiwo":"nifne","english":"small"},{"fiwo":"boke","english":"bad"},{"fiwo":"dwime","english":"long"},{"fiwo":"stobe","english":"hard"},{"fiwo":"veke","english":"short"},{"fiwo":"rese","english":"soft"},{"fiwo":"zave","english":"tall"},{"fiwo":"dote","english":"strong"},{"fiwo":"twave","english":"heavy"},{"fiwo":"paxe","english":"weak"},{"fiwo":"leupe","english":"red"},{"fiwo":"cape","english":"black"},{"fiwo":"kivwe","english":"blue"},{"fiwo":"locwe","english":"white"},{"fiwo":"luare","english":"green"},{"fiwo":"jaxke","english":"new"},{"fiwo":"loske","english":"yellow"},{"fiwo":"waske","english":"old"},{"fiwo":"toce","english":"sorry"},{"fiwo":"romve","english":"again"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"batap gawe","english":"the good food"},{"fiwo":"hoxop leupe","english":"the red door"},{"fiwo":"Sydop tandei.","english":"The house is big."},{"fiwo":"Nibop waskei.","english":"The book is old."},{"fiwo":"Kalop locwei.","english":"The cup is white."},{"fiwo":"Hoxop leupe nifnei.","english":"The red door is small."},{"fiwo":"Suk sapi batap jaxke.","english":"You make the new food."},{"fiwo":"Mik tocei.","english":"I am sorry."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Hoxop","leupe","nifnei."],"blank":1,"answer":"leupe","pre":"","post":"","english":"The red door is small.","fiwo":"Hoxop leupe nifnei."},{"tokens":["Suk","sapi","batap","jaxke."],"blank":3,"answer":"jaxke","pre":"","post":".","english":"You make the new food.","fiwo":"Suk sapi batap jaxke."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Phrase or sentence?","note":"Say which, and translate.","text":"`nibop jaxke`","type":"explain","key":"Phrase — the new book","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Phrase or sentence?","note":"Say which, and translate.","text":"`nibop jaxkei`","type":"explain","key":"Sentence — the book is new","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Phrase or sentence?","note":"Say which, and translate.","text":"`zufop resei`","type":"explain","key":"Sentence — the bed is soft","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The house is old.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sydop waskei.`","answers":["Sydop waskei."],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The cup is white.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kalop locwei.`","answers":["Kalop locwei."],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The small window","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`wilop nifne`","answers":["wilop nifne"],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am sorry.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik tocei.`","answers":["Mik tocei."],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Fix the mistake.","text":"`Sydop tande.` — the learner meant *the house is big*.","type":"explain","key":"This is a phrase (*the big house*), not a sentence. The verb slot is empty. Correct: `Sydop tandei.`","answers":["Sydop tandei."],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Fix the mistake.","text":"`Tande sydop.` — the learner meant *the big house*.","type":"explain","key":"Modifiers look **left**, so this attaches `tande` to nothing and puts a modifier in the subject slot. Correct: `sydop tande`.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"R1","kind":"review","name":"Review 1","title":"Lessons 1–5","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Category from the ending.","note":"What kind of word is each, and how do you know?","text":"`krulo`","type":"explain","key":"Concrete noun (`-o`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Category from the ending.","note":"What kind of word is each, and how do you know?","text":"`grawi`","type":"explain","key":"Verb (`-i`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Category from the ending.","note":"What kind of word is each, and how do you know?","text":"`luare`","type":"explain","key":"Modifier (`-e`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Category from the ending.","note":"What kind of word is each, and how do you know?","text":"`nauxa`","type":"explain","key":"Biological noun (`-a`)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Hello! I am Josh.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sal! Mik Josh.`","answers":["Sal! Mik Josh."],"points":10,"bank":["Josh.","Mik","Sal!"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"This is the door.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sil hoxop.`","answers":["Sil hoxop."],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You eat the bread.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk nomi bryklop.`","answers":["Suk nomi bryklop."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop.","nomi","Suk"]},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The chair is small.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Xiolop nifnei.`","answers":["Xiolop nifnei."],"points":10},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Thank you.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik grawi suk.`","answers":["Mik grawi suk."],"points":10,"bank":["grawi","Mik","suk."]},{"n":10,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"That is a book.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fos nibor.`","answers":["Fos nibor."],"points":10},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik karxi lusop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I find the key.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Sil zufop.`","type":"type-en","key":"This is the bed.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":13,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Hoxop leupe nifnei.`","type":"type-en","key":"The red door is small.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":14,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Suk umzi nauxap.`","type":"type-en","key":"You welcome the friend.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":15,"part":"D","prompt":"Phrase or sentence?","text":"`kalop locwe`","type":"explain","key":"Phrase — the white cup","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":16,"part":"D","prompt":"Phrase or sentence?","text":"`kalop locwei`","type":"explain","key":"Sentence — the cup is white","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":17,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: greet someone, introduce yourself, name three objects near you, and say one thing about each. Aloud, in one continuous go.","text":"Without looking: greet someone, introduce yourself, name three objects near you, and say one thing about each. Aloud, in one continuous go.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L6","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 6","title":"Talk about other people","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> talk about him, her, it, us, and them — and name your family.</p>\n<h3>1. Fiwo makes you choose: does it have a mind?</h3>\n<p>English &quot;it&quot; covers a rock and a dog. Fiwo does not. The third-person pronoun tells you whether the thing has agency.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>dal</code></td>\n<td>he / she / it</td>\n<td>anything alive or with intent, gender unknown or irrelevant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>daq</code></td>\n<td>he</td>\n<td>when you know it is male</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>das</code></td>\n<td>she</td>\n<td>when you know it is female</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ram</code></td>\n<td>it</td>\n<td>objects, plants, machines, ideas — no intent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Dal nofa.</strong> — He/she is a person.\n<strong>Daq peba.</strong> — He is a father.\n<strong>Das mana.</strong> — She is a mother.\n<strong>Ram nibop.</strong> — It is the book.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you use <code>ram</code> for a person you have insulted them. If you use <code>dal</code> for a chair you have said the chair has a mind. Choose deliberately.</p>\n<h3>2. We, with a fork in it</h3>\n<p>Fiwo splits &quot;we&quot; by whether the listener is included.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>muk</code></td>\n<td>we — <em>including you</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nak</code></td>\n<td>we — <em>not including you</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>We are going to eat</em> is two different sentences depending on whether you are invited. English leaves this ambiguous; Fiwo cannot.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Muk nauxa.</strong> — We (you and I) are friends.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Making pronouns plural with <code>je</code></h3>\n<p><code>je</code> is the plural marker. It goes after the word it multiplies.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Dal je nomi batap.</strong> — They eat the food.\n<strong>Suk je usi.</strong> — You all sleep.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>The one ban:</strong> <code>mik</code>, <code>muk</code> and <code>nak</code> never take <code>je</code>. &quot;I&quot; cannot be plural, and <code>muk</code>/<code>nak</code> are <em>already</em> plural — adding <code>je</code> is redundant and crashes the parser.</p>\n<h3>4. Formal you</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>suk</code></td>\n<td>you — friends, peers, children</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>suv</code></td>\n<td>you — elders, strangers, professionals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>5. People and family</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>noqsa</code></td>\n<td>man</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>mana</code></td>\n<td>mother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nosana</code></td>\n<td>woman</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>peba</code></td>\n<td>father</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cada</code></td>\n<td>child</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>taqa</code></td>\n<td>brother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>biba</code></td>\n<td>baby</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>saza</code></td>\n<td>sister</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kehulu</code></td>\n<td>family</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>naxu</code></td>\n<td>group</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nofap cada.</strong> — The person is a child.\n<strong>Naxup nofa je.</strong> — The group is people.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note <code>kehulu</code> and <code>naxu</code> end in <code>-u</code>: a family is an abstract idea, not a physical object.</p>\n<h3>6. More things in the house</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>zafoiro</code></td>\n<td>floor</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>belimo</code></td>\n<td>shoe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>krapo</code></td>\n<td>wall</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>paelo</code></td>\n<td>paper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>relse</code></td>\n<td>light</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kino</code></td>\n<td>knife</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zabato</code></td>\n<td>bag</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>icifo</code></td>\n<td>plate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"dal","english":"he / she / it"},{"fiwo":"daq","english":"he"},{"fiwo":"das","english":"she"},{"fiwo":"ram","english":"it"},{"fiwo":"muk","english":"we — *including you*"},{"fiwo":"nak","english":"we — *not including you*"},{"fiwo":"suk","english":"you — friends, peers, children"},{"fiwo":"suv","english":"you — elders, strangers, professionals"},{"fiwo":"noqsa","english":"man"},{"fiwo":"mana","english":"mother"},{"fiwo":"nosana","english":"woman"},{"fiwo":"peba","english":"father"},{"fiwo":"cada","english":"child"},{"fiwo":"taqa","english":"brother"},{"fiwo":"biba","english":"baby"},{"fiwo":"saza","english":"sister"},{"fiwo":"kehulu","english":"family"},{"fiwo":"naxu","english":"group"},{"fiwo":"zafoiro","english":"floor"},{"fiwo":"belimo","english":"shoe"},{"fiwo":"krapo","english":"wall"},{"fiwo":"paelo","english":"paper"},{"fiwo":"relse","english":"light"},{"fiwo":"kino","english":"knife"},{"fiwo":"zabato","english":"bag"},{"fiwo":"icifo","english":"plate"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Dal nofa.","english":"He/she is a person."},{"fiwo":"Daq peba.","english":"He is a father."},{"fiwo":"Das mana.","english":"She is a mother."},{"fiwo":"Ram nibop.","english":"It is the book."},{"fiwo":"Muk nauxa.","english":"We (you and I) are friends."},{"fiwo":"Dal je nomi batap.","english":"They eat the food."},{"fiwo":"Suk je usi.","english":"You all sleep."},{"fiwo":"Nofap cada.","english":"The person is a child."},{"fiwo":"Naxup nofa je.","english":"The group is people."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Dal","je","nomi","batap."],"blank":0,"answer":"Dal","pre":"","post":"","english":"They eat the food.","fiwo":"Dal je nomi batap."},{"tokens":["Suk","je","usi."],"blank":0,"answer":"Suk","pre":"","post":"","english":"You all sleep.","fiwo":"Suk je usi."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Which pronoun?","note":"`dal`, `daq`, `das` or `ram`?","text":"A dog whose sex you do not know","type":"choice","key":"`dal` — alive, sex unknown","answers":["dal"],"points":10,"options":["dal","daq","das","ram"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Which pronoun?","note":"`dal`, `daq`, `das` or `ram`?","text":"A table","type":"choice","key":"`ram` — no agency","answers":["ram"],"points":10,"options":["dal","daq","das","ram"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Which pronoun?","note":"`dal`, `daq`, `das` or `ram`?","text":"Your mother","type":"choice","key":"`das`","answers":["das"],"points":10,"options":["dal","daq","das","ram"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Which pronoun?","note":"`dal`, `daq`, `das` or `ram`?","text":"An idea","type":"choice","key":"`ram` — abstract, no intent","answers":["ram"],"points":10,"options":["dal","daq","das","ram"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She is a mother.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das mana.`","answers":["Das mana."],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"They eat the food. (mixed group)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Dal je nomi batap.`","answers":["Dal je nomi batap."],"points":10,"bank":["batap.","Dal","das","je","kehulu","nomi"]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We (you and I) are friends.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Muk nauxa.`","answers":["Muk nauxa."],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"It is the book.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Ram nibop.`","answers":["Ram nibop."],"points":10},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Daq taqa.`","type":"type-en","key":"He is a brother.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Suk je usi.`","type":"type-en","key":"You all sleep.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Naxup nofa je.`","type":"type-en","key":"The group is people.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"D","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Mik je nomi batap.` — the learner wanted *we eat the food*.","type":"explain","key":"`mik` never takes `je` (the Redundancy Ban). Use `muk` (including the listener) or `nak` (excluding them): `Muk nomi batap.`","answers":["Muk nomi batap."],"points":6}]},{"id":"L7","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 7","title":"Say what belongs to whom","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say <em>my</em>, <em>your</em>, <em>his</em>, <em>hers</em> — and name the parts of your body.</p>\n<h3>1. Possessives are just pronouns wearing <code>-e</code></h3>\n<p>You do not learn a new set of words. Take the pronoun and add the modifier vowel <code>-e</code> — the same <code>-e</code> you met in Lesson 1.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Possessive</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>mik</code></td>\n<td><code>mike</code></td>\n<td>my</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>suk</code></td>\n<td><code>suke</code></td>\n<td>your</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>suv</code></td>\n<td><code>suve</code></td>\n<td>your (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dal</code></td>\n<td><code>dale</code></td>\n<td>his / her / its</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>daq</code></td>\n<td><code>daqe</code></td>\n<td>his</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>das</code></td>\n<td><code>dase</code></td>\n<td>hers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ram</code></td>\n<td><code>rame</code></td>\n<td>its</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>muk</code></td>\n<td><code>muke</code></td>\n<td>our (including you)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nak</code></td>\n<td><code>nake</code></td>\n<td>our (not including you)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>2. They look left, like every modifier</h3>\n<p>A possessive is a modifier, so it obeys Lesson 5&#39;s rule: it goes <strong>after</strong> the thing owned.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop mike.</strong> — My house.\n<strong>Nibop suke.</strong> — Your book.\n<strong>Meotap dase.</strong> — Her head.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>English says <em>my house</em>; Fiwo says <em>house my</em>. The owner comes second.</p>\n<h3>3. Owning something legally</h3>\n<p>There are two kinds of possession, and Fiwo separates them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>-e</code> possessive</strong> — general association: <em>my house</em> (the one I live in)</li>\n<li><strong><code>ry</code></strong> — strict, legal ownership: <em>the house owned by me</em></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop mike.</strong> — My house.\n<strong>Sydop ry mik.</strong> — The house that belongs to me.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Use <code>-e</code> for everyday talk. Reach for <code>ry</code> when the ownership is the point — contracts, disputes, property.</p>\n<h3>4. Your body</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>soxa</code></td>\n<td>body</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>nyta</code></td>\n<td>nose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>meota</code></td>\n<td>head</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>ylxa</code></td>\n<td>mouth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zipa</code></td>\n<td>face</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>pwosa</code></td>\n<td>tooth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tseja</code></td>\n<td>eye</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xesa</code></td>\n<td>hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fija</code></td>\n<td>ear</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xufa</code></td>\n<td>arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wusa</code></td>\n<td>hand</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>myga</code></td>\n<td>leg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jipri</code></td>\n<td>to wash</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every one ends in <code>-a</code>. Body parts are biological nouns — the vowel is doing its job again.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jipri wusap mike.</strong> — I wash my hand.\n<strong>Tsejap daqe nifnei.</strong> — His eye is small.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"mike","english":"my"},{"fiwo":"suke","english":"your"},{"fiwo":"suve","english":"your (formal)"},{"fiwo":"dale","english":"his / her / its"},{"fiwo":"daqe","english":"his"},{"fiwo":"dase","english":"hers"},{"fiwo":"rame","english":"its"},{"fiwo":"muke","english":"our (including you)"},{"fiwo":"nake","english":"our (not including you)"},{"fiwo":"soxa","english":"body"},{"fiwo":"nyta","english":"nose"},{"fiwo":"meota","english":"head"},{"fiwo":"ylxa","english":"mouth"},{"fiwo":"zipa","english":"face"},{"fiwo":"pwosa","english":"tooth"},{"fiwo":"tseja","english":"eye"},{"fiwo":"xesa","english":"hair"},{"fiwo":"fija","english":"ear"},{"fiwo":"xufa","english":"arm"},{"fiwo":"wusa","english":"hand"},{"fiwo":"myga","english":"leg"},{"fiwo":"jipri","english":"to wash"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Sydop mike.","english":"My house."},{"fiwo":"Nibop suke.","english":"Your book."},{"fiwo":"Meotap dase.","english":"Her head."},{"fiwo":"Sydop mike.","english":"My house."},{"fiwo":"Sydop ry mik.","english":"The house that belongs to me."},{"fiwo":"Mik jipri wusap mike.","english":"I wash my hand."},{"fiwo":"Tsejap daqe nifnei.","english":"His eye is small."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","jipri","wusap","mike."],"blank":1,"answer":"jipri","pre":"","post":"","english":"I wash my hand.","fiwo":"Mik jipri wusap mike."},{"tokens":["Tsejap","daqe","nifnei."],"blank":1,"answer":"daqe","pre":"","post":"","english":"His eye is small.","fiwo":"Tsejap daqe nifnei."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"my book","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nibop mike`","answers":["nibop mike"],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"her house","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`sydop dase`","answers":["sydop dase"],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"your hand (casual)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`wusap suke`","answers":["wusap suke"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"our eye (including you)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`tsejap muke`","answers":["tsejap muke"],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Meotap mike.`","type":"type-en","key":"My head.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Zipap dase tandei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Her face is big.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nibop ry suk.`","type":"type-en","key":"The book that belongs to you.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Which one — `-e` or `ry`?","text":"*My father* (a relationship, not ownership)","type":"choice","key":"`-e` — `peba mike`. Relationships are association, not legal ownership.","answers":["-e"],"points":10,"options":["-e","ry"]},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Which one — `-e` or `ry`?","text":"*The house legally owned by her*","type":"choice","key":"`ry` — `sydop ry das`.","answers":["ry"],"points":10,"options":["-e","ry"]},{"n":10,"part":"D","prompt":"Legal, but wrong.","text":"`Mike sydop.` — the learner meant *my house*. The parser accepts this sentence. So what does it actually say, and why is that a problem?","type":"explain","key":"It parses — but not as *my house*. With nothing to its left to attach to, `Mike` is read as a standalone fragment (*Mine.*), and `sydop` then starts a fresh sentence as its subject. You have said two things, neither of which is \"my house\". Correct: `sydop mike`.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L8","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 8","title":"Say when it happened","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> talk about yesterday and tomorrow, not just now.</p>\n<h3>1. Time is one letter on the verb</h3>\n<p>Fiwo has no separate tense words and no irregular verbs. You add a consonant to the end of the verb, after its <code>-i</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Suffix</th>\n<th>Tense</th>\n<th><code>nomi</code> (to eat)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><em>(none)</em></td>\n<td>present / general</td>\n<td><code>nomi</code> — eats</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-d</code></td>\n<td>past</td>\n<td><code>nomid</code> — ate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-s</code></td>\n<td>future</td>\n<td><code>nomis</code> — will eat</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomid batap.</strong> — I ate the food.\n<strong>Suk xosis hoxop.</strong> — You will open the door.\n<strong>Mik usid.</strong> — I slept.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is the entire tense system for now. There is no &quot;did&quot;, no &quot;will&quot;, no &quot;-ed&quot; versus &quot;went&quot;. Every verb in the language works exactly this way, forever.</p>\n<h3>2. The suffix does not change the category</h3>\n<p><code>nomi</code> ends in <code>-i</code> and is a verb. <code>nomid</code> still ends in <code>-i</code> before the <code>-d</code>, so it is still a verb. Suffixes stack on the outside; the functional vowel underneath never moves.</p>\n<p>Remember Lesson 1: stress rides forward onto the suffix. <code>no-mid</code>, not <code>no-mid</code>. Say it aloud — the tense marker is the loudest part of the word, which is exactly the point.</p>\n<h3>3. Words for when</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>dionu</code></td>\n<td>time</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>nudu</code></td>\n<td>today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>du</code></td>\n<td>day</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>fitydu</code></td>\n<td>tomorrow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dugu</code></td>\n<td>night</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>wecdu</code></td>\n<td>yesterday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cihu</code></td>\n<td>morning</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>nu</code></td>\n<td>now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>vivu</code></td>\n<td>evening</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>peze</code></td>\n<td>later</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>All abstract nouns in <code>-u</code> — time is not a physical object. (<code>peze</code> is a modifier: <em>later</em>.)</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"-d","english":"past"},{"fiwo":"-s","english":"future"},{"fiwo":"dionu","english":"time"},{"fiwo":"nudu","english":"today"},{"fiwo":"du","english":"day"},{"fiwo":"fitydu","english":"tomorrow"},{"fiwo":"dugu","english":"night"},{"fiwo":"wecdu","english":"yesterday"},{"fiwo":"cihu","english":"morning"},{"fiwo":"nu","english":"now"},{"fiwo":"vivu","english":"evening"},{"fiwo":"peze","english":"later"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nomid batap.","english":"I ate the food."},{"fiwo":"Suk xosis hoxop.","english":"You will open the door."},{"fiwo":"Mik usid.","english":"I slept."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"`karxi` → found","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`karxid`","answers":["karxid"],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"`sapi` → will make","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`sapis`","answers":["sapis"],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Add the right suffix.","text":"`roli` → drank","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`rolid`","answers":["rolid"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I ate the bread.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid bryklop.`","answers":["Mik nomid bryklop."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop.","du","fitydu","Mik","nomid"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She will make the food.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das sapis batap.`","answers":["Das sapis batap."],"points":10,"bank":["batap.","Das","dionu","peze","sapis"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You slept.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk usid.`","answers":["Suk usid."],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik karxid lusop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I found the key.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das xosis wilop.`","type":"type-en","key":"She will open the window.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk enconid nauxap.`","type":"type-en","key":"We met the friend.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L9","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 9","title":"Ongoing, finished, and both at once","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say something is happening right now, or is finished and still matters — and combine that with past and future.</p>\n<h3>1. Two more markers</h3>\n<p>Tense says <em>when</em>. Aspect says <em>how the action flows</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Suffix</th>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th><code>nomi</code></th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>-q</code></td>\n<td>continuous</td>\n<td><code>nomiq</code></td>\n<td>is actively eating, right now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-k</code></td>\n<td>perfect</td>\n<td><code>nomik</code></td>\n<td>has eaten, and it matters now</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomiq batap.</strong> — I am eating the food (this instant).\n<strong>Mik nomik batap.</strong> — I have eaten the food (so I am not hungry).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The perfect is not just &quot;past&quot;. <code>nomid</code> says it happened. <code>nomik</code> says it happened <em>and the result is still true</em>.</p>\n<h3>2. Stacking, with a <code>y</code> bridge</h3>\n<p>To combine a tense with an aspect, put both consonants on — with <code>y</code> between them so the cluster stays pronounceable.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>[verb] + [tense d/s] + y + [aspect q/k]</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>nomidyq</code></td>\n<td>was eating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nomidyk</code></td>\n<td>had eaten</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nomisyq</code></td>\n<td>will be eating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nomisyk</code></td>\n<td>will have eaten</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomidyq batap.</strong> — I was eating the food.\n<strong>Das xosidyk hoxop.</strong> — She had opened the door.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read the suffix left to right and it tells you its own meaning: <code>d</code> = past, <code>y</code> = keep going, <code>q</code> = in progress. Past-continuous. Nothing to memorise.</p>\n<h3>3. Saying no</h3>\n<p><code>nes</code> goes immediately before the verb, whatever suffixes it carries.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nes usi.</strong> — I do not sleep.\n<strong>Mik nes nomik.</strong> — I have not eaten.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>4. More everyday verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>gugi</code></td>\n<td>to stand</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>jofi</code></td>\n<td>to help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rizi</code></td>\n<td>to wake</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>wasti</code></td>\n<td>to wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nedci</code></td>\n<td>to play</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>etypi</code></td>\n<td>to get</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>seji</code></td>\n<td>to stop</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Muk jofi cadap.</strong> — We help the child.\n<strong>Nofap seji.</strong> — The person stops.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"nomi","english":"Meaning"},{"fiwo":"-q","english":"continuous"},{"fiwo":"nomiq","english":"is actively eating, right now"},{"fiwo":"-k","english":"perfect"},{"fiwo":"nomik","english":"has eaten, and it matters now"},{"fiwo":"nomidyq","english":"was eating"},{"fiwo":"nomidyk","english":"had eaten"},{"fiwo":"nomisyq","english":"will be eating"},{"fiwo":"nomisyk","english":"will have eaten"},{"fiwo":"gugi","english":"to stand"},{"fiwo":"jofi","english":"to help"},{"fiwo":"rizi","english":"to wake"},{"fiwo":"wasti","english":"to wait"},{"fiwo":"nedci","english":"to play"},{"fiwo":"etypi","english":"to get"},{"fiwo":"seji","english":"to stop"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nomiq batap.","english":"I am eating the food (this instant)."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomik batap.","english":"I have eaten the food (so I am not hungry)."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomidyq batap.","english":"I was eating the food."},{"fiwo":"Das xosidyk hoxop.","english":"She had opened the door."},{"fiwo":"Mik nes usi.","english":"I do not sleep."},{"fiwo":"Mik nes nomik.","english":"I have not eaten."},{"fiwo":"Muk jofi cadap.","english":"We help the child."},{"fiwo":"Nofap seji.","english":"The person stops."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","nomiq","batap."],"blank":1,"answer":"nomiq","pre":"","post":"","english":"I am eating the food (this instant).","fiwo":"Mik nomiq batap."},{"tokens":["Mik","nomik","batap."],"blank":1,"answer":"nomik","pre":"","post":"","english":"I have eaten the food (so I am not hungry).","fiwo":"Mik nomik batap."},{"tokens":["Mik","nomidyq","batap."],"blank":1,"answer":"nomidyq","pre":"","post":"","english":"I was eating the food.","fiwo":"Mik nomidyq batap."},{"tokens":["Mik","nes","nomik."],"blank":2,"answer":"nomik","pre":"","post":".","english":"I have not eaten.","fiwo":"Mik nes nomik."},{"tokens":["Muk","jofi","cadap."],"blank":1,"answer":"jofi","pre":"","post":"","english":"We help the child.","fiwo":"Muk jofi cadap."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `karxi` (to find):","text":"is finding right now","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`karxiq`","answers":["karxiq"],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `karxi` (to find):","text":"has found","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`karxik`","answers":["karxik"],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `karxi` (to find):","text":"had found","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`karxidyk`","answers":["karxidyk"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `karxi` (to find):","text":"will be finding","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`karxisyq`","answers":["karxisyq"],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am eating the bread now.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomiq bryklop nu.`","answers":["Mik nomiq bryklop nu."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop","gugi","Mik","nomiq","nomisyq","nu."]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She woke.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das rizid.`","answers":["Das rizid."],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We are waiting.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Muk wastiq.`","answers":["Muk wastiq."],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I have not eaten.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nes nomik.`","answers":["Mik nes nomik."],"points":10,"bank":["gugi","jofi","Mik","nes","nomik."]},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Biba usiq.`","type":"type-en","key":"The baby is sleeping.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das jofidyk mik.`","type":"type-en","key":"She had helped me.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nes etypik nibop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I have not got the book.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L10","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 10","title":"Put time in its slot","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say <em>when</em> something happened as well as marking it on the verb, and say how often you do things.</p>\n<h3>1. Time goes at the end</h3>\n<p>Fiwo sentences have a fourth slot, after the object, reserved for time.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Subject → Verb → Object → Time</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomis batap fitydu.</strong> — I will eat the food tomorrow.\n<strong>Muk gugid cihu.</strong> — We stood in the morning.\n<strong>Das rizid cihu.</strong> — She woke in the morning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You do not need a word for &quot;in&quot; or &quot;on&quot;. The slot itself says <em>this is when</em>.</p>\n<p>Notice the verb still carries its tense. Fiwo marks time twice — once grammatically on the verb, once lexically in Slot 4. They must agree.</p>\n<h3>2. The rest of the calendar</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>retadu</code></td>\n<td>week</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>gomu</code></td>\n<td>hour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rugoxu</code></td>\n<td>month</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>mepu</code></td>\n<td>minute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jaru</code></td>\n<td>year</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>cimi</code></td>\n<td>to begin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>amute</code></td>\n<td>early</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>fefu</code></td>\n<td>end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hede</code></td>\n<td>late</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>3. How often</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ovce</code></td>\n<td>always</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>amce</code></td>\n<td>never</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tsenge</code></td>\n<td>often</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>evle</code></td>\n<td>sometimes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>strene</code></td>\n<td>soon</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are modifiers, so they look left — put them after the verb they describe.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jipri wusap mike nudu.</strong> — I wash my hand today.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>4. A note for later</h3>\n<p>To say a compound time like <em>tomorrow night</em>, Fiwo turns the second word into a modifier (<code>fitydu dugue</code>). That uses derivation, which you meet properly in Lesson 36. For now, one time word per sentence.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"retadu","english":"week"},{"fiwo":"gomu","english":"hour"},{"fiwo":"rugoxu","english":"month"},{"fiwo":"mepu","english":"minute"},{"fiwo":"jaru","english":"year"},{"fiwo":"cimi","english":"to begin"},{"fiwo":"amute","english":"early"},{"fiwo":"fefu","english":"end"},{"fiwo":"hede","english":"late"},{"fiwo":"ovce","english":"always"},{"fiwo":"amce","english":"never"},{"fiwo":"tsenge","english":"often"},{"fiwo":"evle","english":"sometimes"},{"fiwo":"strene","english":"soon"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nomis batap fitydu.","english":"I will eat the food tomorrow."},{"fiwo":"Muk gugid cihu.","english":"We stood in the morning."},{"fiwo":"Das rizid cihu.","english":"She woke in the morning."},{"fiwo":"Mik jipri wusap mike nudu.","english":"I wash my hand today."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I will eat tomorrow.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomis fitydu.`","answers":["Mik nomis fitydu."],"points":10,"bank":["amute","fitydu.","hede","Mik","nomis"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She slept at night.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das usid dugu.`","answers":["Das usid dugu."],"points":10,"bank":["Das","dugu.","evle","retadu","usid"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We played today.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Muk nedcid nudu.`","answers":["Muk nedcid nudu."],"points":10,"bank":["amute","Muk","nedcid","nudu.","tsenge"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk gugid cihu.`","type":"type-en","key":"We stood in the morning.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das usid dugu.`","type":"type-en","key":"She slept at night.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk nedci nudu.`","type":"type-en","key":"We play today.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Legal, but wrong.","text":"`Mik nomis batap wecdu.` — the parser accepts this without complaint. Why is it still a broken sentence?","type":"explain","key":"It is structurally perfect — the parser checks *grammar*, not *sense*. But the verb says *will eat* while Slot 4 says *yesterday*, so the sentence is nonsense with a flawless parse. Fiwo guarantees that every sentence has exactly one reading; it does not guarantee that reading is true or sensible. That job stays yours. Fix by making them agree: `Mik nomid batap wecdu.` or `Mik nomis batap fitydu.`","answers":["Mik nomid batap wecdu.","Mik nomis batap fitydu."],"points":6}]},{"id":"L11","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 11","title":"Your body and how you feel","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say you are unwell, name the rest of your body, and talk about a doctor.</p>\n<h3>1. The rest of the body</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ovpa</code></td>\n<td>neck</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>dafa</code></td>\n<td>stomach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tukpa</code></td>\n<td>finger</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>maoba</code></td>\n<td>heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bela</code></td>\n<td>foot</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>boja</code></td>\n<td>blood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dorala</code></td>\n<td>back</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>cogja</code></td>\n<td>skin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>You met <code>maoba</code> in Lesson 1&#39;s drill, paired with <code>dwedu</code> — the physical heart versus the emotional core. Same stem, one vowel apart, and now you know why.</p>\n<h3>2. Health</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>farlopa</code></td>\n<td>doctor</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>luhaci</code></td>\n<td>to heal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nasbo</code></td>\n<td>medicine</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>ceni</code></td>\n<td>to live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>picalo</code></td>\n<td>hospital</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>maji</code></td>\n<td>to breathe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>iwe</code></td>\n<td>tired</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kimugi</code></td>\n<td>to die</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>ak</code> is a standalone word: <em>ouch</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Soxa mike iwei.</strong> — My body is tired.\n<strong>Farlopap luhacid mik.</strong> — The doctor healed me.\n<strong>Mik roli nasbop.</strong> — I drink the medicine.\n<strong>Das maji.</strong> — She breathes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Putting Phase 2 together</h3>\n<p>Everything from Lessons 6–11 in one place:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mana mike iwei nudu.</strong> — My mother is tired today.\n<strong>Farlopap luhacidyk taqa mike.</strong> — The doctor had healed my brother.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"ovpa","english":"neck"},{"fiwo":"dafa","english":"stomach"},{"fiwo":"tukpa","english":"finger"},{"fiwo":"maoba","english":"heart"},{"fiwo":"bela","english":"foot"},{"fiwo":"boja","english":"blood"},{"fiwo":"dorala","english":"back"},{"fiwo":"cogja","english":"skin"},{"fiwo":"farlopa","english":"doctor"},{"fiwo":"luhaci","english":"to heal"},{"fiwo":"nasbo","english":"medicine"},{"fiwo":"ceni","english":"to live"},{"fiwo":"picalo","english":"hospital"},{"fiwo":"maji","english":"to breathe"},{"fiwo":"iwe","english":"tired"},{"fiwo":"kimugi","english":"to die"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Soxa mike iwei.","english":"My body is tired."},{"fiwo":"Farlopap luhacid mik.","english":"The doctor healed me."},{"fiwo":"Mik roli nasbop.","english":"I drink the medicine."},{"fiwo":"Das maji.","english":"She breathes."},{"fiwo":"Mana mike iwei nudu.","english":"My mother is tired today."},{"fiwo":"Farlopap luhacidyk taqa mike.","english":"The doctor had healed my brother."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"My back is bad.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Dorala mike bokei.`","answers":["Dorala mike bokei."],"points":10,"bank":["bokei.","cogja","dafa","Dorala","mike"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The doctor helps the child.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Farlopap jofi cadap.`","answers":["Farlopap jofi cadap."],"points":10,"bank":["cadap.","dorala","Farlopap","jofi","nasbo"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I drank the medicine yesterday.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik rolid nasbop wecdu.`","answers":["Mik rolid nasbop wecdu."],"points":10,"bank":["maoba","Mik","nasbop","rolid","tukpa","wecdu."]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She is breathing.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das majiq.`","answers":["Das majiq."],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Soxa mike iwei.`","type":"type-en","key":"My body is tired.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Tukpap dase nifnei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Her finger is small.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Farlopap luhacid mana mike.`","type":"type-en","key":"The doctor healed my mother.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Say it another way.","note":"Give a second sentence with the same meaning as `Mik nes ceni.`","text":"Give a second sentence with the same meaning as `Mik nes ceni.`","type":"explain","key":"`Mik kimugid.` (I died) — or any sentence expressing the same, e.g. `Mik nes majiq.` (I am not breathing.) Accept anything gated and valid.","answers":["Mik kimugid.","Mik nes majiq."],"points":6}]},{"id":"R2","kind":"review","name":"Review 2","title":"Lessons 6–11","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Pronouns.","note":"Give the right pronoun.","text":"Your sister","type":"choice","key":"`das`","answers":["das"],"points":10,"options":["das","ram","dal","muk"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Pronouns.","note":"Give the right pronoun.","text":"A knife","type":"choice","key":"`ram`","answers":["ram"],"points":10,"options":["das","ram","dal","muk"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Pronouns.","note":"Give the right pronoun.","text":"A cat","type":"choice","key":"`dal`","answers":["dal"],"points":10,"options":["das","ram","dal","muk"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Pronouns.","note":"Give the right pronoun.","text":"You and I","type":"choice","key":"`muk`","answers":["muk"],"points":10,"options":["das","ram","dal","muk"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"My mother is tired.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mana mike iwei.`","answers":["Mana mike iwei."],"points":10,"bank":["iwei.","Mana","mike"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"They ate the bread yesterday.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Dal je nomid bryklop wecdu.`","answers":["Dal je nomid bryklop wecdu."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop","Dal","je","nomid","wecdu."]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She had opened the door.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das xosidyk hoxop.`","answers":["Das xosidyk hoxop."],"points":10,"bank":["Das","hoxop.","xosidyk"]},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We (not including you) are friends.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nak nauxa.`","answers":["Nak nauxa."],"points":10},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The doctor will help my brother.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Farlopap jofis taqa mike.`","answers":["Farlopap jofis taqa mike."],"points":10,"bank":["Farlopap","jofis","mike.","taqa"]},{"n":10,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"His hand is small.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Wusap daqe nifnei.`","answers":["Wusap daqe nifnei."],"points":10,"bank":["daqe","nifnei.","Wusap"]},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nibop suke jaxkei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Your book is new.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nes nomik.`","type":"type-en","key":"I have not eaten.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":13,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk nedcid nudu.`","type":"type-en","key":"We played today.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":14,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Tsejap dase capei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Her eye is black.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":15,"part":"D","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `jipri` (to wash):","text":"was washing","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`jipridyq`","answers":["jipridyq"],"points":10},{"n":16,"part":"D","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `jipri` (to wash):","text":"has washed","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`jiprik`","answers":["jiprik"],"points":10},{"n":17,"part":"D","prompt":"Build the verb.","note":"From `jipri` (to wash):","text":"will wash","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`jipris`","answers":["jipris"],"points":10},{"n":18,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: introduce a family member, say one thing about their body, say what they did yesterday, and what they will do tomorrow. Four sentences, aloud, in one go.","text":"Without looking: introduce a family member, say one thing about their body, say what they did yesterday, and what they will do tomorrow. Four sentences, aloud, in one go.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L12","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 12","title":"Ask a yes/no question and answer it","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> ask a question, answer one, and ask why.</p>\n<h3>1. Questions are flagged at the front</h3>\n<p>Fiwo does not use a rising tone or a question mark to make a question. It puts a tag at the very start of the sentence: <code>Kup</code>.</p>\n<p>Everything after it stays in normal SVO order. Nothing moves.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk nomid batap?</strong> — Did you eat the food?\n<strong>Kup das usiq?</strong> — Is she sleeping?\n<strong>Kup biba nomik?</strong> — Has the baby eaten?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Compare with English, which drags the verb to the front (<em>did you eat</em>) or adds a helper word. Fiwo changes nothing but the tag. The statement <code>Suk nomid batap.</code> becomes a question by adding one word at the front.</p>\n<h3>2. Answering</h3>\n<p>A question with no question-word inside it is a <strong>polar question</strong> — it expects exactly one of two answers.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>jas</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nes</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk nomid batap?</strong> — Did you eat the food?\n<strong>Jas.</strong> — Yes.\n<strong>Jas. Mik nomid batap.</strong> — Yes. I ate the food.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>jas</code> and <code>nes</code> stand completely alone. They are not part of the sentence that follows — they finish their own utterance, and the parser starts fresh afterwards.</p>\n<h3>3. Short answers are legal</h3>\n<p>Real conversation is full of replies that are not full sentences. Fiwo allows a bare noun phrase or modifier as a complete answer, and it binds to the question just asked.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk lolei?</strong> — Are you hungry?\n<strong>Jas.</strong> — Yes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You will meet the full range of these fragments later. For now: <code>jas</code>, <code>nes</code>, and short phrases answering a question are all legal.</p>\n<h3>4. Asking why</h3>\n<p><em>Why?</em> on its own is so common that Fiwo gives it a special licence. <code>wal</code> may stand alone as a whole utterance, and it attaches to whatever was just said.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nes nomis mitap.</strong> — I will not eat the meat.\n<strong>Wal?</strong> — Why?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can also say <code>Kup wal?</code> — both are correct. This is the <em>only</em> question word that does not need the <code>Kup</code> tag, precisely because it is not buried inside a sentence: it <em>is</em> the sentence.</p>\n<h3>5. More food</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>vyfla</code></td>\n<td>rice</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>zuta</code></td>\n<td>fruit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mita</code></td>\n<td>meat</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>zoba</code></td>\n<td>vegetable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fuba</code></td>\n<td>fish</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>lole</code></td>\n<td>hungry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>irta</code></td>\n<td>egg</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>rodipe</code></td>\n<td>thirsty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik lolei.</strong> — I am hungry.\n<strong>Mik rodipei.</strong> — I am thirsty.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"jas","english":"yes"},{"fiwo":"nes","english":"no"},{"fiwo":"vyfla","english":"rice"},{"fiwo":"zuta","english":"fruit"},{"fiwo":"mita","english":"meat"},{"fiwo":"zoba","english":"vegetable"},{"fiwo":"fuba","english":"fish"},{"fiwo":"lole","english":"hungry"},{"fiwo":"irta","english":"egg"},{"fiwo":"rodipe","english":"thirsty"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Kup suk nomid batap?","english":"Did you eat the food?"},{"fiwo":"Kup das usiq?","english":"Is she sleeping?"},{"fiwo":"Kup biba nomik?","english":"Has the baby eaten?"},{"fiwo":"Kup suk nomid batap?","english":"Did you eat the food?"},{"fiwo":"Jas.","english":"Yes."},{"fiwo":"Jas. Mik nomid batap.","english":"Yes. I ate the food."},{"fiwo":"Kup suk lolei?","english":"Are you hungry?"},{"fiwo":"Jas.","english":"Yes."},{"fiwo":"Mik nes nomis mitap.","english":"I will not eat the meat."},{"fiwo":"Wal?","english":"Why?"},{"fiwo":"Mik lolei.","english":"I am hungry."},{"fiwo":"Mik rodipei.","english":"I am thirsty."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Jas.","Mik","nomid","batap."],"blank":0,"answer":"Jas","pre":"","post":".","english":"Yes. I ate the food.","fiwo":"Jas. Mik nomid batap."},{"tokens":["Mik","nes","nomis","mitap."],"blank":1,"answer":"nes","pre":"","post":"","english":"I will not eat the meat.","fiwo":"Mik nes nomis mitap."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Turn these statements into questions.","text":"`Suk usid.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk usid?`","answers":["Kup suk usid?"],"points":10,"bank":["jas","Kup","nes","suk","usid?"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Turn these statements into questions.","text":"`Das nomiq bryklop.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup das nomiq bryklop?`","answers":["Kup das nomiq bryklop?"],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop?","das","Kup","lole","nes","nomiq"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Turn these statements into questions.","text":"`Mana suke iwei.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup mana suke iwei?`","answers":["Kup mana suke iwei?"],"points":10,"bank":["iwei?","Kup","mana","mita","suke","zuta"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Did you eat the fish?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk nomid fubap?`","answers":["Kup suk nomid fubap?"],"points":10,"bank":["fuba","fubap?","irta","Kup","nomid","suk"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Are you hungry?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk lolei?`","answers":["Kup suk lolei?"],"points":10,"bank":["irta","Kup","lolei?","suk","zoba"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"No. I am thirsty.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nes. Mik rodipei.`","answers":["Nes. Mik rodipei."],"points":10,"bank":["Mik","Nes.","rodipe","rodipei.","zuta"]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Why?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Wal?` or `Kup wal?`","answers":["Wal?","Kup wal?"],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup suk nomis mitap?`","type":"type-en","key":"Will you eat the meat?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nes. Mik nomis fubap.`","type":"type-en","key":"No. I will eat the fish.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup biba usiq?`","type":"type-en","key":"Is the baby sleeping?","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L13","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 13","title":"Ask who, what, and how many","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> ask for specific information instead of just yes or no.</p>\n<h3>1. Question words do not move</h3>\n<p>This is the rule that surprises every English speaker. In English, <em>What did you find?</em> rips &quot;what&quot; out of the object slot and throws it to the front. Fiwo forbids that completely.</p>\n<p>The question word stays <strong>exactly where the answer will go</strong>. It is a blank waiting to be filled.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk karxid wat?</strong> — What did you find?\n<em>(literally: Question + you + found + WHAT — the blank sits in the object slot)</em></p>\n<p>Answer: <strong>Mik karxid lusop.</strong> — I found the key. The answer drops straight into the same slot.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. The question words</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Stands for</th>\n<th>Sits in</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>wun</code></td>\n<td>who (a person, doing something)</td>\n<td>subject slot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>won</code></td>\n<td>what (a thing or event, doing something)</td>\n<td>subject slot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wat</code></td>\n<td>what (being acted on)</td>\n<td>object slot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wis</code></td>\n<td>how (in what manner)</td>\n<td>after the verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wug</code></td>\n<td>how many</td>\n<td>after the noun</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup wun xosid hoxop?</strong> — Who opened the door?\n<strong>Kup won cimid?</strong> — What began?\n<strong>Kup suk jipris wat?</strong> — What will you wash?\n<strong>Kup suk nedcid wis?</strong> — How did you play?\n<strong>Kup suk karxid nibo wug?</strong> — How many books did you find?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note the split between <code>wun</code> and <code>won</code>: a person versus a thing. It mirrors <code>dal</code> and <code>ram</code> from Lesson 6 — Fiwo makes you commit to whether the unknown has a mind.</p>\n<p><strong>Every one of these needs <code>Kup</code>.</strong> Because the question word is buried in the middle of the sentence, the parser needs warning at the front that a blank is coming. The only exception is the standalone <code>Wal?</code> you met in Lesson 12.</p>\n<h3>3. Asking what something is</h3>\n<p>To ask <em>What is your name?</em> there is no question word at all. Identity statements have no verb (Lesson 2&#39;s Zero Copula), so there is no slot for a blank. Instead, put <code>Kup</code> in front of the noun phrase whose value you want.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup hisup suke?</strong> — What is your name?\n<strong>Kup sil?</strong> — What is this?\n<strong>Kup nibop suke?</strong> — Which is your book?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>hisu</code> = name. This is the pattern for asking what anything is.</p>\n<h3>4. Words for thinking and speaking</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>kani</code></td>\n<td>to know</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>slezi</code></td>\n<td>to ask</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pozi</code></td>\n<td>to think</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>pywi</code></td>\n<td>to listen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gesi</code></td>\n<td>to say</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>upyci</code></td>\n<td>to look</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Plus <code>hisu</code> = name, and two place words: <code>tapo</code> = here, <code>poco</code> = there.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup wun slezid?</strong> — Who asked?\n<strong>Kup suk kani hisup mike?</strong> — Do you know my name?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>5. Where and when — coming later</h3>\n<p>You may have noticed there is no <em>where</em> or <em>why-because</em> here. Those question words attach to prepositions and clause-joiners you have not met yet — they arrive in Lesson 31 and Lesson 21. Everything you learn now stays true when they do.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"wun","english":"who (a person, doing something)"},{"fiwo":"won","english":"what (a thing or event, doing something)"},{"fiwo":"wat","english":"what (being acted on)"},{"fiwo":"wis","english":"how (in what manner)"},{"fiwo":"wug","english":"how many"},{"fiwo":"kani","english":"to know"},{"fiwo":"slezi","english":"to ask"},{"fiwo":"pozi","english":"to think"},{"fiwo":"pywi","english":"to listen"},{"fiwo":"gesi","english":"to say"},{"fiwo":"upyci","english":"to look"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Kup suk karxid wat?","english":"What did you find?"},{"fiwo":"Kup wun xosid hoxop?","english":"Who opened the door?"},{"fiwo":"Kup won cimid?","english":"What began?"},{"fiwo":"Kup suk jipris wat?","english":"What will you wash?"},{"fiwo":"Kup suk nedcid wis?","english":"How did you play?"},{"fiwo":"Kup suk karxid nibo wug?","english":"How many books did you find?"},{"fiwo":"Kup hisup suke?","english":"What is your name?"},{"fiwo":"Kup sil?","english":"What is this?"},{"fiwo":"Kup nibop suke?","english":"Which is your book?"},{"fiwo":"Kup wun slezid?","english":"Who asked?"},{"fiwo":"Kup suk kani hisup mike?","english":"Do you know my name?"}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Kup","suk","karxid","wat?"],"blank":3,"answer":"wat","pre":"","post":"?","english":"What did you find?","fiwo":"Kup suk karxid wat?"},{"tokens":["Kup","wun","xosid","hoxop?"],"blank":1,"answer":"wun","pre":"","post":"","english":"Who opened the door?","fiwo":"Kup wun xosid hoxop?"},{"tokens":["Kup","won","cimid?"],"blank":1,"answer":"won","pre":"","post":"","english":"What began?","fiwo":"Kup won cimid?"},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","jipris","wat?"],"blank":3,"answer":"wat","pre":"","post":"?","english":"What will you wash?","fiwo":"Kup suk jipris wat?"},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","nedcid","wis?"],"blank":3,"answer":"wis","pre":"","post":"?","english":"How did you play?","fiwo":"Kup suk nedcid wis?"},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","karxid","nibo","wug?"],"blank":4,"answer":"wug","pre":"","post":"?","english":"How many books did you find?","fiwo":"Kup suk karxid nibo wug?"},{"tokens":["Kup","wun","slezid?"],"blank":1,"answer":"wun","pre":"","post":"","english":"Who asked?","fiwo":"Kup wun slezid?"},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","kani","hisup","mike?"],"blank":2,"answer":"kani","pre":"","post":"","english":"Do you know my name?","fiwo":"Kup suk kani hisup mike?"}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Who opened the window?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup wun xosid wilop?`","answers":["Kup wun xosid wilop?"],"points":10,"bank":["gesi","Kup","wat","wilop?","wun","xosid"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"What did you find?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk karxid wat?`","answers":["Kup suk karxid wat?"],"points":10,"bank":["karxid","Kup","suk","upyci","wat?","wis"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"How many children?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup cada wug?`","answers":["Kup cada wug?"],"points":10,"bank":["cada","gesi","Kup","pywi","wug?"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"What is your name?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup hisup suke?`","answers":["Kup hisup suke?"],"points":10,"bank":["gesi","hisup","Kup","suke?","won"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup suk nedcid wis?`","type":"type-en","key":"How did you play?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup wun slezid?`","type":"type-en","key":"Who asked?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup sil?`","type":"type-en","key":"What is this?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Answer the question in Fiwo.","note":"Give any sensible full-sentence answer.","text":"`Kup suk karxid wat?`","type":"explain","key":"Any valid answer with the object filled, e.g. `Mik karxid lusop.`","answers":["Mik karxid lusop."],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Answer the question in Fiwo.","note":"Give any sensible full-sentence answer.","text":"`Kup wun jofid suk?`","type":"explain","key":"Any valid answer with the subject filled, e.g. `Mana mike jofid mik.`","answers":["Mana mike jofid mik."],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"D","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Wat suk karxid?` — the learner moved the question word to the front, English-style.","type":"explain","key":"Fiwo forbids moving question words. `wat` must stay in the object slot, and the sentence must open with `Kup`: `Kup suk karxid wat?`","answers":["Kup suk karxid wat?"],"points":6}]},{"id":"L14","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 14","title":"Give a command, make a request, be polite","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> tell someone to do something, ask politely, and address someone with respect.</p>\n<h3>1. Commands get their own tag</h3>\n<p>Just as <code>Kup</code> marks a question, <code>Kop</code> marks a command. It goes first.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kop suk gugi.</strong> — Stand!\n<strong>Kop suk seji.</strong> — Stop!</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Naming the subject is optional in a command, but including <code>suk</code> is normal and clear.</p>\n<h3>2. Please stands alone</h3>\n<p><code>cef</code> means <em>please</em> — but it is a standalone particle, not part of the sentence. English tacks &quot;please&quot; onto the end of a request; <strong>in Fiwo that is illegal</strong>. It must be its own utterance.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ <code>Kop suk jofi mik cef.</code>\n<strong>Cef. Kop suk jofi mik.</strong> — Please. Help me.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The same is true of <code>sal</code>, <code>tex</code>, <code>jas</code> and <code>nes</code>. These words live outside the sentence entirely — they finish their own utterance and the parser resets.</p>\n<h3>3. Respect is built into the pronoun</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Use with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>suk</code></td>\n<td>friends, family, peers, children</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>suv</code></td>\n<td>elders, strangers, professionals, authority</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kop suk neki.</strong> — Sit down. (to a friend)\n<strong>Kop suv neki.</strong> — Please be seated. (to an elder or stranger)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The possessive follows the same pattern: <code>suke</code> (casual) and <code>suve</code> (formal), both from Lesson 7.</p>\n<p>Choosing <code>suk</code> for a stranger is not a grammar error — it is a social one. The parser will accept it; the person will notice.</p>\n<h3>4. More verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>xyfi</code></td>\n<td>to do</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>purki</code></td>\n<td>to turn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nyrsi</code></td>\n<td>to carry, bring</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>ombi</code></td>\n<td>to send</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sigi</code></td>\n<td>to hold</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>uropi</code></td>\n<td>to sing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik sigi nibop.</strong> — I hold the book.\n<strong>Muk uropi.</strong> — We sing.\n<strong>Mik ombis nibop.</strong> — I will send the book.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"suk","english":"friends, family, peers, children"},{"fiwo":"suv","english":"elders, strangers, professionals, authority"},{"fiwo":"xyfi","english":"to do"},{"fiwo":"purki","english":"to turn"},{"fiwo":"nyrsi","english":"to carry, bring"},{"fiwo":"ombi","english":"to send"},{"fiwo":"sigi","english":"to hold"},{"fiwo":"uropi","english":"to sing"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Kop suk gugi.","english":"Stand!"},{"fiwo":"Kop suk seji.","english":"Stop!"},{"fiwo":"Cef. Kop suk jofi mik.","english":"Please. Help me."},{"fiwo":"Kop suk neki.","english":"Sit down. (to a friend)"},{"fiwo":"Kop suv neki.","english":"Please be seated. (to an elder or stranger)"},{"fiwo":"Mik sigi nibop.","english":"I hold the book."},{"fiwo":"Muk uropi.","english":"We sing."},{"fiwo":"Mik ombis nibop.","english":"I will send the book."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Kop","suk","gugi."],"blank":1,"answer":"suk","pre":"","post":"","english":"Stand!","fiwo":"Kop suk gugi."},{"tokens":["Kop","suk","seji."],"blank":1,"answer":"suk","pre":"","post":"","english":"Stop!","fiwo":"Kop suk seji."},{"tokens":["Cef.","Kop","suk","jofi","mik."],"blank":2,"answer":"suk","pre":"","post":"","english":"Please. Help me.","fiwo":"Cef. Kop suk jofi mik."},{"tokens":["Kop","suk","neki."],"blank":1,"answer":"suk","pre":"","post":"","english":"Sit down. (to a friend)","fiwo":"Kop suk neki."},{"tokens":["Kop","suv","neki."],"blank":1,"answer":"suv","pre":"","post":"","english":"Please be seated. (to an elder or stranger)","fiwo":"Kop suv neki."},{"tokens":["Mik","sigi","nibop."],"blank":1,"answer":"sigi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I hold the book.","fiwo":"Mik sigi nibop."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Stop!","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kop suk seji.`","answers":["Kop suk seji."],"points":10,"bank":["Kop","ombi","seji.","sigi","suk"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Please. Help me.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Cef. Kop suk jofi mik.`","answers":["Cef. Kop suk jofi mik."],"points":10,"bank":["Cef.","jofi","Kop","mik.","suk","suv","uropi"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Sit down. (to an elder)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kop suv neki.`","answers":["Kop suv neki."],"points":10,"bank":["Kop","neki.","purki","suv","xyfi"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Bring the water.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kop suk nyrsi dorsop.`","answers":["Kop suk nyrsi dorsop."],"points":10,"bank":["dorsop.","Kop","nyrsi","purki","suk","uropi"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kop suk purki.`","type":"type-en","key":"Turn!","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kop suv jofi mana mike.`","type":"type-en","key":"Please help my mother. (formal)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk uropid wecdu.`","type":"type-en","key":"We sang yesterday.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Fix the mistake.","text":"✗ `Kop suk nyrsi dorsop cef.` — the learner put *please* at the end.","type":"explain","key":"`cef` is a standalone particle and may never trail inside a clause. Correct: `Cef. Kop suk nyrsi dorsop.`","answers":["Cef. Kop suk nyrsi dorsop."],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"D","prompt":"Which pronoun — `suk` or `suv`?","text":"Asking your sister to sit","type":"choice","key":"`suk`","answers":["suk"],"points":10,"options":["suk","suv"]},{"n":10,"part":"D","prompt":"Which pronoun — `suk` or `suv`?","text":"Asking a doctor you have just met to sit","type":"choice","key":"`suv`","answers":["suv"],"points":10,"options":["suk","suv"]}]},{"id":"L15","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 15","title":"Colour what you say","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> mark a sentence as a fact, a feeling, a joke, or a hypothetical — including sarcasm, out loud, unmistakably.</p>\n<h3>1. Tone is a word, not a voice</h3>\n<p>In English, <em>Nice work</em> can be praise or an insult, and only tone tells you which. Machines cannot hear tone, and neither can a reader. Fiwo puts the tone at the front of the sentence as an actual word.</p>\n<p>You have already used two of these: <code>Kup</code> for questions and <code>Kop</code> for commands. Here is the rest of the set.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tag</th>\n<th>Marks the sentence as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Kep</code></td>\n<td>an objective fact — scientific truth, or strong emphasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hap</code></td>\n<td>a feeling — subjective, emotional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hop</code></td>\n<td><strong>sarcasm</strong> — say the opposite of what you mean, on purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hyp</code></td>\n<td>hypothetical — not real, a simulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kep nofa maji.</strong> — It is a fact that people breathe.\n<strong>Hap mik kypei.</strong> — I am <em>so</em> happy!\n<strong>Hop suk sapid batap gawe.</strong> — Oh, <em>great</em> food you made. (sarcastic — you mean it was bad)\n<strong>Hyp mik nomis vyflap.</strong> — I would eat the rice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. <code>Hop</code> is doing something no natural language does</h3>\n<p>Sarcasm is one of the hardest problems in language processing: the words say one thing and mean the opposite, and nothing in the sentence marks the flip. Fiwo marks it. <code>Hop</code> tells the listener — human or machine — to invert the meaning.</p>\n<p>This means Fiwo sarcasm is never missed and never accidental. You cannot be sarcastic by accident, and no one can pretend they did not notice.</p>\n<h3>3. Tags are optional — with one exception</h3>\n<p>If you leave the tag off, the sentence is simply neutral. In everyday speech people drop them and let their voice do the work.</p>\n<p>The exception is <code>Kup</code>: any sentence with a question word buried in it <strong>must</strong> carry it, or the parser cannot find the blank.</p>\n<p>Also note <code>Kep</code> is stronger than English &quot;it&#39;s a fact&quot;. Reserve it for genuine truths and real emphasis — using it for <em>the weather is nice</em> sounds absurd.</p>\n<h3>4. Feelings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>kype</code></td>\n<td>happy</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>lofni</code></td>\n<td>to laugh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>goile</code></td>\n<td>sad</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>rehi</code></td>\n<td>to cry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tsake</code></td>\n<td>angry</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>briwi</code></td>\n<td>to be afraid</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik goilei.</strong> — I am sad.\n<strong>Das tsakei.</strong> — She is angry.\n<strong>Cadap briwid.</strong> — The child was afraid.\n<strong>Biba rehiq.</strong> — The baby is crying.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note <code>kype</code>, <code>goile</code> and <code>tsake</code> are modifiers — they need the stative <code>-i</code> from Lesson 5 to become a sentence. <code>lofni</code>, <code>rehi</code> and <code>briwi</code> are already verbs.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"Kep","english":"an objective fact — scientific truth, or strong emphasis"},{"fiwo":"Hap","english":"a feeling — subjective, emotional"},{"fiwo":"Hyp","english":"hypothetical — not real, a simulation"},{"fiwo":"kype","english":"happy"},{"fiwo":"lofni","english":"to laugh"},{"fiwo":"goile","english":"sad"},{"fiwo":"rehi","english":"to cry"},{"fiwo":"tsake","english":"angry"},{"fiwo":"briwi","english":"to be afraid"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Kep nofa maji.","english":"It is a fact that people breathe."},{"fiwo":"Hap mik kypei.","english":"I am *so* happy!"},{"fiwo":"Hop suk sapid batap gawe.","english":"Oh, *great* food you made. (sarcastic — you mean it was bad)"},{"fiwo":"Hyp mik nomis vyflap.","english":"I would eat the rice."},{"fiwo":"Mik goilei.","english":"I am sad."},{"fiwo":"Das tsakei.","english":"She is angry."},{"fiwo":"Cadap briwid.","english":"The child was afraid."},{"fiwo":"Biba rehiq.","english":"The baby is crying."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Kep","nofa","maji."],"blank":0,"answer":"Kep","pre":"","post":"","english":"It is a fact that people breathe.","fiwo":"Kep nofa maji."},{"tokens":["Hap","mik","kypei."],"blank":0,"answer":"Hap","pre":"","post":"","english":"I am *so* happy!","fiwo":"Hap mik kypei."},{"tokens":["Hyp","mik","nomis","vyflap."],"blank":0,"answer":"Hyp","pre":"","post":"","english":"I would eat the rice.","fiwo":"Hyp mik nomis vyflap."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Which tag?","text":"You are stating a scientific fact.","type":"choice","key":"`Kep`","answers":["Kep"],"points":10,"options":["Kep","Hop","Hyp","Hap"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Which tag?","text":"You mean the opposite of what you are saying.","type":"choice","key":"`Hop`","answers":["Hop"],"points":10,"options":["Kep","Hop","Hyp","Hap"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Which tag?","text":"You are imagining something untrue.","type":"choice","key":"`Hyp`","answers":["Hyp"],"points":10,"options":["Kep","Hop","Hyp","Hap"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Which tag?","text":"You are expressing strong personal feeling.","type":"choice","key":"`Hap`","answers":["Hap"],"points":10,"options":["Kep","Hop","Hyp","Hap"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am sad.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik goilei.`","answers":["Mik goilei."],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The child is crying.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Biba rehiq.`","answers":["Biba rehiq."],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Oh, *wonderful*, you ate my food. (sarcastic)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Hop suk nomid batap mike.`","answers":["Hop suk nomid batap mike."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","Hop","kype","mike.","nomid","rehi","suk"]},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I would sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Hyp mik usis.`","answers":["Hyp mik usis."],"points":10,"bank":["Hyp","kype","lofni","mik","usis."]},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Hap mik kypei!`","type":"type-en","key":"I am so happy!","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kep nofa maji.`","type":"type-en","key":"It is a fact that people breathe.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das tsakei nudu.`","type":"type-en","key":"She is angry today.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L16","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 16","title":"Sound like a person","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> react, sympathise, laugh, and say <em>how you know</em> what you are claiming.</p>\n<h3>1. Particles that stand outside the sentence</h3>\n<p>These words carry no grammar. They are complete utterances on their own, and the sentence after them starts fresh. You have already been using <code>sal</code>, <code>tex</code>, <code>jas</code>, <code>nes</code> and <code>cef</code> this way.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>nel</code></td>\n<td>you&#39;re welcome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ov</code></td>\n<td>oh no (sympathy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tox</code></td>\n<td>cheers (a toast)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>xex</code></td>\n<td>haha</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wox</code></td>\n<td>wow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ha</code></td>\n<td>oh, I see</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jo</code></td>\n<td>hey! (attention)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ak</code></td>\n<td>ouch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Ha. Mik kani nu.</strong> — Oh. I know now.\n<strong>Ov. Mana suke iwei.</strong> — Oh no. Your mother is tired.\n<strong>Jo! Kop suk seji.</strong> — Hey! Stop.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>They never take suffixes and never sit inside a clause. If you want one, it goes on its own.</p>\n<h3>2. Saying how you know</h3>\n<p>This is a feature English lacks entirely. Fiwo lets you mark the <em>source</em> of your information, in one word.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tag</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Sop</code></td>\n<td>I saw/heard/felt it myself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Nop</code></td>\n<td>I worked it out from evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Rop</code></td>\n<td>someone told me</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sop das usid.</strong> — She slept — I saw her.\n<strong>Nop daq usiq.</strong> — He is sleeping — I infer it (his light is off).\n<strong>Rop mana mike iwei.</strong> — My mother is tired — I was told.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Three sentences, identical content, completely different claims about your own reliability. English needs a whole clause for this; Fiwo needs three letters. Use them — they make you a more honest speaker.</p>\n<h3>3. Four more thinking verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>erfi</code></td>\n<td>to understand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rari</code></td>\n<td>to remember</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ebnesi</code></td>\n<td>to forget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fenuti</code></td>\n<td>to tell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik erfi.</strong> — I understand.\n<strong>Mik rarid hisup suke.</strong> — I remembered your name.\n<strong>Mik ebnesid.</strong> — I forgot.\n<strong>Das fenutid mik.</strong> — She told me.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>A full conversation</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sal!</strong> — Hello!\n<strong>Sal. Kup hisup suke?</strong> — Hello. What is your name?\n<strong>Mik Josh. Kup suk lolei?</strong> — I am Josh. Are you hungry?\n<strong>Jas. Mik nes nomid nudu.</strong> — Yes. I have not eaten today.\n<strong>Ov. Cef. Kop suk nomi bryklop mike.</strong> — Oh no. Please. Eat my bread.\n<strong>Mik grawi suk.</strong> — Thank you.\n<strong>Nel. Tex!</strong> — You&#39;re welcome. Goodbye!</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read that aloud. Fifteen lessons ago you could not say a word of it.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"nel","english":"you're welcome"},{"fiwo":"ov","english":"oh no (sympathy)"},{"fiwo":"tox","english":"cheers (a toast)"},{"fiwo":"xex","english":"haha"},{"fiwo":"wox","english":"wow"},{"fiwo":"ha","english":"oh, I see"},{"fiwo":"jo","english":"hey! (attention)"},{"fiwo":"ak","english":"ouch"},{"fiwo":"Sop","english":"I saw/heard/felt it myself"},{"fiwo":"Nop","english":"I worked it out from evidence"},{"fiwo":"Rop","english":"someone told me"},{"fiwo":"erfi","english":"to understand"},{"fiwo":"rari","english":"to remember"},{"fiwo":"ebnesi","english":"to forget"},{"fiwo":"fenuti","english":"to tell"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Ha. Mik kani nu.","english":"Oh. I know now."},{"fiwo":"Ov. Mana suke iwei.","english":"Oh no. Your mother is tired."},{"fiwo":"Jo! Kop suk seji.","english":"Hey! Stop."},{"fiwo":"Sop das usid.","english":"She slept — I saw her."},{"fiwo":"Nop daq usiq.","english":"He is sleeping — I infer it (his light is off)."},{"fiwo":"Rop mana mike iwei.","english":"My mother is tired — I was told."},{"fiwo":"Mik erfi.","english":"I understand."},{"fiwo":"Mik rarid hisup suke.","english":"I remembered your name."},{"fiwo":"Mik ebnesid.","english":"I forgot."},{"fiwo":"Das fenutid mik.","english":"She told me."},{"fiwo":"Sal!","english":"Hello!"},{"fiwo":"Sal. Kup hisup suke?","english":"Hello. What is your name?"},{"fiwo":"Mik Josh. Kup suk lolei?","english":"I am Josh. Are you hungry?"},{"fiwo":"Jas. Mik nes nomid nudu.","english":"Yes. I have not eaten today."},{"fiwo":"Ov. Cef. Kop suk nomi bryklop mike.","english":"Oh no. Please. Eat my bread."},{"fiwo":"Mik grawi suk.","english":"Thank you."},{"fiwo":"Nel. Tex!","english":"You're welcome. Goodbye!"}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Ha.","Mik","kani","nu."],"blank":0,"answer":"Ha","pre":"","post":".","english":"Oh. I know now.","fiwo":"Ha. Mik kani nu."},{"tokens":["Ov.","Mana","suke","iwei."],"blank":0,"answer":"Ov","pre":"","post":".","english":"Oh no. Your mother is tired.","fiwo":"Ov. Mana suke iwei."},{"tokens":["Jo!","Kop","suk","seji."],"blank":0,"answer":"Jo","pre":"","post":"!","english":"Hey! Stop.","fiwo":"Jo! Kop suk seji."},{"tokens":["Sop","das","usid."],"blank":0,"answer":"Sop","pre":"","post":"","english":"She slept — I saw her.","fiwo":"Sop das usid."},{"tokens":["Nop","daq","usiq."],"blank":0,"answer":"Nop","pre":"","post":"","english":"He is sleeping — I infer it (his light is off).","fiwo":"Nop daq usiq."},{"tokens":["Rop","mana","mike","iwei."],"blank":0,"answer":"Rop","pre":"","post":"","english":"My mother is tired — I was told.","fiwo":"Rop mana mike iwei."},{"tokens":["Ov.","Cef.","Kop","suk","nomi","bryklop","mike."],"blank":0,"answer":"Ov","pre":"","post":".","english":"Oh no. Please. Eat my bread.","fiwo":"Ov. Cef. Kop suk nomi bryklop mike."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Which particle?","text":"Someone thanks you.","type":"choice","key":"`nel`","answers":["nel"],"points":10,"options":["nel","ov","ha","jo"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Which particle?","text":"You hear bad news.","type":"choice","key":"`ov`","answers":["ov"],"points":10,"options":["nel","ov","ha","jo"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Which particle?","text":"You suddenly understand something.","type":"choice","key":"`ha`","answers":["ha"],"points":10,"options":["nel","ov","ha","jo"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Which particle?","text":"You want someone's attention urgently.","type":"choice","key":"`jo`","answers":["jo"],"points":10,"options":["nel","ov","ha","jo"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Which evidence tag?","text":"You watched him leave.","type":"choice","key":"`Sop`","answers":["Sop"],"points":10,"options":["Sop","Rop","Nop"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Which evidence tag?","text":"Your sister told you he left.","type":"choice","key":"`Rop`","answers":["Rop"],"points":10,"options":["Sop","Rop","Nop"]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Which evidence tag?","text":"His bag is gone, so you conclude he left.","type":"choice","key":"`Nop`","answers":["Nop"],"points":10,"options":["Sop","Rop","Nop"]},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Oh no. I forgot.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Ov. Mik ebnesid.`","answers":["Ov. Mik ebnesid."],"points":10,"bank":["ak","ebnesid.","ha","Mik","Ov."]},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She slept — I saw it.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sop das usid.`","answers":["Sop das usid."],"points":10,"bank":["das","ebnesi","erfi","Sop","usid."]},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I understand now.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik erfi nu.`","answers":["Mik erfi nu."],"points":10,"bank":["ak","erfi","Mik","nu.","rari"]},{"n":11,"part":"D","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Rop das tsakei.`","type":"type-en","key":"She is angry — I was told.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"D","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Ha. Mik rarid.`","type":"type-en","key":"Oh. I remembered.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"R3","kind":"review","name":"Review 3","title":"Lessons 12–16","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Turn into a question.","text":"`Suk nomid mitap.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk nomid mitap?`","answers":["Kup suk nomid mitap?"],"points":10,"bank":["Kup","mitap?","nomid","suk"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Turn into a question.","text":"`Das erfi.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup das erfi?`","answers":["Kup das erfi?"],"points":10,"bank":["das","erfi?","Kup"]},{"n":3,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Who opened the door?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup wun xosid hoxop?`","answers":["Kup wun xosid hoxop?"],"points":10,"bank":["hoxop?","Kup","wun","xosid"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"What is your name?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup hisup suke?`","answers":["Kup hisup suke?"],"points":10,"bank":["hisup","Kup","suke?"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Please. Help my father. (formal)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Cef. Kop suv jofi peba mike.`","answers":["Cef. Kop suv jofi peba mike."],"points":10,"bank":["Cef.","jofi","Kop","mike.","peba","suv"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am hungry.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik lolei.`","answers":["Mik lolei."],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Oh, *great*, you forgot. (sarcastic)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Hop suk ebnesid.`","answers":["Hop suk ebnesid."],"points":10,"bank":["ebnesid.","Hop","suk"]},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"He is sleeping — I infer it.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nop daq usiq.`","answers":["Nop daq usiq."],"points":10,"bank":["daq","Nop","usiq."]},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup suk karxid nibo wug?`","type":"type-en","key":"How many books did you find?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Cef. Kop suv neki.`","type":"type-en","key":"Please be seated. (formal)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Hap mik kypei!`","type":"type-en","key":"I am so happy!","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Rop mana mike iwei.`","type":"type-en","key":"My mother is tired — I was told.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":13,"part":"D","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Wat suk nomid?`","type":"explain","key":"Question words never move to the front. `Kup suk nomid wat?`","answers":["Kup suk nomid wat?"],"points":6},{"n":14,"part":"D","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Kop suk jofi mik cef.`","type":"explain","key":"`cef` may not trail inside a clause. `Cef. Kop suk jofi mik.`","answers":["Cef. Kop suk jofi mik."],"points":6},{"n":15,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: greet someone, ask their name, ask if they are hungry, react to their answer with a particle, and say goodbye. Aloud, in one go.","text":"Without looking: greet someone, ask their name, ask if they are hungry, react to their answer with a particle, and say goodbye. Aloud, in one go.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L17","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 17","title":"Say how you feel","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say you are bored, interested, or surprised — and say what made you that way.</p>\n<h3>1. English hides two people in one word</h3>\n<p>Think about <em>bored</em> and <em>boring</em>. Same root, two completely different people:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I am bored</em> — I am the one <strong>feeling</strong> it</li>\n<li><em>the book is boring</em> — the book is the <em>source</em> of it</li>\n</ul>\n<p>English marks this with <code>-ed</code> and <code>-ing</code>, inconsistently and with many exceptions. Fiwo marks it with the functional vowels you already know, and never varies.</p>\n<h3>2. The rule</h3>\n<p>For any word naming a feeling:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Example from <code>sysu</code> (boredom)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>-e</code> modifier</td>\n<td>the one who <strong>feels</strong> it</td>\n<td><code>sysue</code> — bored</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-i</code> verb</td>\n<td>the <em>source</em> it comes from</td>\n<td><code>sysui</code> — to bore</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik sysuei.</strong> — I am bored.\n<strong>Nibop sysui.</strong> — The book is boring.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The first uses the stative from Lesson 5 (<code>sysue</code> + <code>-i</code>) to make the feeler into a sentence. The second uses the source verb directly, with the boring thing as the subject.</p>\n<h3>3. How to pick the subject of the <code>-i</code> verb</h3>\n<p>Ask yourself: <strong>where does this feeling come from?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grief comes from the person grieving → <code>motuwui</code> = <em>to grieve</em></li>\n<li>Boredom comes from the boring book → <code>sysui</code> = <em>to bore</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The source is whoever the feeling originates in. Once you ask that question, the verb&#39;s subject is never ambiguous.</p>\n<h3>4. Worked examples</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik veponkuei.</strong> — I am surprised.\n<strong>Nofap veponkui mik.</strong> — The person surprises me.</p>\n<p><strong>Mik sujuei.</strong> — I am interested.\n<strong>Nibop sujui mik.</strong> — The book interests me.</p>\n<p><strong>Mik sysuei. Nibop sysui.</strong> — I am bored. The book is boring.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That last pair is the whole lesson in two sentences. Say them back to back until the difference is automatic.</p>\n<h3>5. More feelings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>tazi</code></td>\n<td>to love</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>wogi</code></td>\n<td>to hope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kyti</code></td>\n<td>to like</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kili</code></td>\n<td>to feel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>reslihi</code></td>\n<td>to hate</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>veponku</code></td>\n<td>surprise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sysu</code></td>\n<td>boredom</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>suju</code></td>\n<td>interest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik tazi suk.</strong> — I love you.\n<strong>Das kyti bryklop.</strong> — She likes the bread.\n<strong>Mik reslihi zobap.</strong> — I hate the vegetable.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"tazi","english":"to love"},{"fiwo":"wogi","english":"to hope"},{"fiwo":"kyti","english":"to like"},{"fiwo":"kili","english":"to feel"},{"fiwo":"reslihi","english":"to hate"},{"fiwo":"veponku","english":"surprise"},{"fiwo":"sysu","english":"boredom"},{"fiwo":"suju","english":"interest"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik sysuei.","english":"I am bored."},{"fiwo":"Nibop sysui.","english":"The book is boring."},{"fiwo":"Mik veponkuei.","english":"I am surprised."},{"fiwo":"Nofap veponkui mik.","english":"The person surprises me."},{"fiwo":"Mik sujuei.","english":"I am interested."},{"fiwo":"Nibop sujui mik.","english":"The book interests me."},{"fiwo":"Mik sysuei. Nibop sysui.","english":"I am bored. The book is boring."},{"fiwo":"Mik tazi suk.","english":"I love you."},{"fiwo":"Das kyti bryklop.","english":"She likes the bread."},{"fiwo":"Mik reslihi zobap.","english":"I hate the vegetable."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","tazi","suk."],"blank":1,"answer":"tazi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I love you.","fiwo":"Mik tazi suk."},{"tokens":["Das","kyti","bryklop."],"blank":1,"answer":"kyti","pre":"","post":"","english":"She likes the bread.","fiwo":"Das kyti bryklop."},{"tokens":["Mik","reslihi","zobap."],"blank":1,"answer":"reslihi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I hate the vegetable.","fiwo":"Mik reslihi zobap."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","note":"Translate.","text":"I am interested.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik sujuei.`","answers":["Mik sujuei."],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","note":"Translate.","text":"The book interests me.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nibop sujui mik.`","answers":["Nibop sujui mik."],"points":10,"bank":["mik.","Nibop","sujui","sysu","veponku"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","note":"Translate.","text":"I am surprised.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik veponkuei.`","answers":["Mik veponkuei."],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","note":"Translate.","text":"She surprises me.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das veponkui mik.`","answers":["Das veponkui mik."],"points":10,"bank":["Das","mik.","reslihi","veponkui","wogi"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik sysuei.`","type":"type-en","key":"I am bored.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nibop sysui.`","type":"type-en","key":"The book is boring.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik tazi mana mike.`","type":"type-en","key":"I love my mother.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Think it through.","note":"`motuwu` means grief. Which is *to grieve* — `motuwue` or `motuwui`? Why?","text":"`motuwu` means grief. Which is *to grieve* — `motuwue` or `motuwui`? Why?","type":"explain","key":"`motuwui`. Grief comes *from* the person grieving, so the griever is the source and takes the `-i` verb.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"D","prompt":"Legal, but wrong.","text":"`Mik sysui.` — the learner meant *I am bored*. The parser accepts it. What did they actually say?","type":"explain","key":"They said *I bore [people]* — that you are the boring one. To say you feel bored: `Mik sysuei.` One letter, opposite meaning.","answers":["Mik sysuei."],"points":6}]},{"id":"L18","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 18","title":"Talk about yourself and each other","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say you did something to yourself, that people did something to each other, and talk about ideas being true or false.</p>\n<h3>1. Reflexive and reciprocal are modifiers, not pronouns</h3>\n<p>Fiwo has no words for <em>myself</em> or <em>each other</em> as pronouns. It has two modifiers:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>wible</code></td>\n<td>self — the action loops back on the doer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>derwe</code></td>\n<td>mutual — the doers act on one another</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Put them straight after the verb and leave the object slot empty.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jipri wible.</strong> — I wash myself.\n<strong>Muk jofi derwe.</strong> — We help each other.\n<strong>Muk enconi derwe.</strong> — We meet each other.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. The same words, used for emphasis</h3>\n<p>Put <code>wible</code> after a <em>noun</em> instead of a verb and it emphasises that noun — because, as always, modifiers look left at whatever is beside them.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik wible xyfis ram.</strong> — I myself will do it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One word, two jobs, decided entirely by what sits to its left. This is the Look Left principle from Lesson 5 doing all the work.</p>\n<h3>3. True, false, and what things mean</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>jolkodu</code></td>\n<td>to mean</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>asku</code></td>\n<td>reason</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fwape</code></td>\n<td>true</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>ladu</code></td>\n<td>idea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ocfe</code></td>\n<td>false</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Ram fwapei.</strong> — It is true.\n<strong>Ram ocfei.</strong> — It is false.\n<strong>Ladu mike jaxkei.</strong> — My idea is new.\n<strong>Askup fwapei.</strong> — The reason is true.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"wible","english":"self — the action loops back on the doer"},{"fiwo":"derwe","english":"mutual — the doers act on one another"},{"fiwo":"jolkodu","english":"to mean"},{"fiwo":"asku","english":"reason"},{"fiwo":"fwape","english":"true"},{"fiwo":"ladu","english":"idea"},{"fiwo":"ocfe","english":"false"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik jipri wible.","english":"I wash myself."},{"fiwo":"Muk jofi derwe.","english":"We help each other."},{"fiwo":"Muk enconi derwe.","english":"We meet each other."},{"fiwo":"Mik wible xyfis ram.","english":"I myself will do it."},{"fiwo":"Ram fwapei.","english":"It is true."},{"fiwo":"Ram ocfei.","english":"It is false."},{"fiwo":"Ladu mike jaxkei.","english":"My idea is new."},{"fiwo":"Askup fwapei.","english":"The reason is true."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","jipri","wible."],"blank":2,"answer":"wible","pre":"","post":".","english":"I wash myself.","fiwo":"Mik jipri wible."},{"tokens":["Muk","jofi","derwe."],"blank":2,"answer":"derwe","pre":"","post":".","english":"We help each other.","fiwo":"Muk jofi derwe."},{"tokens":["Muk","enconi","derwe."],"blank":2,"answer":"derwe","pre":"","post":".","english":"We meet each other.","fiwo":"Muk enconi derwe."},{"tokens":["Mik","wible","xyfis","ram."],"blank":1,"answer":"wible","pre":"","post":"","english":"I myself will do it.","fiwo":"Mik wible xyfis ram."},{"tokens":["Ladu","mike","jaxkei."],"blank":0,"answer":"Ladu","pre":"","post":"","english":"My idea is new.","fiwo":"Ladu mike jaxkei."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I wash myself.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik jipri wible.`","answers":["Mik jipri wible."],"points":10,"bank":["asku","derwe","jipri","Mik","wible."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We help each other.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Muk jofi derwe.`","answers":["Muk jofi derwe."],"points":10,"bank":["derwe.","jofi","ladu","Muk","wible"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"It is false.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Ram ocfei.`","answers":["Ram ocfei."],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I myself will do it.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik wible xyfis ram.`","answers":["Mik wible xyfis ram."],"points":10,"bank":["asku","derwe","Mik","ram.","wible","xyfis"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das upyci wible.`","type":"type-en","key":"She looks at herself.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk enconi derwe.`","type":"type-en","key":"We meet each other.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Ladu suke fwapei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Your idea is true.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Which one — `wible` or `derwe`?","text":"Two friends looking at one another","type":"choice","key":"`derwe` — mutual","answers":["derwe"],"points":10,"options":["wible","derwe"]},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Which one — `wible` or `derwe`?","text":"A child dressing herself","type":"choice","key":"`wible` — reflexive","answers":["wible"],"points":10,"options":["wible","derwe"]}]},{"id":"L19","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 19","title":"Want, need, must, might","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> stack two verbs together, and say how necessary or how likely something is.</p>\n<h3>1. Two verbs in a row need a linker</h3>\n<p><em>I want to eat</em> has two verbs. Fiwo&#39;s parser expects a noun after a verb, so putting <code>fabi</code> and <code>nomi</code> side by side would break it. The fix is the linker <code>-t</code> on the second verb.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik fabid nomit batap.</strong> — I wanted to eat the food.\n<em>(want-past + eat-LINKER + the food)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The <code>-t</code> locks the two verbs into one action block, and the object then slots in normally.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Das cimid uropit.</strong> — She started to sing.\n<strong>Mik vepi usit.</strong> — I need to sleep.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. Necessity and possibility</h3>\n<p>Modality is handled by six modifiers. Put one straight after the verb — before the object — and it scales the verb&#39;s certainty or obligation.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Kind</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>mace</code></td>\n<td>might, maybe</td>\n<td>possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>neme</code></td>\n<td>probably, likely</td>\n<td>possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dide</code></td>\n<td>certainly, definitely</td>\n<td>possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dozme</code></td>\n<td>allowed, may</td>\n<td>obligation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>arife</code></td>\n<td>should</td>\n<td>obligation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fuqe</code></td>\n<td>must</td>\n<td>obligation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Suk usi fuqe.</strong> — You must sleep.\n<strong>Das karxis mace lusop.</strong> — She might find the key.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Where you put <code>nes</code> changes the law</h3>\n<p>This is one of Fiwo&#39;s sharpest tools. Negation is a strict operator — it flips whatever comes immediately after it. Move it, and you change which part is negated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Suk nes neki fuqe.</strong> — You must not sit.\n<em>(the obligation is real; the action is negated)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suk neki nes fuqe.</strong> — You do not have to sit.\n<em>(the action is fine; the obligation is negated)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>English needs careful phrasing and still gets this wrong in contracts and signs. Fiwo settles it with word order. Read both aloud and make sure you feel the difference — this one matters.</p>\n<h3>4. Having, needing, giving, taking</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>jedi</code></td>\n<td>to have</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>steli</code></td>\n<td>to take</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>vepi</code></td>\n<td>to need</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>wafri</code></td>\n<td>to lose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fabi</code></td>\n<td>to want</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>hi</code></td>\n<td>to be</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>stomi</code></td>\n<td>to give</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik stomi nibop.</strong> — I give the book.\n<strong>Mik wafrid lusop.</strong> — I lost the key.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>hi</code> is the explicit verb <em>to be</em>. You have not needed it — the Zero Copula from Lesson 2 covers plain identity — and it stays optional. Use it only for emphasis.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"mace","english":"might, maybe"},{"fiwo":"neme","english":"probably, likely"},{"fiwo":"dide","english":"certainly, definitely"},{"fiwo":"dozme","english":"allowed, may"},{"fiwo":"arife","english":"should"},{"fiwo":"fuqe","english":"must"},{"fiwo":"jedi","english":"to have"},{"fiwo":"steli","english":"to take"},{"fiwo":"vepi","english":"to need"},{"fiwo":"wafri","english":"to lose"},{"fiwo":"fabi","english":"to want"},{"fiwo":"hi","english":"to be"},{"fiwo":"stomi","english":"to give"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik fabid nomit batap.","english":"I wanted to eat the food."},{"fiwo":"Das cimid uropit.","english":"She started to sing."},{"fiwo":"Mik vepi usit.","english":"I need to sleep."},{"fiwo":"Suk usi fuqe.","english":"You must sleep."},{"fiwo":"Das karxis mace lusop.","english":"She might find the key."},{"fiwo":"Suk nes neki fuqe.","english":"You must not sit."},{"fiwo":"Suk neki nes fuqe.","english":"You do not have to sit."},{"fiwo":"Mik stomi nibop.","english":"I give the book."},{"fiwo":"Mik wafrid lusop.","english":"I lost the key."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","vepi","usit."],"blank":1,"answer":"vepi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I need to sleep.","fiwo":"Mik vepi usit."},{"tokens":["Suk","usi","fuqe."],"blank":2,"answer":"fuqe","pre":"","post":".","english":"You must sleep.","fiwo":"Suk usi fuqe."},{"tokens":["Das","karxis","mace","lusop."],"blank":2,"answer":"mace","pre":"","post":"","english":"She might find the key.","fiwo":"Das karxis mace lusop."},{"tokens":["Suk","nes","neki","fuqe."],"blank":3,"answer":"fuqe","pre":"","post":".","english":"You must not sit.","fiwo":"Suk nes neki fuqe."},{"tokens":["Suk","neki","nes","fuqe."],"blank":3,"answer":"fuqe","pre":"","post":".","english":"You do not have to sit.","fiwo":"Suk neki nes fuqe."},{"tokens":["Mik","stomi","nibop."],"blank":1,"answer":"stomi","pre":"","post":"","english":"I give the book.","fiwo":"Mik stomi nibop."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I want to eat.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik fabi nomit.`","answers":["Mik fabi nomit."],"points":10,"bank":["fabi","fuqe","jedi","Mik","nomit."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She started to sing.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das cimid uropit.`","answers":["Das cimid uropit."],"points":10,"bank":["cimid","Das","dide","uropit.","wafri"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You must sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk usi fuqe.`","answers":["Suk usi fuqe."],"points":10,"bank":["dozme","fuqe.","jedi","Suk","usi"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"He might find the water.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Daq karxis mace dorsop.`","answers":["Daq karxis mace dorsop."],"points":10,"bank":["Daq","dorsop.","fuqe","karxis","mace","vepi"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"The legal distinction.","note":"Translate both, and explain the difference.","text":"You must not eat the food.","type":"explain","key":"`Suk nes nomi fuqe batap.` — the obligation stands, the eating is forbidden.","answers":["Suk nes nomi fuqe batap."],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"The legal distinction.","note":"Translate both, and explain the difference.","text":"You do not have to eat the food.","type":"explain","key":"`Suk nomi nes fuqe batap.` — eating is fine, there is simply no requirement.","answers":["Suk nomi nes fuqe batap."],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik vepi usit.`","type":"type-en","key":"I need to sleep.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das jedi nibop.`","type":"type-en","key":"She has the book.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk usi arife.`","type":"type-en","key":"We should sleep.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L20","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 20","title":"Say how much, and compare","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say <em>very</em>, <em>too</em>, <em>bigger</em>, <em>biggest</em> — and stack one description on top of another.</p>\n<h3>1. A flag for modifying a modifier</h3>\n<p>Modifiers look left. So if you write two of them in a row, each one independently describes the noun:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop leupe cape.</strong> — The house that is red and dark. <em>(two separate facts)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But sometimes you want the second modifier to describe the <strong>first</strong> one — dark <em>red</em>, not dark <em>and</em> red. Add <code>-m</code> to the modifier being described:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop leupem cape.</strong> — The dark-red house.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One letter decides whether you have described the house twice or described its colour once.</p>\n<h3>2. Bigger and biggest</h3>\n<p>There is no <code>-er</code> or <code>-est</code> in Fiwo. Flag the trait with <code>-m</code>, then scale it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>tande</code></td>\n<td>big</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tandem jete</code></td>\n<td>bigger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tandem merne</code></td>\n<td>biggest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop tandem jete.</strong> — The house is bigger.\n<strong>Sydop tandem merne.</strong> — The house is the biggest.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>jete</code> = more · <code>merne</code> = most · <code>vome</code> = less</p>\n<h3>3. How intensely?</h3>\n<p>The same <code>-m</code> flag takes degree words, for subjective intensity:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nibop dwimem fe.</strong> — The book is very long.\n<strong>Batap gawem fe.</strong> — Very good food.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>fe</code> = very · <code>slafe</code> = too, excessively · <code>juwe</code> = only</p>\n<p>Fiwo also has a second, <em>numeric</em> intensity scale — attach a number from 1 to 5 to the\n<code>-m</code> flag for objective precision, which is how pain scales and measurements are stated.\nYou meet it in Lesson 27, once you have numbers. Degree words for talking, numbers for\nmeasuring: same slot, two registers.</p>\n<h3>4. Comparing to something — coming in Lesson 31</h3>\n<p>To say <em>bigger <strong>than the house</strong></em> you need the preposition <code>taly</code> (compared to), and prepositions arrive in Lesson 31. Everything here stays true when it does: you will simply add <code>taly</code> and a baseline noun on the end.</p>\n<h3>5. More descriptions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>hiqe</code></td>\n<td>right, correct</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>witu</code></td>\n<td>colour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>okse</code></td>\n<td>wrong</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>baude</code></td>\n<td>beautiful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>curme</code></td>\n<td>easy</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>raure</code></td>\n<td>ugly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>muile</code></td>\n<td>difficult</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>jotse</code></td>\n<td>clean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>labne</code></td>\n<td>same</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>flove</code></td>\n<td>dirty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>felpe</code></td>\n<td>different</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>hobe</code></td>\n<td>young</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tsejap dase baudei.</strong> — Her eyes are beautiful.\n<strong>Buskop flovei.</strong> — The clothes are dirty.\n<strong>Zipap mike jotsei.</strong> — My face is clean.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"tande","english":"big"},{"fiwo":"tandem jete","english":"bigger"},{"fiwo":"tandem merne","english":"biggest"},{"fiwo":"hiqe","english":"right, correct"},{"fiwo":"witu","english":"colour"},{"fiwo":"okse","english":"wrong"},{"fiwo":"baude","english":"beautiful"},{"fiwo":"curme","english":"easy"},{"fiwo":"raure","english":"ugly"},{"fiwo":"muile","english":"difficult"},{"fiwo":"jotse","english":"clean"},{"fiwo":"labne","english":"same"},{"fiwo":"flove","english":"dirty"},{"fiwo":"felpe","english":"different"},{"fiwo":"hobe","english":"young"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Sydop leupe cape.","english":"The house that is red and dark. *(two separate facts)*"},{"fiwo":"Sydop leupem cape.","english":"The dark-red house."},{"fiwo":"Sydop tandem jete.","english":"The house is bigger."},{"fiwo":"Sydop tandem merne.","english":"The house is the biggest."},{"fiwo":"Nibop dwimem fe.","english":"The book is very long."},{"fiwo":"Batap gawem fe.","english":"Very good food."},{"fiwo":"Tsejap dase baudei.","english":"Her eyes are beautiful."},{"fiwo":"Buskop flovei.","english":"The clothes are dirty."},{"fiwo":"Zipap mike jotsei.","english":"My face is clean."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"With or without `-m`?","note":"Translate.","text":"The dark-red door","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`hoxop leupem cape`","answers":["hoxop leupem cape"],"points":10,"bank":["cape","hoxop","leupem","muile","tandem jete"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"With or without `-m`?","note":"Translate.","text":"The door that is red and dark","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`hoxop leupe cape`","answers":["hoxop leupe cape"],"points":10,"bank":["cape","felpe","hoxop","leupe","tande"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"With or without `-m`?","note":"Translate.","text":"The book is very long","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nibop dwimem fe.`","answers":["Nibop dwimem fe."],"points":10,"bank":["dwimem","fe.","muile","Nibop","witu"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The house is bigger.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sydop tandem jete.`","answers":["Sydop tandem jete."],"points":10,"bank":["jete.","muile","okse","Sydop","tandem"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The food is the best.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Batap gawem merne.`","answers":["Batap gawem merne."],"points":10,"bank":["Batap","felpe","flove","gawem","merne."]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"My clothes are too dirty.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Buskop flovem slafe.`","answers":["Buskop flovem slafe."],"points":10,"bank":["baude","Buskop","flovem","okse","slafe."]},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Sydop nifnem merne.`","type":"type-en","key":"The house is the smallest.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nibop muilem fe.`","type":"type-en","key":"The book is very difficult.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Zipap dase baudei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Her face is beautiful.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"R4","kind":"review","name":"Review 4","title":"Lessons 17–20","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","text":"I am bored.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik sysuei.`","answers":["Mik sysuei."],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","text":"The book is boring.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nibop sysui.`","answers":["Nibop sysui."],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Feeler or source?","text":"The person surprises me.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofap veponkui mik.`","answers":["Nofap veponkui mik."],"points":10,"bank":["mik.","Nofap","veponkui"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I want to eat the fish.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik fabi nomit fubap.`","answers":["Mik fabi nomit fubap."],"points":10,"bank":["fabi","fubap.","Mik","nomit"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You must not sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk nes usi fuqe.`","answers":["Suk nes usi fuqe."],"points":10,"bank":["fuqe.","nes","Suk","usi"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You do not have to sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk usi nes fuqe.`","answers":["Suk usi nes fuqe."],"points":10,"bank":["fuqe.","nes","Suk","usi"]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We help each other.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Muk jofi derwe.`","answers":["Muk jofi derwe."],"points":10,"bank":["derwe.","jofi","Muk"]},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The house is the biggest.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sydop tandem merne.`","answers":["Sydop tandem merne."],"points":10,"bank":["merne.","Sydop","tandem"]},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"My clothes are very dirty.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Buskop flovem fe.`","answers":["Buskop flovem fe."],"points":10,"bank":["Buskop","fe.","flovem"]},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik sujuei.`","type":"type-en","key":"I am interested.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das karxis mace lusop.`","type":"type-en","key":"She might find the key.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Hoxop leupem cape.`","type":"type-en","key":"The dark-red door.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":13,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik jipri wible.`","type":"type-en","key":"I wash myself.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":14,"part":"D","prompt":"Explain the difference.","text":"`Sydop leupe cape.` versus `Sydop leupem cape.`","type":"explain","key":"Without `-m`, both modifiers describe the house independently — it is red *and* dark. With `-m`, `cape` describes `leupe`, so the house is dark-red.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":15,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: say how you feel and what caused it, say one thing you must do and one you might do, and describe two objects using a comparative.","text":"Without looking: say how you feel and what caused it, say one thing you must do and one you might do, and describe two objects using a comparative.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L21","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 21","title":"Join two thoughts","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say <em>and</em>, <em>but</em>, <em>because</em>, <em>so</em> — and finally ask <em>why</em> about something specific.</p>\n<h3>1. A wall ends one clause and starts another</h3>\n<p>Fiwo joins whole sentences with <strong>clausal walls</strong>. When the parser hits a wall it finishes the current clause completely, wipes the slots, and expects a brand-new subject.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomid batap bef mik rolid dorsop.</strong> — I ate the food and I drank the water.\n<strong>Mik karxid lusop bul mik wafrid ram.</strong> — I found the key but I lost it.\n<strong>Mik usid kad mik iwei.</strong> — I slept because I am tired.\n<strong>Mik iwei pen mik usis.</strong> — I am tired, so I will sleep.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note what this forces: after <code>kad</code> you must supply a <strong>complete clause</strong>, subject and all. You cannot say <em>because tired</em>. Every wall demands a full sentence on the other side of it.</p>\n<h3>2. The walls</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>bef</code></td>\n<td>and</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bul</code></td>\n<td>but</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rot</code></td>\n<td>or</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kad</code></td>\n<td>because</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pen</code></td>\n<td>so, therefore</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>vel</code></td>\n<td>while, whereas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zol</code></td>\n<td>although</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kof</code></td>\n<td>furthermore</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Suk nomi rot suk roli.</strong> — You eat or you drink.\n<strong>Mik nomid vel das usid.</strong> — I ate while she slept.\n<strong>Mik xyfid ram zol mik iwei.</strong> — I did it although I am tired.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Asking why about something specific</h3>\n<p>In Lesson 12 you learned standalone <code>Wal?</code>. Now that you have <code>kad</code>, you can ask why about a particular statement — put <code>wal</code> after <code>kad</code>, exactly where the reason clause would go.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk usid kad wal?</strong> — Why did you sleep?\n<em>(Question + you + slept + because + WHY — the blank is the whole missing reason)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>wal</code> is the only question word that stands in for an entire clause rather than one slot.</p>\n<h3>4. More food</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cekgo</code></td>\n<td>salt</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>ripe</code></td>\n<td>hot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tanko</code></td>\n<td>sugar</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>jabe</code></td>\n<td>cold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>otani</code></td>\n<td>to cook</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>cacki</code></td>\n<td>to taste</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zuzke</code></td>\n<td>sweet</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Batap zuzkei.</strong> — The food is sweet.\n<strong>Dorsop jabei.</strong> — The water is cold.\n<strong>Das otanid mitap.</strong> — She cooked the meat.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"bef","english":"and"},{"fiwo":"bul","english":"but"},{"fiwo":"rot","english":"or"},{"fiwo":"kad","english":"because"},{"fiwo":"pen","english":"so, therefore"},{"fiwo":"vel","english":"while, whereas"},{"fiwo":"zol","english":"although"},{"fiwo":"kof","english":"furthermore"},{"fiwo":"cekgo","english":"salt"},{"fiwo":"ripe","english":"hot"},{"fiwo":"tanko","english":"sugar"},{"fiwo":"jabe","english":"cold"},{"fiwo":"otani","english":"to cook"},{"fiwo":"cacki","english":"to taste"},{"fiwo":"zuzke","english":"sweet"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nomid batap bef mik rolid dorsop.","english":"I ate the food and I drank the water."},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid lusop bul mik wafrid ram.","english":"I found the key but I lost it."},{"fiwo":"Mik usid kad mik iwei.","english":"I slept because I am tired."},{"fiwo":"Mik iwei pen mik usis.","english":"I am tired, so I will sleep."},{"fiwo":"Suk nomi rot suk roli.","english":"You eat or you drink."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomid vel das usid.","english":"I ate while she slept."},{"fiwo":"Mik xyfid ram zol mik iwei.","english":"I did it although I am tired."},{"fiwo":"Kup suk usid kad wal?","english":"Why did you sleep?"},{"fiwo":"Batap zuzkei.","english":"The food is sweet."},{"fiwo":"Dorsop jabei.","english":"The water is cold."},{"fiwo":"Das otanid mitap.","english":"She cooked the meat."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","nomid","batap","bef","mik","rolid","dorsop."],"blank":3,"answer":"bef","pre":"","post":"","english":"I ate the food and I drank the water.","fiwo":"Mik nomid batap bef mik rolid dorsop."},{"tokens":["Mik","karxid","lusop","bul","mik","wafrid","ram."],"blank":3,"answer":"bul","pre":"","post":"","english":"I found the key but I lost it.","fiwo":"Mik karxid lusop bul mik wafrid ram."},{"tokens":["Mik","usid","kad","mik","iwei."],"blank":2,"answer":"kad","pre":"","post":"","english":"I slept because I am tired.","fiwo":"Mik usid kad mik iwei."},{"tokens":["Mik","iwei","pen","mik","usis."],"blank":2,"answer":"pen","pre":"","post":"","english":"I am tired, so I will sleep.","fiwo":"Mik iwei pen mik usis."},{"tokens":["Suk","nomi","rot","suk","roli."],"blank":2,"answer":"rot","pre":"","post":"","english":"You eat or you drink.","fiwo":"Suk nomi rot suk roli."},{"tokens":["Mik","nomid","vel","das","usid."],"blank":2,"answer":"vel","pre":"","post":"","english":"I ate while she slept.","fiwo":"Mik nomid vel das usid."},{"tokens":["Mik","xyfid","ram","zol","mik","iwei."],"blank":3,"answer":"zol","pre":"","post":"","english":"I did it although I am tired.","fiwo":"Mik xyfid ram zol mik iwei."},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","usid","kad","wal?"],"blank":3,"answer":"kad","pre":"","post":"","english":"Why did you sleep?","fiwo":"Kup suk usid kad wal?"}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I ate the bread and I drank the milk.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid bryklop bef mik rolid lamsop.`","answers":["Mik nomid bryklop bef mik rolid lamsop."],"points":10,"bank":["bef","bryklop","kad","lamsop.","mik","Mik","nomid","rolid","zol"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am hungry but I will not eat.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik lolei bul mik nes nomis.`","answers":["Mik lolei bul mik nes nomis."],"points":10,"bank":["bul","cekgo","lolei","mik","Mik","nes","nomis.","pen"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She slept because she was tired.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das usid kad das iweid.`","answers":["Das usid kad das iweid."],"points":10,"bank":["das","Das","iweid.","kad","ripe","rot","usid"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Why did you cook the fish?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk otanid fubap kad wal?`","answers":["Kup suk otanid fubap kad wal?"],"points":10,"bank":["fubap","jabe","kad","Kup","otanid","suk","tanko","wal?"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik otanid mitap vel das usid.`","type":"type-en","key":"I cooked the meat while she slept.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Dorsop jabei pen mik nes rolis ram.`","type":"type-en","key":"The water is cold, so I will not drink it.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nomid batap zol ram nes zuzkei.`","type":"type-en","key":"I ate the food although it is not sweet.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Mik nes nomid kad wal?` — the learner asked why they did not eat.","type":"explain","key":"Any sentence containing a question variable must open with `Kup`. Correct: `Kup mik nes nomid kad wal?`","answers":["Kup mik nes nomid kad wal?"],"points":6}]},{"id":"L22","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 22","title":"Lists and choices","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> join words rather than whole sentences, and make a list.</p>\n<h3>1. Walls join sentences; glue joins words</h3>\n<p><code>bef</code> joins two complete clauses. To join two <em>words</em>, you need the inline glue <code>lan</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomid bryklop lan mitap.</strong> — I ate the bread and the meat.\n<em>(one clause, two objects)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Compare:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomid bryklop bef mik nomid mitap.</strong> — I ate the bread and I ate the meat. <em>(two clauses)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Using <code>bef</code> between two nouns would crash the parser — it resets the slots and then finds no subject waiting. Words take <code>lan</code>; sentences take <code>bef</code>.</p>\n<h3>2. Inline choice</h3>\n<p><code>ron</code> is the word-level <em>or</em>, matching <code>rot</code> at the clause level.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Suk nomi bryklop ron vyflap.</strong> — You eat the bread or the rice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Longer lists</h3>\n<p>For three or more items, use <code>sek</code> — the spoken comma.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomid bryklop sek mitap sek zobap.</strong> — I ate bread, meat, and vegetables.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>4. Work and school</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>naci</code></td>\n<td>to work</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>ibalira</code></td>\n<td>teacher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jobu</code></td>\n<td>job</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>keci</code></td>\n<td>to learn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ibazo</code></td>\n<td>school</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kritri</code></td>\n<td>to teach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gidu</code></td>\n<td>money</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik naci bef mik keci.</strong> — I work and I learn.\n<strong>Ibalira kritrid mik.</strong> — The teacher taught me.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"naci","english":"to work"},{"fiwo":"ibalira","english":"teacher"},{"fiwo":"jobu","english":"job"},{"fiwo":"keci","english":"to learn"},{"fiwo":"ibazo","english":"school"},{"fiwo":"kritri","english":"to teach"},{"fiwo":"gidu","english":"money"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nomid bryklop lan mitap.","english":"I ate the bread and the meat."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomid bryklop bef mik nomid mitap.","english":"I ate the bread and I ate the meat. *(two clauses)*"},{"fiwo":"Suk nomi bryklop ron vyflap.","english":"You eat the bread or the rice."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomid bryklop sek mitap sek zobap.","english":"I ate bread, meat, and vegetables."},{"fiwo":"Mik naci bef mik keci.","english":"I work and I learn."},{"fiwo":"Ibalira kritrid mik.","english":"The teacher taught me."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","naci","bef","mik","keci."],"blank":1,"answer":"naci","pre":"","post":"","english":"I work and I learn.","fiwo":"Mik naci bef mik keci."},{"tokens":["Ibalira","kritrid","mik."],"blank":0,"answer":"Ibalira","pre":"","post":"","english":"The teacher taught me.","fiwo":"Ibalira kritrid mik."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"`lan` or `bef`?","text":"I ate the bread and the fish.","type":"choice","key":"`lan` — `Mik nomid bryklop lan fubap.`","answers":["lan"],"points":10,"options":["lan","bef"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"`lan` or `bef`?","text":"I ate the bread and I slept.","type":"choice","key":"`bef` — `Mik nomid bryklop bef mik usid.`","answers":["bef"],"points":10,"options":["lan","bef"]},{"n":3,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"You drink the water or the milk.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Suk roli dorsop ron lamsop.`","answers":["Suk roli dorsop ron lamsop."],"points":10,"bank":["dorsop","kritri","lamsop.","naci","roli","ron","Suk"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I ate bread, meat, and fruit.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid bryklop sek mitap sek zutap.`","answers":["Mik nomid bryklop sek mitap sek zutap."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop","ibalira","jobu","Mik","mitap","nomid","sek","sek","zutap."]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The teacher works and teaches.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Ibalira naci bef kritri.`","answers":["Ibalira naci bef kritri."],"points":10,"bank":["bef","gidu","Ibalira","ibazo","kritri.","naci"]},{"n":6,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik jedi jobup lan gidup.`","type":"type-en","key":"I have a job and money.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das kecid vel mik nacid.`","type":"type-en","key":"She learned while I worked.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L23","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 23","title":"Describe someone with a whole clause","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say <em>the person who found the key</em> — a full sentence tucked inside another one.</p>\n<h3>1. Brackets, not word order</h3>\n<p>English uses <em>who</em>, <em>which</em>, <em>that</em> and relies on position. Fiwo puts an actual bracket around the inner clause: <code>tep</code> opens it, <code>tel</code> closes it.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nofap tep karxid lusop tel usid.</strong> — The person who found the key slept.\n<em>(person-the [ found the key ] slept)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Everything between <code>tep</code> and <code>tel</code> is a separate mini-sentence. When the parser hits <code>tel</code> it returns to exactly where it left off. Nothing is ambiguous, ever — you can see the boundaries.</p>\n<h3>2. When the bracket ends the sentence, drop <code>tel</code></h3>\n<p>If the inner clause runs to the end, the full stop closes it for you.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik kani nofap tep otani batap.</strong> — I know the person who cooks the food.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You only need <code>tel</code> when the main sentence continues afterwards. Compare the two above: the first has <code>tel</code> because <code>usid</code> still has to come; the second does not, because nothing follows.</p>\n<h3>3. Reading them aloud</h3>\n<p>A bracket is a <em>held breath</em>. Say the main subject, open <code>tep</code>, deliver the inner clause, land on <code>tel</code>, then finish. Practise that shape — it is what makes long Fiwo sentences comprehensible by ear rather than only on paper.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nibop tep mik lokid tel dwimei.</strong> — The book that I read is long.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>4. More school words</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>loki</code></td>\n<td>to read</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>hadi</code></td>\n<td>to answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jami</code></td>\n<td>to write</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>go</code></td>\n<td>word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tehidi</code></td>\n<td>to study</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>fiwo</code></td>\n<td>language</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik lokid nibop tep suk jamid.</strong> — I read the book that you wrote.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"loki","english":"to read"},{"fiwo":"hadi","english":"to answer"},{"fiwo":"jami","english":"to write"},{"fiwo":"go","english":"word"},{"fiwo":"tehidi","english":"to study"},{"fiwo":"fiwo","english":"language"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Nofap tep karxid lusop tel usid.","english":"The person who found the key slept."},{"fiwo":"Mik kani nofap tep otani batap.","english":"I know the person who cooks the food."},{"fiwo":"Nibop tep mik lokid tel dwimei.","english":"The book that I read is long."},{"fiwo":"Mik lokid nibop tep suk jamid.","english":"I read the book that you wrote."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The person who cooked the food slept.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofap tep otanid batap tel usid.`","answers":["Nofap tep otanid batap tel usid."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","go","Nofap","otanid","tehidi","tel","tep","usid."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I know the teacher who taught you.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik kani ibalirap tep kritrid suk.`","answers":["Mik kani ibalirap tep kritrid suk."],"points":10,"bank":["fiwo","ibalirap","jami","kani","kritrid","Mik","suk.","tep"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The book that I wrote is long.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nibop tep mik jamid tel dwimei.`","answers":["Nibop tep mik jamid tel dwimei."],"points":10,"bank":["dwimei.","hadi","jami","jamid","mik","Nibop","tel","tep"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nofap tep jamid nibop tel nacid.`","type":"type-en","key":"The person who wrote the book worked.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik kani cadap tep kecid fiwop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I know the child who learned the language.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"C","prompt":"`tel` or no `tel`?","note":"Explain your answer.","text":"`Mik lokid nibop tep suk jamid ___`","type":"explain","key":"No `tel` — the sentence ends there, so the full stop closes the bracket.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"`tel` or no `tel`?","note":"Explain your answer.","text":"`Nibop tep suk jamid ___ dwimei.`","type":"explain","key":"`tel` required — the main verb `dwimei` still has to come, so the bracket must be closed explicitly.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L24","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 24","title":"Say that you know that…","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> report what someone said, thought, or wanted — and nest one clause inside another.</p>\n<h3>1. The same bracket, used as an object</h3>\n<p>Verbs like <em>know</em>, <em>think</em>, <em>say</em> and <em>want</em> take a whole sentence as their object. Fiwo uses the same <code>tep</code> bracket, and needs no word for &quot;that&quot;.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik kani tep das usid.</strong> — I know that she slept.\n<strong>Mik pozi tep suk fwapei.</strong> — I think that you are right.\n<strong>Mik fabid tep suk nomi batap.</strong> — I wanted you to eat the food.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. Quoting someone</h3>\n<p>Because brackets are strict delimiters, a quoted sentence keeps its own mood tag inside them.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Das gesid tep hap mik kypei.</strong> — She said, &quot;I am happy.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Shift the pronoun to turn direct speech into indirect:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Daq gesid tep hap dal wible kypei.</strong> — He said that he himself was happy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>wible</code> from Lesson 18 does the disambiguating work — without it, <code>dal</code> would be somebody else entirely.</p>\n<h3>3. Two brackets deep, and no further</h3>\n<p>You may hold <strong>two</strong> open brackets at once. A third crashes the sentence — deliberately, because three is past what a listener can hold in their head.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik karxid nofap tep jofid cadap tep otanid batap.</strong>\n— I found the person [who helped the child [who cooked the food]].</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If the brackets end mid-sentence, close each one explicitly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nofap tep jofid cadap tep otanid batap tel tel usid.</strong>\n— The person [who helped the child [who cooked the food]] slept.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Need a third level? You cannot have one. Flatten it with a wall from Lesson 21 — split it into two sentences joined by <code>bef</code> or <code>pen</code>. This limit is a feature: it is the reason spoken Fiwo stays followable.</p>\n<h3>4. Making someone do something</h3>\n<p><code>sapi</code> (to make) builds physical objects. To say you <em>caused an action</em> — English <em>made the child sing</em> — use <code>lini</code> and put the resulting action inside a bracket.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik linid tep cadap uropi.</strong> — I made the child sing.\n<em>(I caused [ the child sings ])</em></p>\n<p><strong>Batap jabe linid tep mik goilei.</strong> — The cold food made me sad.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The bracket holds a complete inner sentence, exactly as in section 1. Fiwo has no separate causative form — it reuses machinery you already have.</p>\n<h3>5. Where a following phrase attaches</h3>\n<p><code>tel</code> decides what the trailing words modify.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik kani tep suk usid tel nudu.</strong> — Today, I know that you slept.\n<em>(<code>nudu</code> attaches to <code>kani</code>, because <code>tel</code> closed the inner clause first)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Without <code>tel</code>, the time word would land inside the bracket and describe <em>when you slept</em> instead. One word, two different meanings — place it deliberately.</p>\n","vocab":[],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik kani tep das usid.","english":"I know that she slept."},{"fiwo":"Mik pozi tep suk fwapei.","english":"I think that you are right."},{"fiwo":"Mik fabid tep suk nomi batap.","english":"I wanted you to eat the food."},{"fiwo":"Das gesid tep hap mik kypei.","english":"She said, \"I am happy.\""},{"fiwo":"Daq gesid tep hap dal wible kypei.","english":"He said that he himself was happy."},{"fiwo":"Mik linid tep cadap uropi.","english":"I made the child sing."},{"fiwo":"Batap jabe linid tep mik goilei.","english":"The cold food made me sad."},{"fiwo":"Mik kani tep suk usid tel nudu.","english":"Today, I know that you slept."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I know that she cooked the food.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik kani tep das otanid batap.`","answers":["Mik kani tep das otanid batap."],"points":10,"bank":["batap.","das","kani","Mik","otanid","tep"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"He said, \"I am tired.\"","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Daq gesid tep hap mik iwei.`","answers":["Daq gesid tep hap mik iwei."],"points":10,"bank":["Daq","gesid","hap","iwei.","mik","tep"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I want you to help me.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik fabi tep suk jofi mik.`","answers":["Mik fabi tep suk jofi mik."],"points":10,"bank":["fabi","jofi","Mik","mik.","suk","tep"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik pozi tep suk nes fwapei.`","type":"type-en","key":"I think that you are not right.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das gesid tep hap dal wible iwei.`","type":"type-en","key":"She said that she herself is tired.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik kani tep das jamid nibop tel wecdu.`","type":"type-en","key":"Yesterday, I knew that she wrote the book.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Flatten it.","note":"This needs three levels of nesting, which is illegal. Rewrite it as two clauses joined by a wall.","text":"*I know the person who helped the child who cooked the food, and you ate it.*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik kani nofap tep jofid cadap tep otanid batap tel tel bef suk nomid ram.` — the fourth level becomes its own clause after `bef`.","answers":["Mik kani nofap tep jofid cadap tep otanid batap tel tel bef suk nomid ram."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","bef","cadap","jofid","kani","Mik","nofap","nomid","otanid","ram.","suk","tel","tel","tep","tep"]}]},{"id":"L25","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 25","title":"If, then, and as for","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> state a condition, and announce what you are about to talk about.</p>\n<h3>1. Conditions come in two halves</h3>\n<p><code>syn</code> opens the condition, <code>can</code> delivers the result. Both are required, and <code>syn</code> always comes first.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Syn mik lolei can mik nomis.</strong> — If I am hungry, then I will eat.\n<strong>Syn suk jofi mik can mik grawis.</strong> — If you help me, then I will thank you.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You may use <code>pen</code> instead of <code>can</code> when the result is a logical necessity rather than a sequence.</p>\n<h3>2. Unreal conditions</h3>\n<p>Pair <code>syn</code> with the <code>Hyp</code> tag from Lesson 15 to mark the whole thing as counterfactual.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hyp syn mik lolei can mik nomis batap.</strong> — I would eat the food if I were hungry.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The tag goes at the very front, before <code>syn</code>, and colours the entire sentence.</p>\n<h3>3. Announcing your topic</h3>\n<p><code>zet</code> fronts what you are about to talk about, and <code>can</code> returns you to the comment.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Zet batap can ram zuzkei.</strong> — As for the food, it is sweet.\n<strong>Zet nibop can mik lokid ram.</strong> — As for the book, I read it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is how you change subject cleanly, or put weight on something without disturbing the SVO slots. The topic sits <em>outside</em> the sentence proper; what follows <code>can</code> is a complete sentence on its own.</p>\n","vocab":[],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Syn mik lolei can mik nomis.","english":"If I am hungry, then I will eat."},{"fiwo":"Syn suk jofi mik can mik grawis.","english":"If you help me, then I will thank you."},{"fiwo":"Hyp syn mik lolei can mik nomis batap.","english":"I would eat the food if I were hungry."},{"fiwo":"Zet batap can ram zuzkei.","english":"As for the food, it is sweet."},{"fiwo":"Zet nibop can mik lokid ram.","english":"As for the book, I read it."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"If I am tired, then I will sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Syn mik iwei can mik usis.`","answers":["Syn mik iwei can mik usis."],"points":10,"bank":["can","iwei","mik","mik","Syn","usis."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"If you cook the food, then I will eat it.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Syn suk otani batap can mik nomis ram.`","answers":["Syn suk otani batap can mik nomis ram."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","can","mik","nomis","otani","ram.","suk","Syn"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"As for the water, it is cold.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Zet dorsop can ram jabei.`","answers":["Zet dorsop can ram jabei."],"points":10,"bank":["can","dorsop","jabei.","ram","Zet"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I would help you if I were not tired.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Hyp syn mik nes iwei can mik jofis suk.`","answers":["Hyp syn mik nes iwei can mik jofis suk."],"points":10,"bank":["can","Hyp","iwei","jofis","mik","mik","nes","suk.","syn"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Syn mik nomid batap can mik nes lolei.`","type":"type-en","key":"If I ate the food, then I am not hungry.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Zet ibalirap can dal kritri fiwop.`","type":"type-en","key":"As for the teacher, he teaches the language.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"✗ `Mik nomis batap syn mik lolei.` — the learner put the condition second.","type":"explain","key":"`syn` must open the sentence — the condition always comes before the result. Correct: `Syn mik lolei can mik nomis batap.`","answers":["Syn mik lolei can mik nomis batap."],"points":6}]},{"id":"R5","kind":"review","name":"Review 5","title":"Lessons 21–25","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I found the key but I lost it.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik karxid lusop bul mik wafrid ram.`","answers":["Mik karxid lusop bul mik wafrid ram."],"points":10,"bank":["bul","karxid","lusop","mik","Mik","ram.","wafrid"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I ate bread, meat, and fruit.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid bryklop sek mitap sek zutap.`","answers":["Mik nomid bryklop sek mitap sek zutap."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop","Mik","mitap","nomid","sek","sek","zutap."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The person who wrote the book slept.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofap tep jamid nibop tel usid.`","answers":["Nofap tep jamid nibop tel usid."],"points":10,"bank":["jamid","nibop","Nofap","tel","tep","usid."]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I know that she is tired.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik kani tep das iwei.`","answers":["Mik kani tep das iwei."],"points":10,"bank":["das","iwei.","kani","Mik","tep"]},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"If you help me, then I will thank you.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Syn suk jofi mik can mik grawis.`","answers":["Syn suk jofi mik can mik grawis."],"points":10,"bank":["can","grawis.","jofi","mik","mik","suk","Syn"]},{"n":6,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"As for the food, it is sweet.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Zet batap can ram zuzkei.`","answers":["Zet batap can ram zuzkei."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","can","ram","Zet","zuzkei."]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik otanid mitap vel das usid.`","type":"type-en","key":"I cooked the meat while she slept.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik kani ibalirap tep kritrid suk.`","type":"type-en","key":"I know the teacher who taught you.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Daq gesid tep hap dal wible kypei.`","type":"type-en","key":"He said that he himself is happy.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup suk nacid kad wal?`","type":"type-en","key":"Why did you work?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"`lan` or `bef`?","text":"I drank the water and the milk.","type":"choice","key":"`lan`","answers":["lan"],"points":10,"options":["lan","bef"]},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"`lan` or `bef`?","text":"I drank the water and I slept.","type":"choice","key":"`bef`","answers":["bef"],"points":10,"options":["lan","bef"]},{"n":13,"part":"D","prompt":"Explain.","text":"Why is `Mik kani tep suk usid tel nudu.` different from the same sentence without `tel`?","type":"explain","key":"With `tel`, the bracket closes before `nudu`, so *today* attaches to the main verb *know* — today I know that you slept. Without `tel`, `nudu` falls inside the bracket and describes when you slept.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":14,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: tell a three-sentence story about your day that uses one wall, one relative clause, and one conditional.","text":"Without looking: tell a three-sentence story about your day that uses one wall, one relative clause, and one conditional.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L26","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 26","title":"Count things","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> count to ten, say <em>all</em>, <em>some</em>, <em>none</em>, <em>any</em> — and build the words for <em>nobody</em>, <em>nothing</em> and <em>nowhere</em> yourself.</p>\n<h3>1. Numbers are modifiers</h3>\n<p>Like every describing word, a number goes <strong>after</strong> the noun and looks left.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jedi nibo tafe.</strong> — I have three books.\n<strong>Mik karxid cada kage.</strong> — I found two children.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>bime</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>gloke</code></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>prure</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kage</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>raje</code></td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code>marte</code></td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tafe</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code>sluqe</code></td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>zewe</code></td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lere</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><code>noze</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>2. <code>je</code> versus a number</h3>\n<p>You met <code>je</code> in Lesson 6. Use it when the count is unknown or irrelevant. When you give an exact number, <code>je</code> is redundant and must be dropped.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jedi nibop je.</strong> — I have the books.\n<strong>Mik jedi nibo tafe.</strong> — I have three books.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Never both. The number already says <em>more than one</em>.</p>\n<h3>3. Fiwo has no word for <em>nobody</em></h3>\n<p>And it does not need one. You build the whole family from four base nouns and five operators — a grid you can compute rather than memorise.</p>\n<p><strong>Base nouns:</strong> <code>nofa</code> person · <code>cemu</code> thing · <code>rerpo</code> place · <code>dionu</code> time</p>\n<p><strong>Operators:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>-r</code> (Lesson 3)</td>\n<td>some</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>late</code></td>\n<td>all</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>noze</code></td>\n<td>zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dene</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>merne</code></td>\n<td>most</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Multiply them together:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>some</th>\n<th>all</th>\n<th>none</th>\n<th>any</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>person</strong></td>\n<td><code>nofar</code> someone</td>\n<td><code>nofa late</code> everyone</td>\n<td><code>nofa noze</code> nobody</td>\n<td><code>nofa dene</code> anyone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>thing</strong></td>\n<td><code>cemur</code> something</td>\n<td><code>cemu late</code> everything</td>\n<td><code>cemu noze</code> nothing</td>\n<td><code>cemu dene</code> anything</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>place</strong></td>\n<td><code>rerpor</code> somewhere</td>\n<td><code>rerpo late</code> everywhere</td>\n<td><code>rerpo noze</code> nowhere</td>\n<td><code>rerpo dene</code> anywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>time</strong></td>\n<td><code>dionur</code> sometime</td>\n<td><code>dionu late</code> always</td>\n<td><code>dionu noze</code> never</td>\n<td><code>dionu dene</code> anytime</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Twenty English words replaced by nine pieces. Learn the pieces, get the grid free.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nofa late nomi.</strong> — Everyone eats.\n<strong>Cemu noze gawei.</strong> — Nothing is good.\n<strong>Nofa dene nomi.</strong> — Anyone eats.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Two shortcuts exist for the most common cells: <code>ovce</code> (always) and <code>amce</code> (never), which you met in Lesson 10. They mean exactly the same as <code>dionu late</code> and <code>dionu noze</code> — prefer the short ones in speech.</p>\n<h3>4. Vague amounts</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>late</code></td>\n<td>all</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cuje</code></td>\n<td>some</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jedle</code></td>\n<td>many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cohe</code></td>\n<td>few</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jedi nibo jedle.</strong> — I have many books.\n<strong>Nofa cuje usid.</strong> — Some people slept.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"bime","english":"1"},{"fiwo":"gloke","english":"4"},{"fiwo":"prure","english":"7"},{"fiwo":"kage","english":"2"},{"fiwo":"raje","english":"5"},{"fiwo":"marte","english":"8"},{"fiwo":"tafe","english":"3"},{"fiwo":"sluqe","english":"6"},{"fiwo":"zewe","english":"9"},{"fiwo":"lere","english":"10"},{"fiwo":"noze","english":"0"},{"fiwo":"late","english":"all"},{"fiwo":"noze","english":"zero"},{"fiwo":"dene","english":"any"},{"fiwo":"merne","english":"most"},{"fiwo":"late","english":"all"},{"fiwo":"cuje","english":"some"},{"fiwo":"jedle","english":"many"},{"fiwo":"cohe","english":"few"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik jedi nibo tafe.","english":"I have three books."},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid cada kage.","english":"I found two children."},{"fiwo":"Mik jedi nibop je.","english":"I have the books."},{"fiwo":"Mik jedi nibo tafe.","english":"I have three books."},{"fiwo":"Nofa late nomi.","english":"Everyone eats."},{"fiwo":"Cemu noze gawei.","english":"Nothing is good."},{"fiwo":"Nofa dene nomi.","english":"Anyone eats."},{"fiwo":"Mik jedi nibo jedle.","english":"I have many books."},{"fiwo":"Nofa cuje usid.","english":"Some people slept."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","jedi","nibo","tafe."],"blank":3,"answer":"tafe","pre":"","post":".","english":"I have three books.","fiwo":"Mik jedi nibo tafe."},{"tokens":["Mik","karxid","cada","kage."],"blank":3,"answer":"kage","pre":"","post":".","english":"I found two children.","fiwo":"Mik karxid cada kage."},{"tokens":["Nofa","late","nomi."],"blank":1,"answer":"late","pre":"","post":"","english":"Everyone eats.","fiwo":"Nofa late nomi."},{"tokens":["Cemu","noze","gawei."],"blank":1,"answer":"noze","pre":"","post":"","english":"Nothing is good.","fiwo":"Cemu noze gawei."},{"tokens":["Nofa","dene","nomi."],"blank":1,"answer":"dene","pre":"","post":"","english":"Anyone eats.","fiwo":"Nofa dene nomi."},{"tokens":["Mik","jedi","nibo","jedle."],"blank":3,"answer":"jedle","pre":"","post":".","english":"I have many books.","fiwo":"Mik jedi nibo jedle."},{"tokens":["Nofa","cuje","usid."],"blank":1,"answer":"cuje","pre":"","post":"","english":"Some people slept.","fiwo":"Nofa cuje usid."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I have five books.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik jedi nibo raje.`","answers":["Mik jedi nibo raje."],"points":10,"bank":["jedi","merne","Mik","nibo","prure","raje."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Everyone slept.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofa late usid.`","answers":["Nofa late usid."],"points":10,"bank":["cuje","late","marte","Nofa","usid."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Nothing is sweet.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Cemu noze zuzkei.`","answers":["Cemu noze zuzkei."],"points":10,"bank":["Cemu","dene","noze","raje","zuzkei."]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Some people worked.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofa cuje nacid.`","answers":["Nofa cuje nacid."],"points":10,"bank":["cuje","gloke","nacid.","Nofa","zewe"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Build it from the grid.","text":"nowhere","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`rerpo noze`","answers":["rerpo noze"],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Build it from the grid.","text":"someone","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nofar`","answers":["nofar"],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Build it from the grid.","text":"anytime","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`dionu dene`","answers":["dionu dene"],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik karxid cada gloke.`","type":"type-en","key":"I found four children.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nofa noze nacid.`","type":"type-en","key":"Nobody worked.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik jedi gidu cohe.`","type":"type-en","key":"I have little money.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"D","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"`Mik jedi nibop je tafe.` — the learner said *three books*.","type":"explain","key":"`je` and a number cannot both appear — the number already marks plurality. Correct: `Mik jedi nibo tafe.`","answers":["Mik jedi nibo tafe."],"points":6}]},{"id":"L27","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 27","title":"Bigger numbers, order, and how often","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say forty-two, say <em>the third door</em>, and say <em>I did it twice</em>.</p>\n<h3>1. Stacking digits</h3>\n<p>For any number above nine, write the digits in order and glue every one of them with <code>-t</code> — including the last.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nofa gloket kaget</strong> — forty-two people\n<strong>Nofa bimet nozet zewet</strong> — 109 people</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The <code>-t</code> tells the parser these digits form one place-value block rather than separate quantities. Read them left to right, exactly as you would in English.</p>\n<p><code>drabe</code> = one hundred · <code>egmabe</code> = one thousand</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jedi gidu drabe.</strong> — I have a hundred units of money.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. First, second, third</h3>\n<p>Ordinals use the bridge <code>usy</code> — your first preposition. It links the noun to its rank.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hoxo usy tafe</strong> — the third door\n<strong>Cada usy bime usid.</strong> — The first child slept.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Once, twice, three times</h3>\n<p>Frequency uses a second bridge, <code>igy</code>. It attaches to the verb and counts how many times the action happened.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik usid igy kage.</strong> — I slept twice.\n<strong>Das otanid igy tafe batap.</strong> — She cooked the food three times.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why a separate word? Because a bare number after a verb means <em>intensity</em>, not <em>count</em> — <code>Mik nacid raje</code> is <em>I worked intensely</em>, not <em>I worked five times</em>. <code>igy</code> keeps the two apart.</p>\n<p><em>(These are the first two prepositions in the course. The full system arrives in Lesson 31; these two have narrow jobs and you need them now.)</em></p>\n<h3>4. Spreading one description across a list</h3>\n<p>By default a modifier looks left and grabs only the nearest noun. In a list joined by <code>lan</code>, that means it describes just the last item:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik karxid nibo lan kalo jaxke.</strong> — I found a book and a <em>new</em> cup. <em>(only the cup is new)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Add the distributive flag <code>-f</code> to the modifier and it spreads backward across the whole chain:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik karxid nibo lan kalo jaxkef.</strong> — I found a new book and a new cup. <em>(both are new)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>-f</code> acts like mathematical brackets around the list. One letter decides whether you described one item or all of them.</p>\n<h3>5. Remaining quantity words</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>gu</code></td>\n<td>number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ulime</code></td>\n<td>half</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"gu","english":"number"},{"fiwo":"ulime","english":"half"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Nofa gloket kaget","english":"forty-two people"},{"fiwo":"Nofa bimet nozet zewet","english":"109 people"},{"fiwo":"Mik jedi gidu drabe.","english":"I have a hundred units of money."},{"fiwo":"Hoxo usy tafe","english":"the third door"},{"fiwo":"Cada usy bime usid.","english":"The first child slept."},{"fiwo":"Mik usid igy kage.","english":"I slept twice."},{"fiwo":"Das otanid igy tafe batap.","english":"She cooked the food three times."},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid nibo lan kalo jaxke.","english":"I found a book and a *new* cup. *(only the cup is new)*"},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid nibo lan kalo jaxkef.","english":"I found a new book and a new cup. *(both are new)*"}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Write the number.","text":"42 birds *(use `kyfa` — you meet it properly in Lesson 34)*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`kyfa gloket kaget`","answers":["kyfa gloket kaget"],"points":10,"bank":["gloket","gu","kaget","kyfa","ulime"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Write the number.","text":"109 people","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nofa bimet nozet zewet`","answers":["nofa bimet nozet zewet"],"points":10,"bank":["bimet","gu","nofa","nozet","ulime","zewet"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Write the number.","text":"a hundred books","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nibo drabe`","answers":["nibo drabe"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"the second door","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`hoxo usy kage`","answers":["hoxo usy kage"],"points":10,"bank":["gu","hoxo","kage","ulime","usy"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I ate twice.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid igy kage.`","answers":["Mik nomid igy kage."],"points":10,"bank":["gu","igy","kage.","Mik","nomid","ulime"]},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The first person worked.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofap usy bime nacid.`","answers":["Nofap usy bime nacid."],"points":10,"bank":["bime","gu","nacid.","Nofap","ulime","usy"]},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik usid igy raje.`","type":"type-en","key":"I slept five times.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Hoxo usy gloke.`","type":"type-en","key":"the fourth door","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nacid raje.`","type":"type-en","key":"I worked intensely. *(not five times — that would need `igy`)*","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L28","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 28","title":"Buy something","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> shop — ask a price, say it is too expensive, and pay.</p>\n<h3>1. No new grammar</h3>\n<p>This lesson is pure vocabulary and practice. Everything you need you already have: questions from Lesson 12, numbers from Lesson 26, <code>slafe</code> (too) from Lesson 20.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>gidsi</code></td>\n<td>to buy</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xive</code></td>\n<td>expensive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gidafi</code></td>\n<td>to sell</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xyro</code></td>\n<td>shop, store</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>melfu</code></td>\n<td>price</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>skoru</code></td>\n<td>market</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>spoce</code></td>\n<td>cheap</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>pagari</code></td>\n<td>to pay</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik gidsid nibop.</strong> — I bought the book.\n<strong>Das gidafid sydop.</strong> — She sold the house.\n<strong>Melfup xivei.</strong> — The price is expensive.\n<strong>Mik pagarid gidup.</strong> — I paid the money.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. A shopping conversation</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sal! Kup melfup ry bryklop?</strong> — Hello! What is the price of the bread?\n<strong>Gidu lere.</strong> — Ten money.\n<strong>Ov. Ram xivem slafe.</strong> — Oh no. That is too expensive.\n<strong>Kup suk gidsis nibo tafe?</strong> — Will you buy three books?\n<strong>Jas. Mik pagaris nu.</strong> — Yes. I will pay now.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note <code>ry</code> from Lesson 7 doing the work of English <em>of</em>. The <code>-e</code> possessive only applies to <strong>pronouns</strong> — for one noun owning another, you need <code>ry</code>.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"gidsi","english":"to buy"},{"fiwo":"xive","english":"expensive"},{"fiwo":"gidafi","english":"to sell"},{"fiwo":"xyro","english":"shop, store"},{"fiwo":"melfu","english":"price"},{"fiwo":"skoru","english":"market"},{"fiwo":"spoce","english":"cheap"},{"fiwo":"pagari","english":"to pay"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik gidsid nibop.","english":"I bought the book."},{"fiwo":"Das gidafid sydop.","english":"She sold the house."},{"fiwo":"Melfup xivei.","english":"The price is expensive."},{"fiwo":"Mik pagarid gidup.","english":"I paid the money."},{"fiwo":"Sal! Kup melfup ry bryklop?","english":"Hello! What is the price of the bread?"},{"fiwo":"Gidu lere.","english":"Ten money."},{"fiwo":"Ov. Ram xivem slafe.","english":"Oh no. That is too expensive."},{"fiwo":"Kup suk gidsis nibo tafe?","english":"Will you buy three books?"},{"fiwo":"Jas. Mik pagaris nu.","english":"Yes. I will pay now."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I bought three books.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik gidsid nibo tafe.`","answers":["Mik gidsid nibo tafe."],"points":10,"bank":["gidsid","melfu","Mik","nibo","spoce","tafe."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The price is cheap.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Melfup spocei.`","answers":["Melfup spocei."],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She sells the food.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das gidafi batap.`","answers":["Das gidafi batap."],"points":10,"bank":["batap.","Das","gidafi","spoce","xive"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"That is too expensive.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Ram xivem slafe.`","answers":["Ram xivem slafe."],"points":10,"bank":["gidafi","gidsi","Ram","slafe.","xivem"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik pagarid gidu lere.`","type":"type-en","key":"I paid ten money.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Skorup tandei bul xyrop nifnei.`","type":"type-en","key":"The market is big but the shop is small.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"","note":"Write a four-line shopping exchange in Fiwo, buying anything you have a word for.","text":"Write a four-line shopping exchange in Fiwo, buying anything you have a word for.","type":"explain","key":"Any valid four-line exchange.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L29","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 29","title":"Do arithmetic out loud","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> say sums, fractions and decimals — precisely enough that a machine could not misread you.</p>\n<h3>1. The operators</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>lan</code></td>\n<td>plus (the same glue from Lesson 22)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mis</code></td>\n<td>minus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mux</code></td>\n<td>times</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ap</code></td>\n<td>divided by</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pot</code></td>\n<td>decimal point</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>To say <em>equals</em>, use the stative verb <code>labnei</code> — from <code>labne</code> (same), Lesson 20.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kage lan kage labnei gloke.</strong> — Two plus two equals four.\n<strong>Raje mis kage labnei tafe.</strong> — Five minus two equals three.\n<strong>Tafe mux kage labnei sluqe.</strong> — Three times two equals six.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. Fractions</h3>\n<p><code>ap</code> divides, so it also builds fractions.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bime ap kage</strong> — one half\n<strong>Dorso bime ap kage</strong> — half of the water</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. Decimals and negatives</h3>\n<p><code>pot</code> marks the decimal point; digits after it are read singly.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>tafe pot bime gloke</code> — 3.14</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For a negative number, flag it with <code>-m</code> (Lesson 20) and add <code>mise</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>raje mis rajem mise</code> — five minus negative five</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"lan","english":"plus (the same glue from Lesson 22)"},{"fiwo":"mis","english":"minus"},{"fiwo":"mux","english":"times"},{"fiwo":"ap","english":"divided by"},{"fiwo":"pot","english":"decimal point"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Kage lan kage labnei gloke.","english":"Two plus two equals four."},{"fiwo":"Raje mis kage labnei tafe.","english":"Five minus two equals three."},{"fiwo":"Tafe mux kage labnei sluqe.","english":"Three times two equals six."},{"fiwo":"Bime ap kage","english":"one half"},{"fiwo":"Dorso bime ap kage","english":"half of the water"}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Kage","lan","kage","labnei","gloke."],"blank":1,"answer":"lan","pre":"","post":"","english":"Two plus two equals four.","fiwo":"Kage lan kage labnei gloke."},{"tokens":["Raje","mis","kage","labnei","tafe."],"blank":1,"answer":"mis","pre":"","post":"","english":"Five minus two equals three.","fiwo":"Raje mis kage labnei tafe."},{"tokens":["Tafe","mux","kage","labnei","sluqe."],"blank":1,"answer":"mux","pre":"","post":"","english":"Three times two equals six.","fiwo":"Tafe mux kage labnei sluqe."},{"tokens":["Bime","ap","kage"],"blank":1,"answer":"ap","pre":"","post":"","english":"one half","fiwo":"Bime ap kage"},{"tokens":["Dorso","bime","ap","kage"],"blank":2,"answer":"ap","pre":"","post":"","english":"half of the water","fiwo":"Dorso bime ap kage"}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Three plus four equals seven.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Tafe lan gloke labnei prure.`","answers":["Tafe lan gloke labnei prure."],"points":10,"bank":["ap","gloke","labnei","lan","mis","prure.","Tafe"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Ten minus one equals nine.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Lere mis bime labnei zewe.`","answers":["Lere mis bime labnei zewe."],"points":10,"bank":["ap","bime","labnei","Lere","mis","pot","zewe."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"half of the bread","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`bryklo bime ap kage`","answers":["bryklo bime ap kage"],"points":10,"bank":["ap","bime","bryklo","kage","lan","mis"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Gloke mux kage labnei marte.`","type":"type-en","key":"Four times two equals eight.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Lere ap kage labnei raje.`","type":"type-en","key":"Ten divided by two equals five.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L30","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 30","title":"Days, months, and dates","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> name any day or month, and put a date on an event.</p>\n<h3>1. The seven days are single words</h3>\n<p>Each is built from a number plus <code>du</code> (day), and frozen into one word.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>bimedu</code></td>\n<td>Monday</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>rajedu</code></td>\n<td>Friday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kagedu</code></td>\n<td>Tuesday</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>sluqedu</code></td>\n<td>Saturday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tafedu</code></td>\n<td>Wednesday</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>pruredu</code></td>\n<td>Sunday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>glokedu</code></td>\n<td>Thursday</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>They are abstract nouns, so they drop straight into the time slot from Lesson 10.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nacid bimedu.</strong> — I worked on Monday.\n<strong>Mik usis pruredu.</strong> — I will sleep on Sunday.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>To use one as a description, add <code>-e</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Naciu bimedue</strong> — Monday work</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. Months are built, not memorised</h3>\n<p>Months use <code>rugoxu</code> (month) bridged by <code>usy</code> from Lesson 27.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Rugoxu usy bime</strong> — January <em>(month at position one)</em>\n<strong>Rugoxu usy lere</strong> — October\n<strong>Rugoxu usy leret bimet</strong> — November</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Twelve months, zero new words.</p>\n<h3>3. Habits: how to say <em>usually</em></h3>\n<p>One more piece of time vocabulary. <code>hyme</code> marks a habitual action — English <em>usually</em> or <em>used to</em>. It goes straight after the verb.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomi hyme bryklop.</strong> — I usually eat bread.\n<strong>Mik nomid hyme bryklop.</strong> — I used to eat bread.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Put it after the object and it would describe the object instead — <em>habitual bread</em>. Keep it next to the verb.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"bimedu","english":"Monday"},{"fiwo":"rajedu","english":"Friday"},{"fiwo":"kagedu","english":"Tuesday"},{"fiwo":"sluqedu","english":"Saturday"},{"fiwo":"tafedu","english":"Wednesday"},{"fiwo":"pruredu","english":"Sunday"},{"fiwo":"glokedu","english":"Thursday"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nacid bimedu.","english":"I worked on Monday."},{"fiwo":"Mik usis pruredu.","english":"I will sleep on Sunday."},{"fiwo":"Naciu bimedue","english":"Monday work"},{"fiwo":"Rugoxu usy bime","english":"January *(month at position one)*"},{"fiwo":"Rugoxu usy lere","english":"October"},{"fiwo":"Rugoxu usy leret bimet","english":"November"},{"fiwo":"Mik nomi hyme bryklop.","english":"I usually eat bread."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomid hyme bryklop.","english":"I used to eat bread."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","nacid","bimedu."],"blank":2,"answer":"bimedu","pre":"","post":".","english":"I worked on Monday.","fiwo":"Mik nacid bimedu."},{"tokens":["Mik","usis","pruredu."],"blank":2,"answer":"pruredu","pre":"","post":".","english":"I will sleep on Sunday.","fiwo":"Mik usis pruredu."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I worked on Friday.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nacid rajedu.`","answers":["Mik nacid rajedu."],"points":10,"bank":["bimedu","kagedu","Mik","nacid","rajedu."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"March *(month three)*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`rugoxu usy tafe`","answers":["rugoxu usy tafe"],"points":10,"bank":["glokedu","rugoxu","tafe","tafedu","usy"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I usually sleep at night.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik usi hyme dugu.`","answers":["Mik usi hyme dugu."],"points":10,"bank":["dugu.","hyme","kagedu","Mik","tafedu","usi"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik gidsis nibop rajedu.`","type":"type-en","key":"I will buy the book on Friday.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Rugoxu usy marte.`","type":"type-en","key":"August","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Das nacid hyme ky ibazo.` *(`ky` = at — Lesson 31)*","type":"type-en","key":"She used to work at the school.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"R6","kind":"review","name":"Review 6","title":"Lessons 26–30","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I have three books.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik jedi nibo tafe.`","answers":["Mik jedi nibo tafe."],"points":10,"bank":["jedi","Mik","nibo","tafe."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Nobody slept.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofa noze usid.`","answers":["Nofa noze usid."],"points":10,"bank":["Nofa","noze","usid."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"forty-two people","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nofa gloket kaget`","answers":["nofa gloket kaget"],"points":10,"bank":["gloket","kaget","nofa"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"the second door","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`hoxo usy kage`","answers":["hoxo usy kage"],"points":10,"bank":["hoxo","kage","usy"]},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I ate twice.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid igy kage.`","answers":["Mik nomid igy kage."],"points":10,"bank":["igy","kage.","Mik","nomid"]},{"n":6,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Two plus two equals four.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kage lan kage labnei gloke.`","answers":["Kage lan kage labnei gloke."],"points":10,"bank":["gloke.","kage","Kage","labnei","lan"]},{"n":7,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I worked on Monday.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nacid bimedu.`","answers":["Mik nacid bimedu."],"points":10,"bank":["bimedu.","Mik","nacid"]},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Cemu noze zuzkei.`","type":"type-en","key":"Nothing is sweet.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik jedi gidu cohe.`","type":"type-en","key":"I have little money.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Rugoxu usy lere.`","type":"type-en","key":"October","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nomi hyme vyflap.`","type":"type-en","key":"I usually eat rice.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"C","prompt":"Build it from the grid.","text":"everywhere","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`rerpo late`","answers":["rerpo late"],"points":10},{"n":13,"part":"C","prompt":"Build it from the grid.","text":"anyone","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nofa dene`","answers":["nofa dene"],"points":10},{"n":14,"part":"C","prompt":"Build it from the grid.","text":"sometime","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`dionur`","answers":["dionur"],"points":10},{"n":15,"part":"D","prompt":"Explain the difference.","text":"`Mik nacid raje.` versus `Mik nacid igy raje.`","type":"explain","key":"A bare number after a verb scales *intensity* — I worked intensely. `igy` makes it a *count* — I worked five times.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":16,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: say today's day and date, count your possessions of one kind, and ask the price of something.","text":"Without looking: say today's day and date, count your possessions of one kind, and ask the price of something.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L31","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 31","title":"Say where something is","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> put things in places — and finally ask <em>where</em> and <em>when</em>.</p>\n<h3>1. Prepositions are bridges</h3>\n<p>Every preposition ends in <code>-y</code>, so you can spot one instantly. It builds a bridge between the nearest <em>root</em> on its left and the noun on its right.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bata ty zufo.</strong> — The food is on the bed.\n<strong>Lusop deky hanop.</strong> — The key is inside the box.\n<strong>Cadap sacy sydop.</strong> — The child is outside the house.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. The set</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ky</code></td>\n<td>at</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>hemy</code></td>\n<td>near</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ty</code></td>\n<td>on</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>rewy</code></td>\n<td>far from</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>deky</code></td>\n<td>inside</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>zory</code></td>\n<td>between</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sacy</code></td>\n<td>outside</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>zofy</code></td>\n<td>through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>zy</code></td>\n<td>to</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>wy</code></td>\n<td>with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fy</code></td>\n<td>from</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>neswy</code></td>\n<td>without</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>noky</code></td>\n<td>below</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>pary</code></td>\n<td>before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tody</code></td>\n<td>above</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>foly</code></td>\n<td>after</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik xalid fy jurop zy sydop.</strong> — I went from the city to the house.\n<strong>Mik nomid neswy cekgop.</strong> — I ate without salt.\n<strong>Nibop zory kalo lan hanop.</strong> — The book is between the cup and the box.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. A preposition can never dangle</h3>\n<p>It must always have a target to its right. English happily says <em>the house I live in</em>; Fiwo forbids a bare preposition at the end of a clause. (There is one exception, inside <code>tep</code> brackets — look up Rule 30 when you need it.)</p>\n<h3>4. Modifiers are invisible to a bridge</h3>\n<p>A preposition looks past any modifiers to find the nearest <em>root</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nekid zave ky xiolop.</strong> — I sat tall on the chair.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>ky</code> skips over <code>zave</code> and anchors to the verb <code>nekid</code>. This is why you rarely have to think about placement — the bridge finds the root by itself.</p>\n<p>But placement matters in one case:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik jiprid kalop wy dorsop.</strong> — I washed the cup that has water.\n<strong>Mik jiprid wy dorsop kalop.</strong> — I washed the cup using water.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Put the phrase <strong>before</strong> the object when it describes the action; after, when it describes the object.</p>\n<h3>5. Where and when</h3>\n<p>Now that you have prepositions, two question words from Lesson 13 finally work. They sit after a preposition, in the slot the answer would fill.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk cenid ky wer?</strong> — Where did you live?\n<strong>Kup suk usis ty wiq?</strong> — When will you sleep?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>6. Comparing to something</h3>\n<p>Lesson 20 left this unfinished. <code>taly</code> is the comparison bridge — <em>compared to</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sydop tandem jete taly krulop.</strong> — The house is bigger than the room.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>7. Places</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>juro</code></td>\n<td>city</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>wujo</code></td>\n<td>map</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>xali</code></td>\n<td>to go, move</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>caeno</code></td>\n<td>street</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>poxme</code></td>\n<td>left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tivwo</code></td>\n<td>road</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>hiqe</code></td>\n<td>right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>skrehe</code></td>\n<td>on, switched on</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Plus <code>xasu</code> north and <code>ivortu</code> south.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"ky","english":"at"},{"fiwo":"hemy","english":"near"},{"fiwo":"ty","english":"on"},{"fiwo":"rewy","english":"far from"},{"fiwo":"deky","english":"inside"},{"fiwo":"zory","english":"between"},{"fiwo":"sacy","english":"outside"},{"fiwo":"zofy","english":"through"},{"fiwo":"zy","english":"to"},{"fiwo":"wy","english":"with"},{"fiwo":"fy","english":"from"},{"fiwo":"neswy","english":"without"},{"fiwo":"noky","english":"below"},{"fiwo":"pary","english":"before"},{"fiwo":"tody","english":"above"},{"fiwo":"foly","english":"after"},{"fiwo":"juro","english":"city"},{"fiwo":"wujo","english":"map"},{"fiwo":"xali","english":"to go, move"},{"fiwo":"caeno","english":"street"},{"fiwo":"poxme","english":"left"},{"fiwo":"tivwo","english":"road"},{"fiwo":"hiqe","english":"right"},{"fiwo":"skrehe","english":"on, switched on"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Bata ty zufo.","english":"The food is on the bed."},{"fiwo":"Lusop deky hanop.","english":"The key is inside the box."},{"fiwo":"Cadap sacy sydop.","english":"The child is outside the house."},{"fiwo":"Mik xalid fy jurop zy sydop.","english":"I went from the city to the house."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomid neswy cekgop.","english":"I ate without salt."},{"fiwo":"Nibop zory kalo lan hanop.","english":"The book is between the cup and the box."},{"fiwo":"Mik nekid zave ky xiolop.","english":"I sat tall on the chair."},{"fiwo":"Mik jiprid kalop wy dorsop.","english":"I washed the cup that has water."},{"fiwo":"Mik jiprid wy dorsop kalop.","english":"I washed the cup using water."},{"fiwo":"Kup suk cenid ky wer?","english":"Where did you live?"},{"fiwo":"Kup suk usis ty wiq?","english":"When will you sleep?"},{"fiwo":"Sydop tandem jete taly krulop.","english":"The house is bigger than the room."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Bata","ty","zufo."],"blank":1,"answer":"ty","pre":"","post":"","english":"The food is on the bed.","fiwo":"Bata ty zufo."},{"tokens":["Lusop","deky","hanop."],"blank":1,"answer":"deky","pre":"","post":"","english":"The key is inside the box.","fiwo":"Lusop deky hanop."},{"tokens":["Cadap","sacy","sydop."],"blank":1,"answer":"sacy","pre":"","post":"","english":"The child is outside the house.","fiwo":"Cadap sacy sydop."},{"tokens":["Mik","xalid","fy","jurop","zy","sydop."],"blank":2,"answer":"fy","pre":"","post":"","english":"I went from the city to the house.","fiwo":"Mik xalid fy jurop zy sydop."},{"tokens":["Mik","nomid","neswy","cekgop."],"blank":2,"answer":"neswy","pre":"","post":"","english":"I ate without salt.","fiwo":"Mik nomid neswy cekgop."},{"tokens":["Nibop","zory","kalo","lan","hanop."],"blank":1,"answer":"zory","pre":"","post":"","english":"The book is between the cup and the box.","fiwo":"Nibop zory kalo lan hanop."},{"tokens":["Mik","nekid","zave","ky","xiolop."],"blank":3,"answer":"ky","pre":"","post":"","english":"I sat tall on the chair.","fiwo":"Mik nekid zave ky xiolop."},{"tokens":["Mik","jiprid","kalop","wy","dorsop."],"blank":3,"answer":"wy","pre":"","post":"","english":"I washed the cup that has water.","fiwo":"Mik jiprid kalop wy dorsop."},{"tokens":["Mik","jiprid","wy","dorsop","kalop."],"blank":2,"answer":"wy","pre":"","post":"","english":"I washed the cup using water.","fiwo":"Mik jiprid wy dorsop kalop."},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","cenid","ky","wer?"],"blank":3,"answer":"ky","pre":"","post":"","english":"Where did you live?","fiwo":"Kup suk cenid ky wer?"},{"tokens":["Kup","suk","usis","ty","wiq?"],"blank":3,"answer":"ty","pre":"","post":"","english":"When will you sleep?","fiwo":"Kup suk usis ty wiq?"}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The book is on the table.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nibop ty degop.`","answers":["Nibop ty degop."],"points":10,"bank":["degop.","neswy","Nibop","noky","ty"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The key is inside the bag.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Lusop deky zabatop.`","answers":["Lusop deky zabatop."],"points":10,"bank":["deky","Lusop","rewy","tivwo","zabatop."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I went from the school to the city.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik xalid fy ibazop zy jurop.`","answers":["Mik xalid fy ibazop zy jurop."],"points":10,"bank":["fy","ibazop","juro","jurop.","Mik","xalid","zory","zy"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Where did you work?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk nacid ky wer?`","answers":["Kup suk nacid ky wer?"],"points":10,"bank":["foly","juro","Kup","ky","nacid","suk","wer?"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Sydop hemy caenop.`","type":"type-en","key":"The house is near the street.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nomid neswy cekgop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I ate without salt.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Jurop rewy tapo.`","type":"type-en","key":"The city is far from here.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"C","prompt":"Which meaning?","note":"Explain the difference.","text":"`Mik jiprid kalop wy dorsop.` versus `Mik jiprid wy dorsop kalop.`","type":"explain","key":"The first anchors `wy` to `kalop` — the cup that has water. The second puts the phrase before the object, anchoring it to the verb — washed *using* water.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L32","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 32","title":"Get from here to there","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> travel — go, come, arrive, leave, stay.</p>\n<h3>1. Movement verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>breni</code></td>\n<td>to come</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>jobori</code></td>\n<td>to arrive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hici</code></td>\n<td>to walk</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>dwagi</code></td>\n<td>to stay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cafi</code></td>\n<td>to run</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kufugli</code></td>\n<td>to travel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>fwimi</code></td>\n<td>to leave</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik hicid zy ibazop.</strong> — I walked to the school.\n<strong>Das cafid fy sydop.</strong> — She ran from the house.\n<strong>Muk joborid zy sydop.</strong> — We arrived at the house.\n<strong>Mik dwagid ky sydop.</strong> — I stayed at the house.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Direction lives in the preposition, not the verb: <code>zy</code> for toward, <code>fy</code> for away. English bakes direction into words like <em>arrive</em> and <em>depart</em>; Fiwo keeps the verb pure and lets the bridge point.</p>\n<h3>2. Vehicles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>nibilko</code></td>\n<td>car</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>atripo</code></td>\n<td>train</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik xalid ky nibilkop.</strong> — I went by car.\n<strong>Muk kufuglid zy jurop.</strong> — We travelled to the city.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"breni","english":"to come"},{"fiwo":"jobori","english":"to arrive"},{"fiwo":"hici","english":"to walk"},{"fiwo":"dwagi","english":"to stay"},{"fiwo":"cafi","english":"to run"},{"fiwo":"kufugli","english":"to travel"},{"fiwo":"fwimi","english":"to leave"},{"fiwo":"nibilko","english":"car"},{"fiwo":"atripo","english":"train"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik hicid zy ibazop.","english":"I walked to the school."},{"fiwo":"Das cafid fy sydop.","english":"She ran from the house."},{"fiwo":"Muk joborid zy sydop.","english":"We arrived at the house."},{"fiwo":"Mik dwagid ky sydop.","english":"I stayed at the house."},{"fiwo":"Mik xalid ky nibilkop.","english":"I went by car."},{"fiwo":"Muk kufuglid zy jurop.","english":"We travelled to the city."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I walked to the city.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik hicid zy jurop.`","answers":["Mik hicid zy jurop."],"points":10,"bank":["atripo","fwimi","hicid","jurop.","Mik","zy"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She came from the school.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das brenid fy ibazop.`","answers":["Das brenid fy ibazop."],"points":10,"bank":["brenid","Das","fy","ibazop.","jobori","kufugli"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"We will travel to the sea by train.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Muk kufuglis zy tubsop ky atripop.`","answers":["Muk kufuglis zy tubsop ky atripop."],"points":10,"bank":["atripop.","fwimi","jobori","kufuglis","ky","Muk","tubsop","zy"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I stayed at home.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik dwagid ky sydop.`","answers":["Mik dwagid ky sydop."],"points":10,"bank":["atripo","dwagi","dwagid","ky","Mik","sydop."]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk joborid zy jurop rajedu.`","type":"type-en","key":"We arrived at the city on Friday.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Cadap cafid zy mana dase.`","type":"type-en","key":"The child ran to her mother.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L33","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 33","title":"What was done to you","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> put the receiver of an action first — the passive voice.</p>\n<h3>1. One word flips the sentence</h3>\n<p>Normally Slot 1 is the doer. Put <code>fap</code> at the front and the roles reverse: Slot 1 becomes the <em>receiver</em>, and Slot 3 — if you fill it — becomes the doer.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Fap nibop jamid.</strong> — The book was written.\n<strong>Fap nibop jamid nofap.</strong> — The book was written by the person.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Fiwo needs no word for <em>by</em>. Flipping the switch flips the slots, so the doer simply occupies the object slot.</p>\n<h3>2. Passive questions</h3>\n<p><code>fap</code> sits right after the mood tag.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup fap nibop jamid?</strong> — Was the book written?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>3. When to use it</h3>\n<p>Use <code>fap</code> when the receiver matters more than the doer, or when you do not know who did it. It is common in instructions, reports and news — and rare in casual speech, exactly as in English.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Fap batap sapid mana mike.</strong> — The food was made by my mother.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>4. Clothes</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ikamo</code></td>\n<td>shirt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>meoko</code></td>\n<td>hat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hekri</code></td>\n<td>to wear</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik hekri ikamop.</strong> — I wear the shirt.\n<strong>Meokop mike leupei.</strong> — My hat is red.\n<strong>Belimop flovei.</strong> — The shoe is dirty.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"ikamo","english":"shirt"},{"fiwo":"meoko","english":"hat"},{"fiwo":"hekri","english":"to wear"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Fap nibop jamid.","english":"The book was written."},{"fiwo":"Fap nibop jamid nofap.","english":"The book was written by the person."},{"fiwo":"Kup fap nibop jamid?","english":"Was the book written?"},{"fiwo":"Fap batap sapid mana mike.","english":"The food was made by my mother."},{"fiwo":"Mik hekri ikamop.","english":"I wear the shirt."},{"fiwo":"Meokop mike leupei.","english":"My hat is red."},{"fiwo":"Belimop flovei.","english":"The shoe is dirty."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Mik","hekri","ikamop."],"blank":1,"answer":"hekri","pre":"","post":"","english":"I wear the shirt.","fiwo":"Mik hekri ikamop."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Make it passive.","text":"`Nofap jamid nibop.` → The book was written by the person.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fap nibop jamid nofap.`","answers":["Fap nibop jamid nofap."],"points":10,"bank":["Fap","hekri","ikamo","jamid","nibop","nofap."]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Make it passive.","text":"`Mana mike sapid batap.` → The food was made by my mother.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fap batap sapid mana mike.`","answers":["Fap batap sapid mana mike."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","Fap","ikamo","mana","meoko","mike.","sapid"]},{"n":3,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The house was built. *(use `sapi`)*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fap sydop sapid.`","answers":["Fap sydop sapid."],"points":10,"bank":["Fap","ikamo","meoko","sapid.","sydop"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Was the food eaten?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup fap batap nomid?`","answers":["Kup fap batap nomid?"],"points":10,"bank":["batap","fap","ikamo","Kup","meoko","nomid?"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I wear a red hat.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik hekri meoko leupe.`","answers":["Mik hekri meoko leupe."],"points":10,"bank":["hekri","ikamo","leupe.","meoko","Mik"]},{"n":6,"part":"C","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Fap ikamop jipris cadap.`","type":"type-en","key":"The shirt will be washed by the child.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L34","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 34","title":"Weather, nature, and animals","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> talk about the sky, the land, and the creatures on it.</p>\n<h3>1. Weather and sky</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>fevo</code></td>\n<td>sun</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>domlo</code></td>\n<td>rain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grexo</code></td>\n<td>moon</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>vezo</code></td>\n<td>snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>stalo</code></td>\n<td>star</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xabeso</code></td>\n<td>wind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>byso</code></td>\n<td>sky</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>mipko</code></td>\n<td>cloud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fyjebe</code></td>\n<td>warm</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bysop kivwei.</strong> — The sky is blue.\n<strong>Domlop jabei.</strong> — The rain is cold.\n<strong>Xabesop dotei.</strong> — The wind is strong.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. Land and growing things</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>mosa</code></td>\n<td>tree</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>dreko</code></td>\n<td>stone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mula</code></td>\n<td>flower</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>monto</code></td>\n<td>mountain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kegla</code></td>\n<td>grass</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>xadoro</code></td>\n<td>river</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fuego</code></td>\n<td>fire</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>tubso</code></td>\n<td>sea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bucygo</code></td>\n<td>earth</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Montop zavei.</strong> — The mountain is tall.\n<strong>Tubsop tandem merne.</strong> — The sea is the biggest.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note <code>mosa</code>, <code>mula</code> and <code>kegla</code> end in <code>-a</code> — plants are living things. <code>dreko</code> and <code>monto</code> end in <code>-o</code>. The vowel keeps sorting the world for you.</p>\n<h3>3. Animals</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fiwo</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>latwa</code></td>\n<td>animal</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kyfa</code></td>\n<td>bird</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>deta</code></td>\n<td>dog</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>kaba</code></td>\n<td>horse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>catsa</code></td>\n<td>cat</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>zala</code></td>\n<td>insect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Detap cafid.</strong> — The dog ran.\n<strong>Catsap usiq.</strong> — The cat is sleeping.\n<strong>Kyfa je xalid.</strong> — The birds went.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>All <code>-a</code>. Every animal you will ever meet ends in <code>-a</code>.</p>\n","vocab":[{"fiwo":"fevo","english":"sun"},{"fiwo":"domlo","english":"rain"},{"fiwo":"grexo","english":"moon"},{"fiwo":"vezo","english":"snow"},{"fiwo":"stalo","english":"star"},{"fiwo":"xabeso","english":"wind"},{"fiwo":"byso","english":"sky"},{"fiwo":"mipko","english":"cloud"},{"fiwo":"fyjebe","english":"warm"},{"fiwo":"mosa","english":"tree"},{"fiwo":"dreko","english":"stone"},{"fiwo":"mula","english":"flower"},{"fiwo":"monto","english":"mountain"},{"fiwo":"kegla","english":"grass"},{"fiwo":"xadoro","english":"river"},{"fiwo":"fuego","english":"fire"},{"fiwo":"tubso","english":"sea"},{"fiwo":"bucygo","english":"earth"},{"fiwo":"latwa","english":"animal"},{"fiwo":"kyfa","english":"bird"},{"fiwo":"deta","english":"dog"},{"fiwo":"kaba","english":"horse"},{"fiwo":"catsa","english":"cat"},{"fiwo":"zala","english":"insect"}],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Bysop kivwei.","english":"The sky is blue."},{"fiwo":"Domlop jabei.","english":"The rain is cold."},{"fiwo":"Xabesop dotei.","english":"The wind is strong."},{"fiwo":"Montop zavei.","english":"The mountain is tall."},{"fiwo":"Tubsop tandem merne.","english":"The sea is the biggest."},{"fiwo":"Detap cafid.","english":"The dog ran."},{"fiwo":"Catsap usiq.","english":"The cat is sleeping."},{"fiwo":"Kyfa je xalid.","english":"The birds went."}],"cloze":[{"tokens":["Kyfa","je","xalid."],"blank":0,"answer":"Kyfa","pre":"","post":"","english":"The birds went.","fiwo":"Kyfa je xalid."}],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The sky is dark.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Bysop capei.`","answers":["Bysop capei."],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The dog slept near the fire.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Detap usid hemy fuegop.`","answers":["Detap usid hemy fuegop."],"points":10,"bank":["Detap","fuegop.","grexo","hemy","usid","xabeso"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The mountain is bigger than the tree.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Montop tandem jete taly mosap.`","answers":["Montop tandem jete taly mosap."],"points":10,"bank":["jete","monto","Montop","mosap.","taly","tandem","xabeso"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I saw the birds.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik upycid kyfa je.`","answers":["Mik upycid kyfa je."],"points":10,"bank":["byso","je.","kyfa","latwa","Mik","upycid"]},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mulap baudei bul kegla jotsei.`","type":"type-en","key":"The flower is beautiful but the grass is clean.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Xadorop dwimem merne.`","type":"type-en","key":"The river is the longest.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Catsap usiq ty zufop.`","type":"type-en","key":"The cat is sleeping on the bed.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"R7","kind":"review","name":"Review 7","title":"Lessons 31–34","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The key is inside the box.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Lusop deky hanop.`","answers":["Lusop deky hanop."],"points":10,"bank":["deky","hanop.","Lusop"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I walked from the city to the sea.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik hicid fy jurop zy tubsop.`","answers":["Mik hicid fy jurop zy tubsop."],"points":10,"bank":["fy","hicid","jurop","Mik","tubsop.","zy"]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Where did you sleep?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk usid ky wer?`","answers":["Kup suk usid ky wer?"],"points":10,"bank":["Kup","ky","suk","usid","wer?"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The book was written by the teacher.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fap nibop jamid ibalirap.`","answers":["Fap nibop jamid ibalirap."],"points":10,"bank":["Fap","ibalirap.","jamid","nibop"]},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The mountain is bigger than the house.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Montop tandem jete taly sydop.`","answers":["Montop tandem jete taly sydop."],"points":10,"bank":["jete","Montop","sydop.","taly","tandem"]},{"n":6,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The cat is sleeping near the fire.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Catsap usiq hemy fuegop.`","answers":["Catsap usiq hemy fuegop."],"points":10,"bank":["Catsap","fuegop.","hemy","usiq"]},{"n":7,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik nomid neswy cekgop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I ate without salt.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Fap batap sapid mana mike.`","type":"type-en","key":"The food was made by my mother.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":9,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Muk kufuglis zy jurop ky atripop.`","type":"type-en","key":"We will travel to the city by train.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":10,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Xabesop dotei bul fevop ripei.`","type":"type-en","key":"The wind is strong but the sun is hot.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":11,"part":"C","prompt":"Explain the difference.","text":"`Mik jiprid kalop wy dorsop.` versus `Mik jiprid wy dorsop kalop.`","type":"explain","key":"The first anchors `wy` to the cup — the cup that has water. The second anchors it to the verb — washed *using* water.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"D","prompt":"Spoken.","note":"Without looking: describe today's weather, say where three objects are, and describe a journey you made this week.","text":"Without looking: describe today's weather, say where three objects are, and describe a journey you made this week.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]},{"id":"L35","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 35","title":"Say exactly what you do not mean","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> negate one word instead of a whole sentence, and never be misunderstood about what you are denying.</p>\n<h3>1. <code>nes</code> flips only what comes next</h3>\n<p>You have used <code>nes</code> since Lesson 2. Here is the whole rule: it is a logical operator that reverses <strong>the single block immediately to its right</strong>. Nothing more.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nes kyti batap.</strong> — I do not like the food.\n<strong>Daq nes brenid.</strong> — He did not come.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>2. Move it, and you deny something else</h3>\n<p>Because it binds only rightward, its position chooses the target.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nes latwa xalid.</strong> — It was not the <em>animal</em> that moved. <em>(something else did)</em>\n<strong>Latwa nes xalid.</strong> — The animal did not <em>move</em>. <em>(it did something else)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>English needs stress or a rewritten sentence for this — <em>it wasn&#39;t the ANIMAL that moved</em>. Fiwo moves one word. You met this in Lesson 19 with <em>must not</em> versus <em>do not have to</em>; the principle is the same and it applies everywhere.</p>\n<h3>3. Negation does not spread across a list</h3>\n<p>This catches everyone. <code>nes</code> binds one block, so in a <code>lan</code> list it negates only the first item.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik karxid nes batap lan dorso.</strong> — I found no food, but I <em>did</em> find water.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>To deny both, say it twice:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik karxid nes batap lan nes dorso.</strong> — I found no food and no water.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And to say <em>I didn&#39;t find both</em> — denying the combination rather than each part — use <code>ron</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik karxid nes batap ron nes dorso.</strong> — I failed to find at least one of them.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Those are three genuinely different claims. English blurs all of them into &quot;I didn&#39;t find food and water.&quot;</p>\n<h3>4. No double negatives</h3>\n<p><code>nes nes</code> does not intensify anything; it cancels. Two flips return you to where you started, exactly as in arithmetic. There is no dialect of Fiwo where <em>I didn&#39;t find nothing</em> means you found nothing.</p>\n","vocab":[],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Mik nes kyti batap.","english":"I do not like the food."},{"fiwo":"Daq nes brenid.","english":"He did not come."},{"fiwo":"Nes latwa xalid.","english":"It was not the *animal* that moved. *(something else did)*"},{"fiwo":"Latwa nes xalid.","english":"The animal did not *move*. *(it did something else)*"},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid nes batap lan dorso.","english":"I found no food, but I *did* find water."},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid nes batap lan nes dorso.","english":"I found no food and no water."},{"fiwo":"Mik karxid nes batap ron nes dorso.","english":"I failed to find at least one of them."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo, negating precisely.","text":"I do not eat meat.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nes nomi mitap.`","answers":["Mik nes nomi mitap."],"points":10,"bank":["Mik","mitap.","nes","nomi"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo, negating precisely.","text":"It was not the child who slept. *(someone else did)*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nes cadap usid.`","answers":["Nes cadap usid."],"points":10,"bank":["cadap","Nes","usid."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo, negating precisely.","text":"I found no bread and no milk.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik karxid nes bryklop lan nes lamsop.`","answers":["Mik karxid nes bryklop lan nes lamsop."],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop","karxid","lamsop.","lan","Mik","nes","nes"]},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English — carefully.","text":"`Mik gidsid nes nibop lan kalop.`","type":"type-en","key":"I bought no book, but I did buy the cup.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English — carefully.","text":"`Nes mana mike otanid batap.`","type":"type-en","key":"It was not my mother who cooked the food.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English — carefully.","text":"`Suk nomi nes fuqe.`","type":"type-en","key":"You do not have to eat.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"Explain.","note":"What is the difference between sentence 4 and `Mik gidsid nes nibop lan nes kalop.`?","text":"What is the difference between sentence 4 and `Mik gidsid nes nibop lan nes kalop.`?","type":"explain","key":"Sentence 4 negates only the book. The second version negates both — no book and no cup.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L36","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 36","title":"Build a word you were never taught","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> generate words nobody taught you, correctly, on demand. This is the lesson that turns your core vocabulary into a vocabulary several times its size.</p>\n<h3>1. Change the last vowel, change the category</h3>\n<p>You have known this since Lesson 1. Now use it deliberately. Take any root and append a new functional vowel — never removing the original.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From</th>\n<th>Add</th>\n<th>Get</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>jami</code> write</td>\n<td><code>-a</code></td>\n<td><code>jamia</code> writer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jami</code> write</td>\n<td><code>-o</code></td>\n<td><code>jamio</code> document</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sydo</code> house</td>\n<td><code>-a</code></td>\n<td><code>sydoa</code> resident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cada</code> child</td>\n<td><code>-u</code></td>\n<td><code>cadau</code> childhood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nofa</code> person</td>\n<td><code>-u</code></td>\n<td><code>nofau</code> humanity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mosa</code> tree</td>\n<td><code>-o</code></td>\n<td><code>mosao</code> lumber</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dorso</code> water</td>\n<td><code>-e</code></td>\n<td><code>dorsoe</code> wet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bata</code> food</td>\n<td><code>-e</code></td>\n<td><code>batae</code> edible</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Jamiap brenid.</strong> — The writer came.\n<strong>Mik lokid jamiop.</strong> — I read the document.\n<strong>Sydoap usiq.</strong> — The resident is sleeping.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The shifts are systematic. A living thing shifted to <code>-u</code> gives its collective or its state of being; a concrete object shifted to <code>-a</code> gives the person who works it; any noun shifted to <code>-e</code> gives &quot;having the qualities of&quot;.</p>\n<h3>2. Two derivations, maximum</h3>\n<p>You may derive twice, never three times. A third vowel is illegal.</p>\n<h3>3. The dictionary does not limit you</h3>\n<p>Some forms you build will not appear in any dictionary. <strong>They are still correct.</strong> <code>jurou</code> (urban life, from <code>juro</code> city) is a legal Rule 5 derivation whether or not anyone has written it down — a listener parses it from the pieces, exactly as you built it.</p>\n<p>This is the difference between a vocabulary you memorised and a vocabulary you can <em>generate</em>. Your 328 core roots are really well over a thousand words.</p>\n<h3>4. Two nouns cannot simply sit together</h3>\n<p><code>bata mosa</code> does not mean <em>tree food</em>. Two nouns in a row is the Zero Copula from Lesson 2 — it means <em>the food is a tree</em>. This is the single most common mistake, and it produces a legal sentence with the wrong meaning, so nothing will warn you.</p>\n<p>To combine two concepts, use one of two methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Shift the second one to a modifier:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bata mosae</strong> — tree food <em>(food that is tree-related)</em>\n<strong>Moblo fuegoe</strong> — fire vehicle, i.e. a fire engine</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Or bridge them with a preposition:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bata zy bucygo</strong> — food of the earth</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>5. When Fiwo genuinely has no word</h3>\n<p>Work down this list in order and stop at the first one that works:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A native word</strong> — search the dictionary properly first</li>\n<li><strong>A derivation</strong> — can you build it from a root you have? (§1)</li>\n<li><strong>A compound</strong> — can you build it from two roots? (§4)</li>\n<li><strong>A borrowing</strong> — last resort: take the foreign word, hyphenate, give it a functional vowel: <code>computer-o</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Most beginners jump to step 4. Almost everything is reachable at step 2 or 3.</p>\n","vocab":[],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Jamiap brenid.","english":"The writer came."},{"fiwo":"Mik lokid jamiop.","english":"I read the document."},{"fiwo":"Sydoap usiq.","english":"The resident is sleeping."},{"fiwo":"Bata mosae","english":"tree food *(food that is tree-related)*"},{"fiwo":"Moblo fuegoe","english":"fire vehicle, i.e. a fire engine"},{"fiwo":"Bata zy bucygo","english":"food of the earth"}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Derive it.","text":"*teacher* from `kritri` (to teach)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`kritria`","answers":["kritria"],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Derive it.","text":"*childhood* from `cada`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`cadau`","answers":["cadau"],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Derive it.","text":"*wet* from `dorso`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`dorsoe`","answers":["dorsoe"],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Derive it.","text":"*the act of eating* from `nomi`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`nomiu`","answers":["nomiu"],"points":10},{"n":5,"part":"B","prompt":"Compound it.","note":"Give both the modifier form and the bridge form.","text":"*school building* (`foiso` building + `ibazo` school)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`foiso ibazoe` or `foiso zy ibazo`","answers":["foiso ibazoe","foiso zy ibazo"],"points":10},{"n":6,"part":"B","prompt":"Compound it.","note":"Give both the modifier form and the bridge form.","text":"*water bird* (`kyfa` + `dorso`)","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`kyfa dorsoe` or `kyfa zy dorso`","answers":["kyfa dorsoe","kyfa zy dorso"],"points":10},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"What is wrong?","text":"`Bata mosa.` — the learner meant *tree food*.","type":"explain","key":"Two nouns side by side is the Zero Copula — this says *the food is a tree*. Correct: `bata mosae`.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":8,"part":"D","prompt":"","note":"You need the word *keyboard* and Fiwo has no root for it. Walk down the four steps and produce your best answer.","text":"You need the word *keyboard* and Fiwo has no root for it. Walk down the four steps and produce your best answer.","type":"explain","key":"Any reasoned answer. A good one: `guto jamie` (writing tool) by compounding, rather than borrowing.","answers":[],"points":6}]},{"id":"L37","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 37","title":"Speak like a person, not a parser","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> use the short forms real speech runs on.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note: this lesson is still partly incomplete.</strong> The correction particle landed 2026-08-07 (Rule 39) and is taught in §5 below. Two spoken-mode questions remain open (Todo 7.4b/c): prosody around brackets and walls, and exactly which markers may be dropped in casual speech beyond the ones taught here.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>1. Short answers are real sentences</h3>\n<p>You met these in Lesson 12. A bare noun phrase, modifier, or prepositional phrase standing alone is a complete, legal utterance — it binds to whatever was just said.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Kup suk lolei?</strong> — Are you hungry?\n<strong>Jas.</strong> — Yes.</p>\n<p><strong>Kup suk nomis wat?</strong> — What will you eat?\n<strong>Bryklop.</strong> — The bread.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The rule that keeps this unambiguous: a fragment is only legal when it <strong>opens an utterance</strong>. A bare modifier in the middle of a running clause is still an error.</p>\n<h3>2. Drop <code>tel</code> whenever the sentence ends</h3>\n<p>From Lesson 23. If the bracket runs to the end, the full stop closes it. In speech this saves a syllable on almost every complex sentence — use it.</p>\n<h3>3. Mood tags are optional in casual speech</h3>\n<p>From Lesson 15. In conversation people drop <code>Kep</code>, <code>Hap</code> and the rest and let their voice carry the tone. <code>Kup</code> is the exception and stays.</p>\n<h3>4. When you misspeak: <code>gix</code></h3>\n<p>This is the one that matters most, and it is the one every other language lets you do without thinking about it.</p>\n<p>Fiwo has exactly one legal parse per sentence. So if you say the wrong word, the sentence is not merely unclear — it is broken, and no amount of carrying on will fix it. <code>gix</code> is your backspace.</p>\n<p><strong>Say <code>gix</code>, and the word right before it is gone.</strong> Then just carry on with the right one.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mik nomid vyflap gix bryklop.</strong> — I ate the rice — <em>no</em> — the bread.\nThe listener hears exactly: <strong>Mik nomid bryklop.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say it twice to erase two words, three times for three. There is no limit inside the clause.</p>\n<p><strong>If you are already too far gone, start the sentence again.</strong> Say <code>gix</code> <em>first</em>, before anything else, and everything you just said is thrown away:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Gix mik nomid bryklop.</strong> — Scratch that. I ate the bread.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Those are the only two behaviours, and <strong>where you say it decides which one you get</strong> — with a word behind it, it erases that word; at the very start, it wipes the last sentence. You never have to choose.</p>\n<p>Three things it will not do:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It never reaches past <code>bef</code>, <code>kad</code>, <code>tep</code> or any other structural word — repair stays inside the clause you are in.</li>\n<li>It cannot erase those structural words themselves. Said the wrong <code>bef</code>? Finish the sentence, then open the next one with <code>gix</code>.</li>\n<li>It needs a finished word to erase. <code>Mik hicid zy gix sydop</code> is wrong, because <code>zy</code> is still waiting for its own noun — nothing is finished yet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Leave a small gap before <code>gix</code> when you say it, so it lands as a correction and not as another word in the sentence.</p>\n<p>One warning: <strong><code>gix</code> is for speaking, not for writing.</strong> When you write, you have a delete key — use that. You will not find <code>gix</code> anywhere else in this course.</p>\n<h3>5. Registers</h3>\n<p>Fragments and dropped tags belong to conversation and texting. In precise registers — instructions, legal, mathematical — write full clauses with tags, because a fragment&#39;s meaning depends on the sentence before it.</p>\n","vocab":[],"examples":[{"fiwo":"Kup suk lolei?","english":"Are you hungry?"},{"fiwo":"Jas.","english":"Yes."},{"fiwo":"Kup suk nomis wat?","english":"What will you eat?"},{"fiwo":"Bryklop.","english":"The bread."},{"fiwo":"Mik nomid vyflap gix bryklop.","english":"I ate the rice — *no* — the bread."},{"fiwo":"Gix mik nomid bryklop.","english":"Scratch that. I ate the bread."}],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Give the shortest legal answer.","text":"`Kup suk usid?`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Jas.` or `Nes.`","answers":["Jas.","Nes."],"points":10},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Give the shortest legal answer.","text":"`Kup suk nomis wat?` *(you will eat rice)*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Vyflap.`","answers":["Vyflap."],"points":10},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Give the shortest legal answer.","text":"`Kup melfup ry bryklop?` *(ten money)*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Gidu lere.`","answers":["Gidu lere."],"points":10},{"n":4,"part":"B","prompt":"Shorten it.","note":"Remove everything droppable.","text":"`Kep mik kani tep suk usid.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik kani tep suk usid.` — the tag is optional and the final `tel` is closed by the full stop.","answers":["Mik kani tep suk usid."],"points":10,"bank":["kani","Mik","suk","tep","usid."]},{"n":5,"part":"C","prompt":"What does the listener hear?","note":"Carry out the repair.","text":"`Mik nomid bryklop gix vyflap.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nomid vyflap.` — `gix` erased `bryklop`.","answers":["Mik nomid vyflap."],"points":10,"bank":["Mik","nomid","vyflap."]},{"n":6,"part":"C","prompt":"What does the listener hear?","note":"Carry out the repair.","text":"`Kup suk nomis vyflap gix bryklop?`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Kup suk nomis bryklop?` — repair works inside a question like anywhere else; `Kup` is untouched because it is structural.","answers":["Kup suk nomis bryklop?"],"points":10,"bank":["bryklop?","Kup","nomis","suk"]},{"n":7,"part":"C","prompt":"What does the listener hear?","note":"Carry out the repair.","text":"`Mik nomid vyflap gix gix hicid.`","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik hicid.` — two `gix` erased `vyflap` and then `nomid`, so the verb slot was free for `hicid`.","answers":["Mik hicid."],"points":10},{"n":8,"part":"D","prompt":"Repair it yourself.","note":"You meant to say `Mik nomid bryklop`, but you have already said `Mik nomid vyflap`. Finish the sentence out loud without starting over.","text":"You meant to say `Mik nomid bryklop`, but you have already said `Mik nomid vyflap`. Finish the sentence out loud without starting over.","type":"explain","key":"`Mik nomid vyflap gix bryklop.`","answers":["Mik nomid vyflap gix bryklop."],"points":6}]},{"id":"L38","kind":"lesson","name":"Lesson 38","title":"Read a story unaided","html":"<p><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong> read continuous Fiwo without a dictionary — and write your own.</p>\n<p>Every word below has been taught in this course. Read it aloud, all the way through, before looking at anything else. Do not translate as you go; let it run.</p>\n<h3>Nofap lan bryklop</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Nofap rizid cihu.</strong>\n<strong>Dal loleid kad dal nes nomid wecdu.</strong>\n<strong>Dal upycid deky hanop bul cemu noze deky ram.</strong>\n<strong>Pen dal hicid zy skorup.</strong>\n<strong>Dal enconid nauxap dale ky skorup.</strong>\n<strong>Nauxap gesid tep hap mik kypei kad mik upyci suk.</strong>\n<strong>Nofap slezid tep Kup melfup ry bryklop.</strong>\n<strong>Nauxap hadid tep gidu raje.</strong>\n<strong>Nofap pagarid gidup bef dal gidsid bryklop.</strong>\n<strong>Dal brenid zy sydop dale bef dal nomid bryklop.</strong>\n<strong>Hap dal nes lolei nu.</strong>\n<strong>Dal usid kad dal iweid.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"?","prompt":"Comprehension","text":"`Kup nofap loleid kad wal?`","type":"explain","key":"`Dal loleid kad dal nes nomid wecdu.`","answers":["Dal loleid kad dal nes nomid wecdu."],"points":6},{"n":2,"part":"?","prompt":"Comprehension","text":"`Kup dal karxid wat deky hanop?`","type":"explain","key":"`Dal karxid cemu noze.`","answers":["Dal karxid cemu noze."],"points":6},{"n":3,"part":"?","prompt":"Comprehension","text":"`Kup dal enconid wat ky skorup?`","type":"explain","key":"`Dal enconid nauxap dale.`","answers":["Dal enconid nauxap dale."],"points":6},{"n":4,"part":"?","prompt":"Comprehension","text":"`Kup melfup ry bryklop?`","type":"explain","key":"`Gidu raje.`","answers":["Gidu raje."],"points":6},{"n":5,"part":"?","prompt":"Comprehension","text":"`Kup dal usid kad wal?`","type":"explain","key":"`Dal usid kad dal iweid.`","answers":["Dal usid kad dal iweid."],"points":6}]},{"id":"FR","kind":"review","name":"Final Review","title":"the whole course","html":"","vocab":[],"examples":[],"cloze":[],"exercises":[{"n":1,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Hello. What is your name?","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sal. Kup hisup suke?`","answers":["Sal. Kup hisup suke?"],"points":10,"bank":["hisup","Kup","Sal.","suke?"]},{"n":2,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I am bored because the book is boring.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik sysuei kad nibop sysui.`","answers":["Mik sysuei kad nibop sysui."],"points":10,"bank":["kad","Mik","nibop","sysuei","sysui."]},{"n":3,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The person who wrote the book is my friend.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofap tep jamid nibop tel nauxa mike.`","answers":["Nofap tep jamid nibop tel nauxa mike."],"points":10,"bank":["jamid","mike.","nauxa","nibop","Nofap","tel","tep"]},{"n":4,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"If you help me, then I will thank you.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Syn suk jofi mik can mik grawis.`","answers":["Syn suk jofi mik can mik grawis."],"points":10,"bank":["can","grawis.","jofi","mik","mik","suk","Syn"]},{"n":5,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I found no food and no water.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik karxid nes batap lan nes dorsop.`","answers":["Mik karxid nes batap lan nes dorsop."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","dorsop.","karxid","lan","Mik","nes","nes"]},{"n":6,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The house is bigger than the school.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Sydop tandem jete taly ibazop.`","answers":["Sydop tandem jete taly ibazop."],"points":10,"bank":["ibazop.","jete","Sydop","taly","tandem"]},{"n":7,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"She said that she herself was tired.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Das gesid tep hap dal wible iweid.`","answers":["Das gesid tep hap dal wible iweid."],"points":10,"bank":["dal","Das","gesid","hap","iweid.","tep","wible"]},{"n":8,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"I must not sleep.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Mik nes usi fuqe.`","answers":["Mik nes usi fuqe."],"points":10,"bank":["fuqe.","Mik","nes","usi"]},{"n":9,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"Everyone arrived on Monday.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Nofa late joborid bimedu.`","answers":["Nofa late joborid bimedu."],"points":10,"bank":["bimedu.","joborid","late","Nofa"]},{"n":10,"part":"A","prompt":"Translate into Fiwo.","text":"The food was made by my mother.","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`Fap batap sapid mana mike.`","answers":["Fap batap sapid mana mike."],"points":10,"bank":["batap","Fap","mana","mike.","sapid"]},{"n":11,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Kup suk nacid kad wal?`","type":"type-en","key":"Why did you work?","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":12,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Mik karxid nes batap lan dorso.`","type":"type-en","key":"I found no food, but I did find water.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":13,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Nofap tep jofid cadap tep otanid batap tel tel usid.`","type":"type-en","key":"The person who helped the child who cooked the food slept.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":14,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Zet bysop can ram kivwem fe.`","type":"type-en","key":"As for the sky, it is very blue.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":15,"part":"B","prompt":"Translate into English.","text":"`Hop suk sapid batap gawe.`","type":"type-en","key":"Oh, *great* food you made. (sarcastic — it was bad)","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":16,"part":"C","prompt":"Derive or compound it.","text":"*writer*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`jamia`","answers":["jamia"],"points":10},{"n":17,"part":"C","prompt":"Derive or compound it.","text":"*childhood*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`cadau`","answers":["cadau"],"points":10},{"n":18,"part":"C","prompt":"Derive or compound it.","text":"*fire vehicle*","type":"type-fiwo","key":"`moblo fuegoe`","answers":["moblo fuegoe"],"points":10},{"n":19,"part":"D","prompt":"Explain.","text":"Why does `Mik sysui` not mean *I am bored*?","type":"explain","key":"`-i` marks the source of a feeling, not the one feeling it. `Mik sysui` says *I bore people*. The experiencer takes `-e` plus the stative: `Mik sysuei`.","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":20,"part":"D","prompt":"Explain.","text":"Why is `Bata mosa` not *tree food*?","type":"explain","key":"Two nouns side by side is the Zero Copula — it says *the food is a tree*. Compounds need a modifier shift (`bata mosae`) or a bridge (`bata zy mosa`).","answers":[],"points":6},{"n":21,"part":"E","prompt":"Spoken — the real test.","note":"Without notes, speak for two minutes about your day. Then answer three questions about it from someone reading this book.","text":"Without notes, speak for two minutes about your day. Then answer three questions about it from someone reading this book.","type":"oral","key":"","answers":[],"points":4}]}]};
